hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4201","Al-Harith said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Said ibn Abi Said, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Quran comes on the Day of Resurrection in the finest adornment and finest form, saying: My Lord, You gave every worker the wage of his work, so where is the wage of my work? He said: The companion of the Quran is clothed a garment and crowned the crown of dominion. He said: Then he says: My Lord, I desired for him greater than this. He said: He is given eternity in his right and bliss in his left. He said: It is said to him: Are you pleased? He says: Yes, my Lord. Sent, sound."
"4202","Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated us, Dawud Abu Bahr narrated, from a brother-in-law of his called Muslim ibn Muslim, from Muwarriq al-Ijli, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When one of you rises at night, let him raise his voice in his recitation, for devils and wicked jinn flee at the loudness of his recitation; the angels in the air and the dwellers of the house listen to his recitation and pray with his prayer. When this night passes and the newly coming angels approach, they say: Wake him at his hour, and be gentle upon him. When death attends him, the Quran comes and stands at his head while they wash him; when finished with him, it enters till it is between his breast and shroud. When placed in his pit and Munkar and Nakir come to him, the Quran emerges till it is between him and them; they say to him: Away from us, we wish to question him. It says: By Allah I shall not leave him till I admit him to Paradise; if you were commanded in him with anything, your affair with him. Then it looks at him saying: Do you know me? He says: No, by Allah. It says: I am the Quran who kept your night wakeful, made your day thirsty, barred your desire, hearing, and sight; you find me of friends a truthful friend, and of brothers a truthful brother; so rejoice, for after the questioning of Munkar and Nakir no care nor sorrow is upon you. Then they leave him, so the Quran ascends to its Lord asking for him a bedding and cover. He said: He is commanded a bedding, cover, lamp of Paradise, and jasmine of the jasmine of Paradise; a thousand angels of the near ones of the lowest heaven carry it. He said: The Quran precedes them to him saying: Were you lonely after me? I ceased not with my Lord from whom I came till I commanded for you a bedding, cover, and light of the light of Paradise. The angels enter upon him, carrying him, spreading that bedding beneath him, placing the cover beneath his heart, the jasmine at his breast; then they carry him till they place him upon his right side, then ascend from him, and he lies upon it, gazing at the angels till they join in the heaven. Then the Quran is raised in a side of the grave, and it is widened for him as much as willed of that — Abu Abd al-Rahman said: In the book of Muawiyah ibn Hammad to me: it is widened a journey of four hundred years — then the jasmine is carried from his breast and placed at his nose, and he smells it fresh till the day the Trumpet is blown. Then he visits his family every two days once or twice, bringing their news, supplicating for them good and prosperity; if one of his children learns the Quran, he gives him glad tidings of that; if his issue is evil issue, he comes to the house morning and evening weeping over him till the day the Trumpet is blown — or as he said."
"4203","Chapter: the punishment of one who learns the Quran then forgets it, or does not act upon it, or shows off with it, and the prohibition of disputing in it. Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated, from Abu Aishah al-Sadi, from Yazid ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, mentioning the hadith, and in it: Whoever learns the Quran then forgets it deliberately meets Allah leprous, fettered, and Allah the Exalted sets upon him for every verse a snake biting him in the Fire. Whoever learns the Quran and does not act upon it, preferring over it the debris of the world and its adornment, deserves the wrath of Allah the Exalted and is in the rank of Jews and Christians who cast the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, behind their backs and bought with it a small price. Whoever recites the Quran for show and repute, or intending thereby the world, meets Allah, the Exalted, with his face a bone with no flesh upon it, and the Quran thrusts at his back till it casts him into the Fire, falling therein with who falls. Whoever recites it and does not act upon it, Allah the Exalted raises him on the Day of Resurrection blind, saying to Him: My Lord, why did You raise me blind when I was seeing? So your Lord says: Thus Our verses came to you and you forgot them, and thus today you are forgotten; then he is commanded to the Fire. Whoever learns the Quran seeking the face of Allah the Exalted and understanding in the religion of Allah, mighty and majestic, has reward like all Allah the Exalted gave the angels, prophets, and messengers. Whoever learns the Quran for show and repute, to argue with it the fools, vie with it the scholars, and seek thereby the world, Allah the Exalted scatters his bones on the Day of Resurrection, and he is of the severest of people in punishment; no kind of punishment remains except he is punished with it, due to the severity of Allah's punishment and wrath upon him."
"4204","Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, Khalid ibn Dinar narrated, saying: I heard Abu al-Aliyah saying: We counted of the greatest sins that a man learn the Quran then sleep past it till he forgets it."
"4205","Musaddad said: Ismail narrated us, Ziyad ibn Mikhraq narrated, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, from Abu Kinanah, who said: Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, gathered those who had recited the Quran, and they were near three hundred; so he magnified the Quran, then said: This Quran will be for you a wage, will be for you a store, and will be upon you a burden; so follow the Quran and let not the Quran follow you, for whoever follows the Quran, it descends with him upon the meadows of Paradise; and whoever is followed by the Quran, it thrusts at his back casting him into the Fire."
"4206","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Murra, from Abu Kanaf who said: Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I dislike that the reciter should be fat. He said: So I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: Abd Allah said: I dislike seeing the reciter fat and forgetful of the Qur'an."
"4207","Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, Ata' narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Do not strike parts of the Book of Allah against one another, for that casts doubt into your hearts."
"4208","Al-Tayalisi said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Salim Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not dispute about the Qur'an, for disputing about it is disbelief."
"4209","Chapter: Whoever disliked teaching the young boy the Qur'an until he can discern. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, al-Hasan ibn Amr narrated to me, from Fudayl, from Ibrahim, and he is al-Nakha'i, who said: They used to dislike teaching their children the Qur'an until they could understand."
"4210","Chapter: The command to recite the Qur'an with correct grammar. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to us, from his father, or from his grandfather, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite the Qur'an with correct Arabic grammar, and seek out its rare expressions. Abu Mu'awiya doubted."
"4211","And Ibn Abi Shayba said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn al-Maqburi, from his grandfather with the same, and he did not doubt."
"4212","Chapter: In how many days the Qur'an should be recited. Musaddad said: Yazid narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, Hafsa narrated to me, from Abu al-Aliya who said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) would not recite the Qur'an in less than three days."
"4213","Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Muhammad ibn Dhakwan who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) would complete the Qur'an from Friday to Friday, and in Ramadan he would complete it in three."
"4214","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Al-Muqri' narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, from Abu Ubayda who said: Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) would recite the Qur'an every three days, and he would rarely recite any of it during the daytime."
"4215","Book of Exegesis. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Sulayman, from Abd Allah ibn Murra, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: What sky would shade me, and what earth would carry me, if I were to say about the Book of Allah what I do not know, or what I have not heard?"
"4216","Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq narrated to us, Ma'n narrated to us, from Fulan ibn Muhammad ibn Khalid, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from A'isha: That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not interpret anything of the Qur'an except a number of verses which Jibril (peace be upon him) had taught him."
"4217","Abu Musa narrated to us, Amr ibn Asim narrated to us, al-Mu'tamir narrated to us from his father from Qatada from al-Hasan from Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him) that it reached him from Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him), or he heard it from him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he mentioned that in my nation there will be a people who recite the Qur'an, scattering it like the scattering of unripe dates, interpreting it with other than its true interpretation."
"4218","Chapter of Surah al-Fatiha. Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Abu Zubayd whose name is Abthar narrated to us, from al-Ala' ibn al-Musayyab, from Fudayl ibn Amr, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: He was asked about the Opening of the Book, and he said: Our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us, then his color changed and he repeated it for a while when he mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he said: It was sent down from a treasure beneath the Throne."
"4219","Musaddad said: Isma'il narrated to us, Yunus informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Seven Oft-Repeated is the Opening of the Book. Yunus said: And al-Hasan also used to say that."
"4220","And Ishaq said: Rawh ibn Ubada informed us, Malik informed us, from al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Amir ibn Kurayz, informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was praying in the mosque, so he glanced at him; when he finished praying he caught up with him, so he placed his hand in his hand and said: I hope you will not leave the mosque until you know a surah the like of which Allah has not sent down in the Torah nor in the Gospel. He said: So I began to walk slowly hoping he would mention that, and I said: What you promised me, O Messenger of Allah? So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you recite when you begin the prayer? So I said: All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, until I reached the end of the surah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is the Seven Oft-Repeated and the Magnificent Qur'an which I have been given. This is mursal with a sound chain, but there is disagreement over it from al-Ala': al-Darawardi narrated it from him, from his father, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to Ubayy ibn Ka'b. Al-Tirmidhi reported it. And Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated it from al-Ala', from his father, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned it. Ibn Hibban and al-Hakim reported it."
"4221","And Abd said: Husayn al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'ida, from Aban, from Shahr ibn Hawshab (may Allah be pleased with him), from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Opening of the Book equals two-thirds of the Qur'an. I say: Aban is al-Raqashi, abandoned."
"4222","Surah al-Baqarah. Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Hashim, and he is Ibn al-Qasim, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Talha narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad who said: Whoever recites al-Baqarah in a night will be crowned with a crown in Paradise."
"4223","Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, al-Hasan ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Sha'bi who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stopped at al-Rawha' and saw people hastening toward some stones, so he said: What is this? They said: They say the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed toward these stones. So he said: Glory be to Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only a rider who passed by a valley, prayer time came so he prayed, then he narrated and said: I used to visit the Jews on the day of their study, and they said: None of your companions is more honored to us than you, because you come to us. I said: That is only because I wonder at the Books of Allah, how they confirm one another, how the Torah confirms the Criterion and the Criterion confirms the Torah. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by one day while I was speaking to them, so I said: I adjure you by Allah and what you read of His Book, do you know that he is the Messenger of Allah? They said: Yes. So I said: You are ruined; by Allah, if you know he is the Messenger of Allah and then do not follow him? They said: We are not ruined, but we asked him who brings him his prophethood? He said: Our enemy Jibril, because he (peace be upon him) descends with severity, harshness, war, destruction and the like. So I said: Who is your friendly one among the angels? They said: Mika'il descends with rain and mercy and such. I said: What is their station with their Lord? They said: One is on His right and the other on the other side. I said: It is not lawful for Jibril to be hostile to Mika'il, nor lawful for Mika'il to befriend the enemy of Jibril, and I bear witness that they and their Lord are at peace with whoever is at peace with them, and at war with whoever wars against them. Then I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intending to inform him, but he met me and informed me of what I had said to them and what they had said."
"4224","Jarir informed us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Umayr ibn Sa'id who said: I heard Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) informing the people: This Venus the Arabs call al-Zuhara and the Persians call Anahid. The two angels used to judge between people, then each of them came to her without the knowledge of his companion. One of them said to his companion: O my brother, there is something in my soul I want to mention to you? He said: Mention it, O my brother; perhaps what is in my soul is like what is in yours. So they agreed on a matter concerning that. She said to them: No, until you inform me of what you ascend with to heaven and what you descend with to earth. They said: With the Greatest Name of Allah we descend, and with it we ascend. She said: I will not comply with what you want until you teach it to me. One of them said to his companion: Teach it to her. He said: How can we bear the severity of Allah's punishment? The other said: We hope for the vastness of Allah's mercy. So they taught it to her, then she uttered it and flew to heaven. An angel was startled by her ascent, bowed his head, and never sat after that, and Allah transformed her and she became a star."
"4225","Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Isra'il, from Jabir, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Allah cursed Suhayl, for he was a tax-collector in Yemen and was transformed; and Allah cursed al-Zuhara, for she tempted the two angels."
"4226","And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad al-Akhnasi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, al-Kalbi narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding His saying: A whirlwind wherein is fire. He said: The whirlwind is a violent wind."
"4227","And with the same chain regarding His saying: Like a rainstorm from the sky. He said: The rainstorm is rain."
"4228","And with the same chain to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding His saying: Those who consume usury will not stand except as one stands whom Satan has driven mad by touch. He said: They will be recognized on the Day of Resurrection by that; they will not be able to stand except as the afflicted epileptic stands, because they said: Trade is only like usury, and they lied against Allah. And Allah has permitted trade and forbidden usury, up to His saying: So whoever returns and eats usury, such are the companions of the Fire, abiding therein. And His saying: O you who believe, fear Allah and leave what remains of usury. He said: We have heard, by Allah, that this verse was revealed concerning Banu Amr ibn Awf of Thaqif and Banu al-Mughira of Banu Makhzum; Banu al-Mughira used to take usury from Thaqif. When Allah made His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prevail over Makkah, on that day he abolished all usury. The people of Ta'if had made peace on condition that their usury dues remained, and whatever usury was owed to them was cancelled; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote at the end of their document: They have what the Muslims have, and upon them is what is upon the Muslims, that they neither consume usury nor feed it. Then Banu Amr ibn Umayr came to Banu al-Mughira before Attab ibn Asid (may Allah be pleased with him), who was over Makkah. Banu al-Mughira said: What has made us the most wretched of people by usury; it is lifted from people other than us. Banu Amr ibn Umayr said: We made peace on condition that our usury remains. So Attab ibn Asid (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and this verse was revealed: If you do not do so, then take notice of war from Allah and His Messenger; and if you repent, yours is your principal. And whoever returns, such are the companions of the Fire."
"4229","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Warqa' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, and he mentioned a hadith. Yazid said: And from al-Fadl ibn Atiya who said: They wandered over twelve farsakhs for forty years, and among them was placed a stone like the head of a bull; when they camped, twelve springs gushed from it."
"4230","He said: And from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Mijlaz regarding His saying: And We shaded you with clouds. They were shaded with it in the wilderness."
"4231","Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from al-Hasan, from a man, from Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man would divorce then say: I was joking, and would free a slave then say: I was joking. So Allah revealed: And do not take the verses of Allah in jest. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever divorces or frees a slave and says: I was joking, his word is nothing; it takes effect upon him and binds him. Sufyan said: Meaning the matter binds him."
"4232","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Qatada, from Abu al-Aliya, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to recite: And look at the bones how We spread them."
"4233","And from Yahya, from Hisham ibn Hassan from Hafsa from Abu al-Aliya who said: Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) used to recite: And look at the bones how We raise them, pronouncing the zay with a dot."
"4234","And Abu Ya'la said: Abd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, Abda narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Arabi narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding His saying: To your food and your drink, it has not changed, and look. He said: It has not altered."
"4235","And Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid who said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about faith, so he recited: Righteousness is not that you turn your faces toward the east and the west. This is mursal with a sound chain, and it has a witness."
"4236","Yazid ibn Harun informed us, Sufyan ibn Husayn informed us, from Abu Ali al-Rahabi, from Ikrima (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Al-Hasan ibn Ali (may Allah be pleased with them both) was asked, on his return from Syria, about faith, so he recited: Righteousness is not. And it has another route in the Book of Faith."
"4237","And al-Harith said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Ziyad, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no Muslim afflicted by a calamity who remembers his calamity after forty years and renews the return-to-Allah phrase for it, except Allah will give him at that time reward like what He gave him on the day he was afflicted."
"4238","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Qatada, from Abu Hassan al-A'raj, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I bear witness that advance payment for a fixed term has been permitted by Allah and He has allowed it. Allah said: O you who believe, when you contract a debt for a fixed term, write it down."
"4239","Ishaq said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim who said: Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to consider it to be the dawn prayer, meaning the middle prayer."
"4240","And Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I asked him, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about the middle prayer. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is the Asr prayer which was neglected."
"4241","And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythama narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali and Nafi', the freedman of Ibn Umar, narrated to me, both saying: Amr ibn Rafi', the freedman of Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), narrated to them that he used to write copies of the Mushaf in the era of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with them). He said: Hafsa (may Allah be pleased with her) had me write a Mushaf for her, and said: When you reach this verse of Surah al-Baqarah, do not write it until you come to me so I dictate it to you as I memorized it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When I reached it, I brought her the sheet I was writing on, and she said: Write: Guard the prayers and the middle prayer and the Asr prayer and stand for Allah devoutly. The hadith of Abu Ubayda (may Allah be pleased with him) on the interpretation of the degree has preceded in the chapter on the merit of archery in the Book of Jihad. And the hadith of Ubada (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding: And do not take the verses of Allah in jest, has preceded in the enforcement of divorce in the Book of Marriage."
"4242","And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us who said: I heard Khusayf narrating from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: So no obscenity. He said: Obscenity is intercourse. And no wickedness. He said: Wickedness is sins. And no quarreling in Hajj. He said: Quarreling is disputing."
"4243","And al-Harith said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib who said: Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him) came emigrating to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and a group of Quraysh followed him. So he dismounted his camel, poured out what was in his quiver, then said: O company of Quraysh, you know I am among the best archers of men; by Allah, you will not reach me until I shoot every arrow with me in my quiver, then strike with my sword as long as anything remains in my hand, then do what you wish. And if you wish, I will guide you to my money buried in Makkah, and you let me go my way? They said: Yes, we accept. When he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah, he said: The bargain has profited, O Abu Yahya; the bargain has profited, O Abu Yahya. He said: And there was revealed: And among people is one who sells himself seeking Allah's pleasure, and Allah is Kind to the servants. Ibn Abi Hatim narrated it in al-Tafsir: My father narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us with the same."
"4244","And Abu Bakr said: Abu Usama narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Maysara who said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the station of the Friend of our Lord; shall we not take it as a place of prayer? So there was revealed: And take the Station of Ibrahim as a place of prayer."
"4245","And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hisham narrated to us, Abu Amir narrated to us, from Zam'a, from Salama, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding His saying: Except that Allah comes to them in shaded clouds and the angels. He said: Shades of cloud cut into layers."
"4246","Shayban narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Mankind was one nation. He said: All upon Islam. And al-Kalbi said: All upon disbelief."
"4247","Al-Harith ibn Surayj narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Nafi' narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man had intercourse with his wife from behind in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they said: So-and-so did so with his wife. So Allah revealed: Your women are a tilth for you, so come to your tilth as you wish."
"4248","Chapter on the merit of Surah al-Baqarah. Ishaq said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham informed us, my father narrated to me, from Qatada, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: While I was praying one night, I saw something like lamps of light descending from the sky; when I saw that, I fell prostrate. I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Why did you not continue, O Abu Atik? He said: I could not; when I saw it, I fell prostrate. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you continued, you would have seen wonders; those are the angels descending for the Qur'an."
"4249","Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla who said: Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: While I was praying standing one night, and I had recited al-Baqarah, and he mentioned something similar."
"4250","Sufyan ibn Uyayna informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik. And Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik who said: Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him) said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned something similar."
"4251","Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: While Usayd ibn Hudayr (may Allah be pleased with him) was praying one night, Usayd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Something like a cloud with lamps covered me, my wife sleeping beside me while pregnant, and the horse tied in the house; I feared the horse would shy and frighten the woman so she would miscarry. So I ended my prayer. I mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite, Usayd; that was an angel listening to the Qur'an. I say: Al-Bukhari narrated it as a suspended report, and Muslim and Ahmad from the hadith of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), and al-Nasa'i in al-Kubra from the Musnad of Usayd (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4252","Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divided Surah al-Baqarah."
"4253","And al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Khalid ibn Sa'id al-Madani narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything has a peak, and the peak of the Qur'an is Surah al-Baqarah; whoever recites it in his house at night, Satan will not enter his house for three nights, and whoever recites it by day, Satan will not enter his house for three days. Ibn Hibban reported it in his Sahih from Abu Ya'la."
"4254","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'ba, Abu Ishaq narrated to me, from Amr, from Sa'd or Sa'id, from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I did not consider anyone with sense to sleep until he recites the last verses of Surah al-Baqarah, for they are from a treasure beneath the Throne."
"4255","Chapter on the merit of Ayat al-Kursi. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad, and he is Ibn Salama, narrated to us, Ma'bad informed us, Fulan informed me, from Awf ibn Malik who said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned a hadith like a previous hadith, in it: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of what Allah sent down to you is greatest? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer, until you complete it. And the hadith of Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) similar to it is at the beginning of the hadiths of the Prophets (peace be upon them), and in it is the description of the Kursi."
"4256","And Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Za'ida, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay', from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf (may Allah be pleased with him), when he entered his house, would recite Ayat al-Kursi in its corners."
"4257","And al-Harith said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of the Qur'an is Surah al-Baqarah, and the greatest verse in it is Ayat al-Kursi, and Satan flees from the house in which Surah al-Baqarah is recited. The hadith is mursal; its chain to al-Hasan is sound."
"4258","Surah Al Imran. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Ar'ara who said: When Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed, he mentioned a story from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), then he said: Indeed the first house set for people is the one at Bakkah. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Yet it is not the first house that existed; Nuh (peace be upon him) was before Ibrahim (peace be upon him), and they lived in houses, and Ibrahim lived in houses. But it is the first house set for people, blessed and a guidance for the worlds, wherein are clear signs, the Station of Ibrahim, and whoever enters it is safe."
"4259","And al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us with the same. And in the chapter on the battle of Uhud in the Maghazi there is a hadith on the interpretation of: Then He sent down upon you after distress security, slumber, all the verses."
"4260","And Musaddad said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, Humayd al-A'raj narrated to us, from Mujahid who said: And whoever enters it is safe. He said: It is like your saying: Enter while you are safe."
"4261","And Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, Asbat narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abd Khayr, from Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I did not think any of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) desired this world until there was revealed: Among you are some who desire this world and among you are some who desire the Hereafter."
"4262","Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, from Dawud, and he is Ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Qaza'a, from Hujayr ibn Bayan (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no possessor of kinship who comes to his kin asking from the bounty Allah has given him, and he is miserly toward him, except a bald serpent that smacks its lips will be brought forth for him on the Day of Resurrection until it encircles him. Then he recited: And let not those who are miserly with what Allah has given them of His bounty. The verse."
"4263","Al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutayba narrated to us, Isra'il narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: You are the best nation brought forth for people, enjoining right and forbidding wrong: They are those who emigrated to Madinah."
"4264","And Musaddad said: Ja'far narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Mujahid who said: Al-Harith ibn Suwayd had become Muslim, then joined his people and disbelieved; so Allah revealed this verse: How shall Allah guide a people who disbelieved after their belief and bore witness that the Messenger is true, to the end of the verse. He said: A man of his people carried it to him and recited it to him. Al-Harith said: By Allah, as far as I know you are truthful, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more truthful than you, and Allah is the most truthful of the three. Then he returned and became Muslim with a good Islam."
"4265","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Asim ibn Bahdala, from Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Drowsiness at fighting is security, and drowsiness in prayer is from Satan."
"4266","And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu al-Huwayrith that he heard al-Hakam ibn Minhal saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar (may Allah be pleased with him): Gather for me whoever is here of Quraysh. The hadith. It has preceded in the chapter on scrupulousness and piety in al-Raqa'iq."
"4267","And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hasan narrated to us, Abu Amr al-Qari' narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Abd al-Rahman who said: I said to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both): Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) recites: And it is not for a prophet to betray, with fatha on the ghayn. He said to me: It is allowed for him to betray and to kill; rather it is: That he should be betrayed, with damma on the ghayn, and Allah would not make a prophet treacherous."
"4268","Surah al-Nisa'. Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj al-Sawwaf, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab who said: I traveled to A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) concerning this verse: It is not by your wishes nor the wishes of the People of the Book; whoever does evil will be recompensed for it. She said: It is what befalls you in this world."
"4269","Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Muttalib narrated to us, my father narrated to me that he heard Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he said: The major sins are seven: associating with Allah, killing the soul Allah has forbidden except by right, disobedience to parents, fleeing from battle, accusing a chaste woman, consuming usury, and consuming the orphan's wealth. This is a good chain."
"4270","Ibn Ulayya informed us, from Ziyad ibn Mikhraq, from Taysala ibn Mayyas al-Hudhali who said: I was with the Najdat and committed sins I considered major. So I came to Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and he said: They are nine, and he listed them: associating with Allah, killing a soul without its right, fleeing from battle, accusing a chaste woman, consuming usury, consuming the orphan's wealth, heresy in the Sacred Mosque, one who seeks sorcery, and making parents weep through disobedience. When Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) saw my fear, he said: Do you fear entering the Fire? I said: Yes. He said: Do you love to enter Paradise? I said: Yes. He said: Are your parents alive? And he mentioned the hadith."
"4271","And al-Harith said: Mu'awiya ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Habib ibn Abi Amra, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr who said: al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad (may Allah be pleased with him) went out in a detachment; they passed by some polytheists who fled, while a man remained with his family and wealth and said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah. Al-Miqdad (may Allah be pleased with him) killed him. It was said to him: Did you kill him while he bore witness there is no god but Allah? He said: Would that he had fled with his family and wealth. They said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so ask him. They came to him and mentioned that to him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you kill him while he bore witness there is no god but Allah? He said: O Messenger of Allah, would that he had fled with his wealth and family. He said: Then this verse was revealed: O you who believe, when you go forth in Allah's way, verify, up to: You were like that before, meaning: you concealed your faith while with the polytheists, then Allah favored you and Islam appeared, so verify."
"4272","And Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us. And Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us. And al-Bazzar said: Abu Kamil narrated to us. They said: Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from al-Falatan ibn Asim who said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when revelation came down upon him; when it came down upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his gaze would fix, his eyes open, and his hearing and sight would be devoted to what came to him from Allah. When he finished, he said to the scribe: Write: Those believers who sit back, other than the disabled, are not equal to those who strive in Allah's way with their wealth and lives. The verse. He said: Ibn Umm Maktum the blind stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, what is our fault? So Allah revealed: Other than the disabled. We said to the blind man: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is receiving revelation. He said: He remained standing, saying: O Allah, I repent to You. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, he said: Write: Other than the disabled. Ibn Hibban reported it in his Sahih from Ibn Ya'la."
"4273","And Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Abd al-Jalil, and he is Ibn Atiya, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir who said: A man said to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): I know the severest verse in the Book of Allah. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) reached out and struck him with the stick, saying: What is with you, that you searched it out until you knew it? He left, until the next day Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: The verse you mentioned yesterday. He said: Did you leave me to inform you of it? Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: I did not sleep last night. He said: O Commander of the Faithful, Allah said: Whoever does evil will be recompensed for it. There is none of us who does evil except he will be recompensed for it. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: When it was revealed, neither food nor drink benefited us, until Allah later sent down and gave concession. He said: And whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness will find Allah Forgiving, Merciful."
"4274","Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Ar'ara who said: When Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was killed, he mentioned a hadith containing: Then another stood and asked him, meaning Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), about: If a woman fears cruelty or desertion from her husband, there is no sin upon them to make peace between them. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Ask about the like of this; it is a man with two wives, one of whom is incapable or unattractive, so he makes peace with her to visit her once every two or three nights."
"4275","And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Amr ibn Dinar informed us, Salama, a man from the children of Umm Salama, informed me, from Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with him) disputed with a man before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the man said: He only judged for him because he is his aunt's son. So Allah revealed: But no, by your Lord, they will not believe until they make you judge in what is disputed between them. The verse."
"4276","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from a man from the children of Umm Salama who said: I think Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her) said: al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) disputed with a man before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged for him. He said: He only judged for him because he is his aunt's son, sneering with his mouth. A Jew said: Look at this one slighting his Prophet; we are more obedient than them: our prophet commanded us to kill ourselves, so we killed ourselves."
"4277","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to me, from Zadham who said: Abd Allah, and he is Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), said: Being killed in Allah's way expiates all sins except trusts: the martyr in Allah's way will be brought and told: Fulfill your trust. He will say: From where can I fulfill it when the world is gone? He said: He will say: Take him to the Abyss; when he is brought to the bottom of the Abyss, his trust will be shown to him as it was on the day it was lost. He will carry it and place it on his shoulder, climbing in the Fire; when he thinks he has come out of it, it will fall, and he will fall after it forever. Then Abd Allah recited: Indeed Allah commands you to render trusts to their owners."
"4278","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When this verse was revealed: They ask you; say: Allah gives you a ruling about kalala, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in his house, he looked and there was Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him); so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it to him and taught it to Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him). Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him) looked and there was Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), so he taught it to him. When Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) became caliph, he wanted to rule on kalala, so he met Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him. He said: By Allah, I would be a fool if I thought your leadership could burden me with a yoke and say: Teach it to me; it is what I told you. He said: May Allah have mercy on you; that is not what I meant. He said: It was revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he taught it to me, and I taught you as he taught me; by Allah, I will never add anything to that."
"4279","And Abu Ya'la said: Uqba ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Ziyad ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, from Nafi' ibn al-Harith, from Abu Barza (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah will raise on the Day of Resurrection a people whose mouths blaze with fire. It was said: Who are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Have you not seen that Allah says: Those who consume the wealth of orphans unjustly."
"4280","Sa'id narrated to us, Isa ibn Sadaqa informed me who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: Fear Allah and fulfill trusts, for Allah says: And render trusts to their owners."
"4281","Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Ash'ath, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Damra ibn Jundub left his house emigrating; he said to his family: Carry me and take me out of the land of the polytheists to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So revelation came down: And whoever leaves his house emigrating to Allah. The verse."
"4282","Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Mubashshir, and he is Ibn Isma'il, narrated to us, from Tammam ibn Najih, from Ka'b ibn Dhuhl al-Iyadi who said: I used to visit Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him), and I heard him narrating from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: One came to me from my Lord and said: And whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness will find Allah Forgiving, Merciful. The previous verse: Whoever does evil will be recompensed for it, had weighed heavily upon them. I wanted to give glad tidings to my companions. I said: O Messenger of Allah, even if he commits adultery and steals then seeks Allah's forgiveness, He forgives him? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. Then I said, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, despite Uwaymir. Then Ka'b ibn Dhuhl (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I saw Abu al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) striking the tip of his own nose with his finger."
"4283","Surah al-Ma'ida. Al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Isa al-Tabba' narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'a narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fulfill for the allies the compacts your oaths have made. They said: What is their compact, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Blood-money on their behalf, and support for them."
"4284","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Warqa', from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid who said: Musa (peace be upon him) chose two men from each tribe; they entered the city of the tyrants, and each group came out forbidding their tribe to enter, except Yusha' ibn Nun and Kalib ibn Yuqanna."
"4285","He said: Yazid narrated to us, from al-Fadl ibn Atiya who said: They wandered over twelve farsakhs for forty years, and among them was placed a stone like the head of a bull; when they camped, twelve springs gushed from it, and when they traveled they carried it on a bull and the water held."
"4286","And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Warqa' narrated to us with this up to his saying: That they enter upon them."
"4287","Yazid narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Atiya al-Awfi who said: They wandered over twelve farsakhs for forty years, and a stone like the head of a bull was made for them, carried on a bull; when they camped they placed it and Musa (peace be upon him) struck it, and twelve springs gushed from it; when they traveled they carried it on a bull and the water held."
"4288","Yazid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit, from Ikrima regarding His saying: Then it is forbidden to them for forty years, wandering in the land. He said: Forbidden for them to ever enter, and wandering in the land for forty years."
"4289","And Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Umm Amr bint Isa who said: My uncle (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that he was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his house when Surah al-Ma'ida was sent down upon him; we knew revelation was descending upon him, and the shoulder of his she-camel al-Adba' broke under the weight of the surah."
"4290","And Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata', from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I never saw a people better than the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); they asked him only thirteen questions until he passed away, all of them from the Qur'an."
"4291","And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Zakariyya narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding: Eager listeners to falsehood. He said: The Jews of Madinah. Eager listeners for other people. He said: The people of Fadak. Who have not come to you, to the end of the verse."
"4292","And Abu Bakr said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Simak, from Iyad al-Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him): They are the people of this one, meaning His saying: Allah will bring a people He loves and who love Him."
"4293","And al-Harith said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, from Uthman, from Qatada (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When there was later revealed: And judge between them by what Allah has sent down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Today we judge over the Jews and Christians, and over all other religions besides them."
"4294","And Abu Bakr said: Mu'awiya ibn Hisham narrated to us, Nusayr ibn Ziyad al-Ta'i narrated to us, al-Salt narrated to me, from Hamiya ibn Riqab who said: I asked Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) about this verse: That is because among them are priests and monks. He said: Leave the priests in the monasteries and ruins; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught it to me: That is because among them are truthful ones and monks."
"4295","And al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Nusayr ibn Ziyad narrated to us, and he mentioned it with the wording: He said: They are the monks in the monasteries and ruins; leave them in them. Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I recited to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That is because among them are priests, and he taught me: That is because among them are truthful ones."
"4296","And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Sinan, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara' ibn Azib (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: A year would pass when I wanted to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about something but held back in awe of him; we used to wish for the Bedouins. The hadith of al-Hasan from Abu Bakra (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding: They did not forbid one another from the wrong they did, is in the Book of Faith."
"4297","And Ishaq said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us, from Abu Ja'far al-Razi, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliya who said: They were with Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), and they mentioned this verse: Today I have perfected your religion for you. A Jewish man said ... the hadith. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Allah perfected the matter for us, so we knew the matter would thereafter decline. I say: Its basis is reported by them from the hadith of Tariq ibn Shihab from Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) without what is here."
"4298","And Abd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ikrima who said: Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited Surah al-Ma'ida and Surah al-Tawba in his sermon, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Permit what Allah has permitted in them, and forbid what Allah has forbidden in them."
"4299","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya who said: I heard al-Qasim saying: A'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: When I heard people saying: Every predator with fangs is forbidden, I said: I do not find in what was revealed to me forbidden to one who eats it, to the end of the verse. He said: A cooking pot may have yellowness in its water, yet that does not forbid it."
"4300","And Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, from Burd ibn Sinan, from Ubada ibn Nusayy, from Ghudayf ibn al-Harith who said: Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) wrote to me: We have Samaritan neighbors who read part of the Torah and Gospel but do not believe in the unseen; what do you think of their slaughtered animals? So he wrote to him: If they read part of the Torah and part of the Gospel, and observe the Sabbath, their slaughtered animals are like those of the People of the Book."
"4301","And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man of the people of Fadak committed adultery. The people of Fadak wrote to some Jews in Madinah: Ask Muhammad about that; if he commands you with flogging, take it from him, and if he commands you with stoning, do not take it from him. They asked him about that, and he said: Send to me your two most learned men. They brought a one-eyed man called Ibn Suriya and another. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Are you the most learned of those before you? They said: Our people have inclined to that. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Do you not have the Torah with Allah's ruling in it? They said: Yes. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I adjure you by the One who parted the sea for Musa, shaded you with clouds, saved you from Pharaoh's people, and sent down manna and quails upon the Children of Isra'il: What do you find in the Torah concerning stoning? One of them said to the other: I was never adjured with the like of this? Then he said: We find repeated looking is adultery, and strutting is adultery; if four bear witness that they saw him going in and out as the kohl stick enters the kohl container, stoning becomes due. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is it. So he commanded him and he was stoned. And there was revealed: If they come to you, judge between them or turn away from them. The verses."
"4302","And Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, from Sufyan, and he mentioned it, its beginning: If you are given this, take it, and if you are not given it, beware. He said: It was revealed about Ibn Suriya when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them; he mentioned the hadith without mentioning its beginning, and added in it: And the kiss is adultery. Its end: As the kohl stick enters the kohl container, then stoning. He did not mention what follows, and it is with Abu Dawud and others in abridged form, also in it."
"4303","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, Nafi' informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Hurayra asked Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) about many fish cast up by the sea. Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: Is it dead meat? He said: Yes. So he forbade him. When he entered, he called for the Mushaf and recited: Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food as provision. He said: Its food is what comes out of it, so eat it; there is no harm in it. Everything in it that is eaten dead in it or dead beside it. I say: Malik narrated it in al-Muwatta' from Nafi' similarly; he said after his saying: So he recited: Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food as provision: He sent me to Abd al-Rahman and said: There is no harm in it, so eat it."
"4304","And al-Harith said: Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, Marwan, and he is Ibn Mu'awiya, narrated to us, Khusayf narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr who said: The Negus sent a delegation of his companions to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the hadith. And there was revealed concerning them: You will find the most hostile of people to the believers are the Jews and those who associate partners, and you will find the nearest in affection to the believers are those who say: We are Christians; that is because among them are priests and monks and they are not arrogant. The verse. And it will come, Allah willing, in the Prophetic biography."
"4305","Surah al-An'am. Ishaq said: Jarir informed us from Layth, from Shahr ibn Hawshab: Surah al-An'am was sent down, and with it a din of angels that filled what is between the lowest heaven and the earth; it is Makkan except for two verses: Say: Come, I will recite."
"4306","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Musa, from al-Aswad ibn Hilal, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said to his companions: What do you say about these two verses: Those who say: Our Lord is Allah, then are steadfast, and: Those who believed and did not mix their belief with wrongdoing? They said: Those who say: Our Lord is Allah, then act by it, then are steadfast upon His command. They said: And those who believed and did not mix their belief with wrongdoing did not sin. He said: You have loaded it upon a severe matter: Those who believed and did not mix their belief with wrongdoing means with polytheism; and those who said: Our Lord is Allah, then were steadfast upon it, never deviating from it by polytheism or otherwise."
"4307","Abu Usama informed us from some Makkans, from Mujahid regarding His saying: And do not follow the paths, lest they separate you from His way. He said: Innovations and doubts."
"4308","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mujammi' al-Taymi, Mahan narrated to him who said: Some people came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We have committed great sins; I do not think he answered them. When they turned away, there was revealed: And when those who believe in Our verses come to you, say: Peace be upon you; your Lord has decreed mercy upon Himself. The verse. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called them and recited it to them."
"4309","Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated to us, from Hassan al-Fihri, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding: And no leaf falls. He said: There is no tree on land or sea except an angel is entrusted with it, recording what falls of its leaves."
"4310","And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Badr narrated to us, from Amr ibn Qays, from a man, from Umm Salama (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Let a person beware of being separate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in anything. Then she recited: Those who divided their religion and became sects, you are not of them in anything."
"4311","And Abu Ya'la said: Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, Abu Malik al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from Nafi' ibn Khalid al-Khuza'i, from his father, and he was among the companions of the Tree (may Allah be pleased with them), who said: When people sat around him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray a light prayer complete in bowing and prostration. One day he prostrated and lengthened the prostration until some of us gestured to one another to be silent, for revelation was descending upon him. When he finished, one of the people said to him: O Messenger of Allah, you lengthened the prostration until some of us gestured to one another that revelation had descended upon you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, but it was a prayer of hope and fear: I asked Allah three things; He gave me two and withheld one. I asked Him not to destroy you with a punishment He punished those before you with, and He gave it to me. I asked Him not to give power over your generality to an enemy who would destroy it, and He gave it to me. And I asked Him not to clothe you in sects and make some taste the violence of others, and He withheld it from me. He said: I said to him: Your father, did you hear it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes; he mentioned he heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as many as these ten fingers."
"4312","And al-Bazzar said: Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, similar to it."
"4313","Husayn ibn Amr al-Anqazi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Asbat ibn Nasr narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Sa'id al-Azdi, from Abu al-Kanud, from Khabbab ibn al-Aratt (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding: And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening seeking His Face; nothing of their account is upon you, and nothing of your account is upon them, lest you drive them away and be among the wrongdoers. He said: al-Aqra' ibn Habis and Uyayna ibn Hisn al-Fazari (may Allah be pleased with them both) came and found the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting with Bilal, Suhayb, Khabbab (may Allah be pleased with them) and some weak believers. When they saw them around him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they belittled them. They came to him and were alone with him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: We wish you would set aside for yourself a sitting place by which the Arabs will recognize our merit, for we are the notables of the Arabs who come to you; we are ashamed that the Arabs see us with these slaves. When we come to you, keep them away from us; when we finish, seat them if you wish. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. They said: Write for us a document against you. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for the sheet and called Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) to write, while we sat in a corner, when Jibril (peace be upon him) descended with: And do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening seeking His Face, up to: Among the wrongdoers. Then He said: And when those who believe in Our verses come to you, say: Peace be upon you; your Lord has decreed mercy upon Himself. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threw the sheet from his hand, then called us and we came to him while he said: Peace be upon you; your Lord has decreed mercy upon Himself."
"4314","And Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, Asbat ibn Nasr narrated to us, and he mentioned it in full. Ibn Majah reported it through Amr al-Anqazi in abridged form."
"4315","Surah al-A'raf. Ishaq said: Affan informed us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit who said: Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We heard Allah saying: Those who took the calf will be overtaken by anger from their Lord and humiliation in the life of this world; thus We recompense the fabricators. He said: We consider the people only to have fabricated a lie, which I think will befall them; he mentioned it during his hadith."
"4316","Affan informed us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub who said: Abu Qilaba recited this verse and said: By Allah, it is for every fabricator until the Day of Resurrection: humiliation in the life of this world."
"4317","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Abu Usama narrated to us, Atiq ibn Hayyan al-Azdi narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The seventy whom Musa chose from his people were seized by the tremor only because they neither forbade the calf nor were pleased with it. His saying: My Lord has only forbidden indecencies, apparent and hidden, its interpretation: And what is hidden, has preceded in the chapter on enjoining women in the Book of Marriage."
"4318","And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-Harith who said: I heard Tubay' ibn the wife of Ka'b saying regarding: In it you live, in it you die. He said: The earth: from it Allah created Adam, in it they are buried when they die, and from it they are brought forth; the sky rains forty nights and the dead come out of the earth. It has a witness in the Sahih."
"4319","And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, both saying: Abu Ma'shar informed us, Yahya ibn Shibl narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Muzani, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the companions of the Heights? He said: They are a people killed in Allah's way in disobedience to their parents; their being killed in Allah's way barred them from the Fire, and their disobedience to their parents barred them from Paradise. And al-Harith added: Al-Kalbi said: A people whose good and evil deeds are equal, so they are barred from Paradise and the Fire; Allah will admit them into His mercy. I do not know whether he mentioned a slain one or not."
"4320","And al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Kathir ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Hilali, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: One said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who are the companions of the Heights? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A people who went out in Allah's way without their parents' permission and were martyred; martyrdom barred them from entering the Fire, and disobedience barred them from entering Paradise."
"4321","Muhammad ibn Umar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"4322","And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Khaythama narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Layth, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, from Umm Salama, Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about transformation: Does it have offspring? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No transformed one ever had offspring or descendants. I say: It is with Muslim through al-Ma'rur from Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Umm Habiba (may Allah be pleased with her) asked; and this is the correct one, while Layth is weak in memory."
"4323","Chapter of Surah al-Anfal. Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, Wasil ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Ata' and Abu Sawra, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment which Allah aided and granted victory; whoever brought something would be given extra from it after the fifth. Some men returned who would advance, capture, and kill, leaving the spoils behind them without taking anything of the spoils. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what about men of us who advanced, captured, while men stayed behind who did not reach the fighting, yet you gave them extra from the spoils? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent. Jibril (peace be upon him) descended: They ask you about the spoils; say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger, so fear Allah and set right what is between you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called them and said to them: Return what you took and divide it among you justly and equally. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we have spent and eaten. He said: Then count it as such."
"4324","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim who said: He said: A man asked Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) about nafl, and he said: The horse is of nafl, and spoils taken from the slain are of nafl. He said: He repeated to him, and he said: This is like the deed of Sabigh whom Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) struck."
"4325","And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn Rashid narrated to us, Abu Haywa Shurayh ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Sinan, from al-Mulayki, from his father, from his grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding: And others besides them whom you do not know, Allah knows them. He said: They are the jinn. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Satan does not confound anyone in a house in which there is an aged horse."
"4326","And al-Tayalisi said: al-Salt ibn Dinar narrated to us, Uqba ibn Suhban and Abu Raja' al-Utaridi narrated to us, both saying: We heard al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) reciting this verse: And fear a trial which will not strike only those who wronged among you. I recited this verse for a time, and: Fear a trial, for a time, never considering myself among its people, yet we have become among its people."
"4327","Surah al-Tawba. Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Shu'ba narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The last of the Qur'an to be revealed: There has come to you a Messenger from among yourselves, grievous to him is what you suffer, eager over you, to the believers kind, merciful."
"4328","Waki' informed us from Shu'ba with this chain the like of it. This is a good chain."
"4329","And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Mansur narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), similar to it. The hadith of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) on postponement has preceded in the chapter on the sanctity of Makkah."
"4330","And Ishaq said: Abda ibn Sulayman informed us, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqama narrated to us, Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by a man reciting: And the foremost, the first of the Emigrants and Helpers, until he completed the verse. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Turn aside, turn aside. He said: Who taught you this surah? He said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) taught it to me. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Do not leave me until we go to him. He came and sought permission while he was reclining, and he gave him permission. He said: This one claims you taught him such-and-such verse, and he recited it to him. He said: He spoke the truth. Umar said to Ubayy (may Allah be pleased with him): Did you receive it from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) repeated three times, each time Ubayy (may Allah be pleased with him) saying to him: Yes. Then he said: I bear witness Allah sent it down upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Jibril (peace be upon him) brought it from Allah, neither al-Khattab nor his son was consulted in it. He said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) went out saying: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest."
"4331","And Abu Ya'la said: Mahmud ibn Khidash narrated to us, Sayf ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A dinar will not be upon a dinar, nor a dirham upon a dirham, but his skin will be stretched, then their foreheads, sides, and backs will be branded with them. The verse. This is very weak due to the weakness of Sayf."
"4332","And Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Wasil ibn al-Sa'ib al-Raqashi, from Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, and from Abu Sawra, from his uncle Abu Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who are these about whom He said: Men who love to purify themselves, and Allah loves the purified? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They would cleanse with water, and they would not sleep the whole night. Abu Sawra is weak."
"4333","And Musaddad said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ubayd ibn Umayr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the wanderers? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are the fasting ones. This is mursal with a sound chain."
"4334","And Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to pray over Abd Allah ibn Ubayy, so Jibril (peace be upon him) took his garment and said: And never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand over his grave. This is a weak hadith; Yazid, despite his weakness, contradicted what is established in the two Sahihs from the hadith of Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that he prayed over him, and that the verse was only revealed after that."
"4335","And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb who said: I heard Hudhayfa (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: A hypocrite died and I did not pray over him. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What prevented you from praying over him? I said: He is of them. He said: Am I of them, by Allah? I said: No. He said: Then he wept. Its chain is sound, though Ya'qub ibn Sufyan deemed it objectionable from the hadith of Zayd ibn Wahb."
"4336","Musaddad said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us from al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad: I heard al-Hasan saying: O Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and hypocrites. He said: Striving against the disbelievers is with the sword, and striving against the hypocrites is with the tongue."
"4337","And Musaddad said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, Talq ibn Habib narrated to me, who said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: I saw smoke in the mosque of harm when it collapsed."
"4338","And Abu Bakr said: Mu'awiya ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Musa ibn Ubayda, from Iyas ibn Salama ibn al-Akwa', from his father (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: Allah will see your work, and His Messenger, and the believers."
"4339","Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ghaylan narrated to us, from Uthman Abu al-Yaqzan, from Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas who said: When this verse was revealed: And those who hoard gold and silver, it weighed heavily upon the Muslims, who said: None of us can leave wealth for his child after him. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I will relieve you. They set out, and Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) set out, followed by Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him). They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Prophet of Allah, this verse has weighed heavily upon your companions. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Zakat was only made obligatory for what remains of your wealth, and inheritance was only prescribed in wealth to remain after you. He said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) exclaimed Allah is Greatest. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Shall I inform you of what is hoarded? A righteous woman: when he looks at her she pleases him, when he commands her she obeys him, and when he is absent she guards him."
"4340","And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us with this."
"4341","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jud'an, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu Talha (may Allah be pleased with him) recited this verse: Go forth light and heavy and strive with your wealth and lives in Allah's way. He said: Allah has left no excuse for anyone. Then he went out to Syria and remained there striving until he died there."
"4342","And Ibn Abi Shayba said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubayda, Khalid ibn Yasar informed us, from Ibn Abi Aqil, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) that he spent a night dragging a rope on his back for two sa's of dates. I returned with one to my family and brought the other to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seeking nearness to my Lord with it. I informed him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what happened, and he said: Spread it in the mosque. The hypocrites spoke and mocked him: Allah was in no need of this poor man's sa'. So Allah revealed: Those who criticize the volunteers. The verse."
"4343","Surah Yunus. And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Abbad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and from a man, from Abd Allah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever utters a takbir in Allah's way, and he mentioned the hadith which has preceded in the merit of jihad. He said: He looks upon the Majestic, the Noble morning and evening, as you see the sun without doubting its sight; and his is of honor and bliss as Allah said: For those who did good is the best and increase. He said: Those who did good are those who said: There is no god but Allah. The best is Paradise, and the increase is looking at the Face of Allah."
"4344","And Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Fudayl ibn Marzuq, from Atiya, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he used to recite: So in that let them rejoice, with feminine ta'."
"4345","Surah Hud. And Abu Bakr said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ikrima who said: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What has made you gray? He said: Hud, al-Waqi'a, al-Mursalat, Amma yatasa'alun, and Idha al-shamsu kuwwirat have made me gray. This is mursal, sound, though described as unsettled."
"4346","Abu Ya'la reported it from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid and Khalaf ibn Hisham, separating them, both from Abu al-Ahwas with the same. And al-Tirmidhi narrated it in al-Shama'il through another route from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Juhayfa, and he mentioned it with the wording: Hud and its sisters."
"4347","And Musaddad said: Muhammad ibn Jabir narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani who said: I heard al-Dahhak ibn Qays reciting: You do with our wealth what You will, meaning with ta'."
"4348","Surah Yusuf. Ishaq said: Amr ibn Muhammad informed us, Khallad al-Saffar narrated to us, from Amr ibn Qays al-Mula'i, from Amr ibn Murra, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from Sa'd regarding: We relate to you the best of stories. The verse. He said: Allah sent down the Qur'an upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recited it to them for a time. They said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you told us stories? So Allah sent down: Alif Lam Ra, these are the verses of the clear Book, up to: We relate to you the best of stories. The verse. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it for a time, and they said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you narrated to us? So Allah sent down: Allah has sent down the best discourse, a Book consistent. The verse. All of that commanding them with the Qur'an. Khallad said: Another added in it: He said: They said: O Messenger of Allah, would that you reminded us? So Allah sent down: Has the time not come for those who believed that their hearts humble to Allah's remembrance. The verse. This is a good hadith. And Ibn Mardawayh narrated it in his Tafsir from Ahmad ibn al-Hasan from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Shirawayh from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh."
"4349","And Abu Ya'la and al-Bazzar both said: al-Husayn ibn Amr ibn Muhammad al-Anqazi narrated to us, my father narrated to us with the same."
"4350","And al-Bazzar also narrated it from al-Husayn ibn al-Aswad and Isma'il ibn Hafs, from Amr ibn Muhammad, saying: We know it from Sa'd only by this chain."
"4351","Abu Yala said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya, Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Mawsili, Muhammad ibn Hatim al-Mu'addib, and al-Mu'alla ibn Mahdi narrated to us, and I copied it from the book of Zakariyya and it is his wording, they said: al-Hakam ibn Zuhayr informed us, from al-Suddi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man of the Jews called Nusayb the Jew came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Muhammad, inform me of the stars which Yusuf saw prostrating to him in the horizon of the sky, what are their names? The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not answer him that day with anything, then Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him and informed him, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent for Nusayb the Jew and said to him: Will you accept Islam if I inform you of their names? Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are Khirban, al-Dhayyal, al-Tariq, al-Lisan, Qayis, Waththab, Amudan, al-Faylaq, al-Musabbih, al-Saruh, and Dhu al-Fara'. He said: Nusayb says: By Allah, indeed these are their names. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Yusuf, peace and blessings be upon him, saw them, he related them to his father, so his father said to him: A scattered matter which Allah will gather afterwards; he said: And the sun is his father, and the moon is his mother. Al-Hakim brought it through another chain from al-Suddi."
"4352","Abu Yala said: al-Akhnasi narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, from al-Kalbi, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {Confused dreams}, he said: They are false dreams."
"4353","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Iyad, from Husayn, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {And she prepared for them a reclining couch}, he said: The citron."
"4354","Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr regarding His saying: {The king's cup}, he said: It is the Persian measure in which the non-Arabs drink, whose two ends meet."
"4355","Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Warith, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan who said: The suwa' and the siqayah are one; it is the vessel in which one drinks."
"4356","Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Isra'il narrated, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Yusuf, peace and blessings be upon him, was reproached for his saying: {Mention me to your lord, but Satan made him forget the mention of his lord}, and for his saying to his brothers: {Indeed, you are thieves}, and: {They said: If he steals, a brother of his stole before}, and his saying: {That he may know that I did not betray him in absence}; so Jibril, peace and blessings be upon him, said to him: Not even when you inclined? He said: {And I do not absolve myself}, the verse."
"4357","Abdullah ibn Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Warraq narrated to us, Aththam narrated, from al-A'mash who said: I heard Shaqiq saying: O Allah, if You have written us with You as wretched, then erase us and write us as fortunate, and if You have written us as fortunate, then keep us firm, for indeed You erase what You will and confirm, and with You is the Mother of the Book."
"4358","Abu Yala said: Rawh ibn Abd al-Mu'min narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Musa narrated, from Sulayman ibn Arqam, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: {And he who has knowledge of the Book}."
"4359","Al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Hammam narrated, from al-Kalbi, regarding His saying: {Allah erases what He wills and confirms, and with Him is the Mother of the Book}, he said: Allah, mighty and majestic, erases what He wills of things from the origin, and adds to it what He wills. I said to him: Who narrated to you? He said: Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And the hadith of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, on the revelation of His saying: {And if there were a Qur'an by which the mountains were moved}, will come, Allah willing, in the commentary of Surah al-Shu'ara."
"4360","Abu Yala said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Bakri narrated, Amr ibn Malik narrated, from Abu al-Jawza, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {By your life}, he said: By your life."
"4361","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Zayd narrated, from Amr ibn Malik, from Abu al-Jawza, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Allah, mighty and majestic, has not created nor brought forth any soul nobler to Him than Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I have not heard Allah, blessed and exalted, swearing by the life of anyone except by his life, when He said: {By your life, indeed they were in their intoxication, wandering blindly}."
"4362","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, to a well of the polytheists to draw water from it, and around it were three rows guarding it, so he drew water in his skins, then came until he reached the first row and they seized him, so he said: Leave me, for I am only drawing water for your companions, so they left him. Then he returned a second time and they seized him and did the same to him, then they left him. Then he went and returned and they seized him and did the same to him. When they wanted him to utter disbelief, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent cavalry and they rescued him, so this verse was revealed about him: {Except one who is compelled while his heart is secure in faith}."
"4363","Musaddad said: Yahya informed us, Shu'bah narrated, Firas narrated to me, from Amir, from Masruq who said: Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, recited: Indeed, Mu'adh was a nation, devout to Allah, upright, so Farwah ibn Nawfal said: He forgot: Indeed, Ibrahim. Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I did not forget; we used to liken him to Ibrahim. And Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about the nation, he said: The teacher of good. And he was asked about the devout, he said: The one obedient to Allah the Exalted and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4364","Abu Yala said: Surayh ibn Yunus narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abdullah ibn Murrah, from Masruq, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying: {Those who disbelieved and barred from the way of Allah, We increased them in torment over torment because they used to corrupt}, he said: They were given more scorpions whose fangs are like tall palm trees. Al-Hakim authenticated it."
"4365","Abu Yala said: Surayh narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sulayman narrated, from al-A'mash, from al-Hasan, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {We increased them in torment over torment}, he said: Five rivers, they are tormented with some of them at night and some of them in the day."
"4366","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, al-Furat ibn al-Sa'ib narrated, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Allah, mighty and majestic, sent down this letter upon the tongue of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And your Lord has enjoined that you worship none but Him, then one of the two waws joined to the other, so he recited to us: {And your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him}. Had it been revealed as a decree, no one would have associated anything with Him. And Maymun used to say: Indeed, upon its commentary there is light. Allah, blessed and exalted, said: {He has ordained for you of the religion what He enjoined upon Nuh}."
"4367","Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Amir, regarding His saying: {And We certainly gave Musa nine clear signs}, he said: His hand, his staff, the years of drought, the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, the blood, and shortage of fruits."
"4368","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated, from Abu Salih and Ikrimah, regarding His saying: {And We certainly gave Musa nine clear signs}, they said: The years: He withheld rain from them, and shortage of fruits, and the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, the blood, his staff, and his hand."
"4369","Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {And do not recite your prayer loudly nor lower it}, he said: They used to raise their voices in supplication: O Allah, have mercy on me. When this verse was revealed, they were commanded neither to lower it inaudibly nor to raise it loudly. And the hadith of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, on the revelation of His saying: {And nothing prevented Us from sending the signs except that the former peoples denied them}, the three verses, will come, Allah willing, in the commentary of Surah al-Shu'ara."
"4370","Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abu Qurrah al-Asadi, then al-Saydawi, a man of the desert people, informed us; he said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrating from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It has been revealed to me that whoever says: {So whoever hopes for the meeting of his Lord, let him do righteous work and not associate anyone in the worship of his Lord}, there will be for him a light from Aden Abyan to Makkah, its filling being the angels."
"4371","Abu Yala said: al-Jarrah ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Umar ibn Yunus narrated, Isa ibn Awn narrated, Abd al-Malik ibn Zurarah narrated, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah, mighty and majestic, has not bestowed upon a servant a blessing of family, wealth, or child, and he says: What Allah wills, there is no power except with Allah, and then he sees in it an affliction short of death. And he used to interpret this verse: {And why, when you entered your garden, did you not say: What Allah wills, there is no power except with Allah}."
"4372","Al-Bazzar said: Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah al-Attar narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated, Abu Bakr al-Hudhali narrated, from Thumamah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sees something and it pleases him, then says: What Allah wills, there is no power except with Allah, it will not harm him. And a hadith on the revelation of His saying: {And do not obey one whose heart We have made heedless of Our remembrance}, has passed in al-An'am."
"4373","Abu Bakr and Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated, a freedman of Abdullah ibn Amr narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw the sun when it set, and said: In Allah's intensely hot Fire; were it not for what restrains it of the command of Allah, mighty and majestic, it would have destroyed what is upon the earth."
"4374","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Yazid narrated with it."
"4375","Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated, in the commentary of Ibn Jurayj: {And he found near it a people}, he said: A city having twelve thousand gates; were it not for the voices of its people, the people would have heard the rumble of the sun when it sets."
"4376","Al-Hasan narrated, from Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding: {A shelter}: A building; no building was ever built in it, and no building was ever built over them in it; when the sun rose, they entered their tunnels until the sun declined."
"4377","Musaddad said: Bishr narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated, from Abu Hazim, from al-Nu'man ibn Abi Ayyash, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The straitened livelihood which Allah the Exalted mentioned is the torment of the grave."
"4378","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated, Amr ibn al-Harith narrated, that Abu al-Samh informed him, from Ibn Hujayrah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of it in the course of a hadith."
"4379","Al-Harith said: Aswad ibn Amir Shadhan narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from Salim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {Ta Ha}, meaning: Ta, O man, and it is in Nabataean."
"4380","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Farqad al-Sabakhi, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The calf, when it lowed, they prostrated, and when it fell silent, they raised their heads."
"4381","Yazid narrated to us, Juwaybir narrated, from al-Dahhak, regarding His saying: {A lowing sound}, he said: It lowed a single lowing, it did not repeat. Have you not seen that Allah, mighty and majestic, said: {Does it not return any word to them}, and He, majestic and exalted, said: {Did they not see that it neither spoke to them nor guided them to a way}."
"4382","Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Yazid ibn Abdillah, he is Ibn Qusayt, from Abu Rafi', may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A guest came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent me to a Jew, saying: Say to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) says to you: Sell to us or lend to us until Rajab. So I said it to him, and he said: By Allah, I will neither sell to him nor lend to him except with a pledge. So I returned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed, by Allah, had he sold to me or lent to me, I would have repaid him; indeed, I am trusted in the heaven, trusted in the earth; go with my iron armor. So I went with it, and this verse was revealed consoling the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {And do not extend your eyes to what We have given enjoyment to spouses among them}."
"4383","Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, Sufyan narrated, from al-Suddi, from Murrah, from Abdullah who said: Whoever intends an evil deed and does not do it, nothing is written against him; but if he intends in Aden Abyan to kill in the Sacred Mosque, Allah will make him taste a painful torment. Then he recited: {And whoever intends therein deviation with wrongdoing}, the verse. Mawquf, strong in chain."
"4384","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban narrated, from Mansur, from Mujahid who said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to pitch two domes, a dome in the Hil and a dome in the Haram. It was said to him: If only you were with your cousin and your family? He said: Makkah is Bakkah, and we were informed that among deviation therein is: Nay, by Allah, and Yes, by Allah. This is mawquf, sahih. The saying of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: Let the desert dweller camp where he wills, has passed at the beginning of Hajj."
"4385","Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid who said: Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about people of a religion I was with, and I mentioned their prayer, fasting, and worship, so the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic, was revealed: {Indeed, those who believed and those who were Jews and the Sabeans and the Christians and the Magians}, to His saying: {A witness}."
"4386","Ishaq said: Ahmad ibn Ayyub al-Dabbi informed us, Abu Hamzah al-Sukkari narrated, from Jabir, from Amir who said: Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was writing this verse while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was dictating it to me: {And We certainly created man from an extract of clay}, until he reached: then {We produced him as another creation}, so Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So blessed is Allah, the best of creators. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) laughed, and he said to him: Why did you laugh? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This verse was sealed with what you say: {So blessed is Allah, the best of creators}. Jabir is al-Ju'fi, weak."
"4387","Ibn Abi Umar said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafi'i narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated, he said: I heard al-Thawri saying regarding His saying: {Our Lord, our wretchedness overcame us}, he said: The decree."
"4388","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Nili narrated to us, Salih al-Murri narrated, from al-Hasan, from some of the Emigrants, and from Yazid al-Raqashi and Ja'far ibn Zayd, from Zufar who said: One of the Emigrants said: I sought this verse all my life and could not attain it, the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: {And if it is said to you: Go back, then go back}. And I ask permission of some of my brothers, and it is said to me: Go back, so I go back with a contented eye."
"4389","Ahmad ibn Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, al-Kalbi narrated, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {He sends the winds and they stir clouds, then He spreads them in the sky as He wills and makes them fragments}, saying: Pieces, making some above others; {Then you see the rain}, meaning the rain, {Coming out from between them}, from among them."
"4390","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Aban narrated, al-Zuhri narrated, Urwah narrated, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I went out, I and Umm Mistah al-Ansariyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, for a need of ours, and she stumbled and said: May Mistah perish. I said: Evil is what you said of a man who accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When my innocence was revealed, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O A'ishah, rejoice. Then my father and mother, may Allah be pleased with them both, stood to me and kissed me, but I pushed against their chests and said: Without your praise nor the praise of your companion; I praise Allah, mighty and majestic, for what He excused me and declared me innocent, while your opinion was bad, as you did not think good of yourselves; the hadith."
"4391","Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan regarding: {A forbidden ban}, he said: When a woman saw something she disliked, she said: Hijr."
"4392","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' informed us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, regarding His saying: {Into the cities as gatherers}, he said: The police."
"4393","Yazid narrated to us, from al-Mas'udi, from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Children of Isra'il entered Egypt as seventy-three persons, and left it as six hundred thousand, so Pharaoh said: {Indeed, these are a small band}."
"4394","Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Dawud ibn Shabur narrated, from Mujahid, regarding His saying: {And your turning among those who prostrate}, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to see behind him in prayer as he saw in front of him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4395","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Abdillah narrated, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Ka'b, from his uncle Ubaydullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When this verse was revealed: {And the poets, the misguided follow them}, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer strives with his hand and his tongue; by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, it is as if you charge at them with arrows."
"4396","Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Ali al-Ansari narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Tamim narrated, from Abd al-Jabbar ibn Umar al-Ayli, from Abdillah ibn Ata ibn Ibrahim, from his grandmother Umm Ata, freedwoman of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, who said: She heard al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: When the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic, was revealed: {And warn your nearest kin}, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cried out on Abu Qubays: O family of Abd Manaf, indeed I am a warner. Quraysh came to him; he warned them and cautioned them, and they said: You claim you are a prophet inspired to you, and that to Sulayman, peace and blessings be upon him, were subjected the wind and the mountains, and to Musa, peace and blessings be upon him, was subjected the sea, and Isa, peace be upon him, used to give life to the dead; so call upon Allah the Exalted to move these mountains from us and burst rivers for us, that we may take them as crossings, plant and eat. Otherwise call upon Allah, mighty and majestic, to give life to our dead that we may speak to them and they speak to us. Otherwise call upon Allah the Exalted to turn this rock beneath you into gold, that we may carve from it and it suffices us from the winter and summer journeys, for you claim you are like them. While we were around him, the signs of revelation descended upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when it was lifted from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, He has given me what you asked, had I wished it would have been. But He, majestic and exalted, gave me choice between your entering the door of mercy so your believer believes, and between leaving you to what you chose for yourselves so you stray from the door of mercy and none of your believers believe. So I chose the door of mercy so your believer believes. And He informed me that if He gave you that and then you disbelieved, He would torment you with a torment He has not tormented anyone of the worlds."
"4397","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated, Kathir ibn Qarwand narrated, from Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying: {Indeed, He who imposed upon you the Qur'an will return you to the place of return}, he said: His return is his Hereafter."
"4398","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah narrated to us, al-Mu'ammal, he is Ibn Isma'il, narrated, Isra'il narrated, Abu Ishaq narrated, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When: {Alif Lam Mim. The Romans have been defeated in the nearest land}, the verse, was revealed, some people met Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Do you not see your companion claiming that the Romans will defeat Persia? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke the truth. They said: Shall we make a wager with you on that? He said: Yes. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: What did you intend by this? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I did it only affirming Allah the Exalted and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Then challenge them, magnify the stake, and set it to a few years, for if the years pass until the Romans appear over Persia. He said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by them and said: Will you take a return, for the return is more praiseworthy? They said: Yes. So they wagered with him and magnified the stake. The years did not pass until the Romans appeared over Persia, so he, may Allah be pleased with him, took the stake and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is for the noble she-camels."
"4399","Musaddad said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, from Layth, from Tawus who said: Surah Alif Lam Mim Tanzil and Tabarak were favored over every surah of the Qur'an by sixty good deeds."
"4400","Abu Yala said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Abu Lubabah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite every night: Tanzil al-Sajdah."
"4401","Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Bajalah al-Tamimi who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, found a mushaf in the lap of a slave boy of his, in it: The Prophet is more worthy of the believers than themselves, and he is a father to them, and his wives are their mothers. He said: Scratch it out, boy. He said: I will not scratch it, and it is in the mushaf of Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, went to Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: The Qur'an busied me, and striking deals in the markets busied you, when you present your mill on your neck at the door of Ibn al-Ajma. This chain is sahih upon the condition of al-Bukhari."
"4402","Abu Yala said: Ahmad al-Dawraqi narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated, from Abu Raja Abdillah ibn Waqid, from Muhammad ibn Malik, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying: {Their greeting on the Day they meet Him is peace}, he said: The day they meet the Angel of Death; there is no believer whose soul is taken except he greets him with peace. And the hadith of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, on the revelation of His saying: {O you who believed, remember the favor of Allah upon you when armies came to you}, will come, Allah willing, in the campaigns, in the Battle of the Trench."
"4403","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated, from al-Hakam, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, regarding His saying: {Do not be like those who harmed Musa, so Allah declared him innocent of what they said}, he said: Musa and Harun, peace and blessings be upon them both, ascended, and he mentioned the hadith on: {Do not be like those who harmed Musa}, and it has passed in the reports of the prophets, peace and blessings be upon them."
"4404","Abu Bakr said: Yahya ibn Ya'la al-Aslami narrated to us, Yunus ibn Khabbab narrated, from Nafi', from Abu al-Hamra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I witnessed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eight months; whenever he went out to prayer, or he said the dawn prayer, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the door of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: Peace be upon you, O people of the House: {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O people of the House, and to purify you thoroughly}."
"4405","Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated, Abu Dawud narrated, from Abu al-Hamra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I stayed in guard at Medina for seven months in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when dawn rose, come to the door of Ali and Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with them both, and say: Prayer, prayer: {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O people of the House, and to purify you thoroughly}. Abu Dawud is Nafi', as in the one before it."
"4406","Abd said: al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to me, Abu Dawud al-Sabi'i narrated to me, Abu al-Hamra, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: I accompanied the Messenger seven months; when morning came, he would come to the door of Ali and Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: Prayer, may Allah have mercy on you, {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O people of the House}, the verse. Abu Dawud is Nafi', and it is said Nufay' the blind; Qatadah declared him a liar, and he is very weak. And Ahmad brought it through Ali ibn Zayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, with its meaning."
"4407","Abu Dawud said: al-Salt ibn Dinar Abu Shu'ayb narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Suhban al-Huna'i narrated, he said: I asked A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: {Then We caused to inherit the Book those We chose of Our servants}, the verse, and she said to me: O my son, all of these are in Paradise. As for the forerunner in good deeds: whoever passed at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) testified for him with life and provision. As for the moderate: whoever followed his trace among his companions until he joins him. As for the wrongdoer to himself: like me and like you. He said: She placed herself, may Allah be pleased with her, with us."
"4408","Abu Yala said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated, from Hisham ibn Ziyad, from al-Hasan who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites Ya-Sin in a night wakes forgiven."
"4409","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yusuf ibn Atiyyah al-Saffar al-Basri narrated to us, from Harun ibn Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites Ya-Sin seeking thereby the Face of Allah the Exalted is forgiven; whoever recites Ya-Sin, it is as if he recited the Qur'an twelve times; whoever recites Ya-Sin while in the agonies of death, Ridwan, keeper of Paradise, comes with a drink of the drink of Paradise until he gives him to drink while on his bed, until he dies satiated and is raised satiated."
"4410","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Hushaym narrated, Husayn informed us, from Abu Malik who said: Ubayy ibn Khalaf came with a decayed bone to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), crumbled it before him, and said: O Muhammad, will Allah resurrect this after it decayed? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, He will resurrect this, then cause you to die, then give you life, then admit you to Hell. He said: So the verses at the end of Surah Ya-Sin were revealed: {Did man not see that We created him from a sperm drop, and behold}, to the end of the surah."
"4411","Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hammad ibn Amr narrated, from al-Sari ibn Khalid ibn Shaddad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Ali, recite Ya-Sin, for in Ya-Sin are ten blessings: no hungry one recites it except he is fed, no thirsty except he is quenched, no naked except he is clothed, no unmarried except he marries, no fearful except he is secured, no prisoner except he is released, no traveler except he is aided in his travel, no one whose lost item is lost except he finds it, no sick except he is healed, and it is not recited at a dead one except it is lightened for him."
"4412","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying: {Gather those who wronged and their spouses}, he said: And their likes. This chain is sahih."
"4413","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Hanzalah ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated, from al-Dahhak ibn Qays, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: A time came upon us when we did not know the sense of this verse: {Glorifying in the evening and the morning brightness}, until we saw the people praying Duha."
"4414","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn Imran narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, al-Kalbi narrated, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying, majestic is His majesty: {Gentle wherever it turned}, he said: Gentle means obedient. As for {Wherever it turned}, he said: Wherever it intended."
"4415","Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Sayyar Abu al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn Abbad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prostrated in Sad."
"4416","Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Ibrahim ibn Muhajir narrated to me, from Mujahid, regarding His saying: {And the eminent among them went forth: Go and be patient over your gods}, he said: Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt."
"4417","With it, from Mujahid, regarding His saying: {We did not hear of this in the later religion}, he said: In Christianity."
"4418","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Hasan ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Layth narrated, from Mu'awiyah, meaning Ibn Salih, from Abu Yahya, from Abu Yazid, from Abu Sallam al-Aswad, from Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the dawn prayer, and he said: My Lord came to me tonight in the best form; the hadith at length, and he added at its end: O Allah, I ask You Your love, the love of whoever loves You, and love that conveys to me Your love."
"4419","Abu Yala said: Surayj narrated to us, Abu Hafs al-Abbar narrated, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My Lord came to me in the best form and said: O Muhammad. I said: At Your service, my Lord, and ready at Your service. He said: Do you know concerning what the Highest Assembly disputes? I said: I do not know. He said: He placed His hand upon my chest, and I felt its coolness between my shoulders; he said: He placed His hand between my shoulders, and I felt its coolness in my chest. He said: O Muhammad. I said: At Your service and ready. He said: Do you know concerning what the Highest Assembly disputes? I said: Concerning degrees and expiations. As for degrees: perfecting ablution in disliked conditions, moving feet to congregations, and waiting for prayer after prayer. As for expiations: feeding food, spreading peace, and prayer at night while people sleep. Whoever does that lives well, and will be of his sins as the day his mother bore him. And He said to me: O Muhammad, say: O Allah, I ask You doing good deeds, leaving evil deeds, loving the poor, and when You intend temptation for a people and I am among them, take me to You untempted."
"4420","Abu Yala said: Shuja' ibn Makhlad Abu al-Fadl narrated to us, Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to me, al-Aghlab ibn Tamim narrated, from Makhlad ibn Hudhayl, from Abd al-Rahman al-Madani, from Abdillah ibn Umar, from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the commentary of His saying: {To Him belong the keys of the heavens and the earth}. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None asked me about it before you. Its commentary is: There is no god but Allah, Allah is Greatest, glory be to Allah and with His praise, I seek Allah's forgiveness, there is no power nor strength except with Allah, the First and the Last, the Manifest and the Hidden, in His Hand is good, He gives life and causes death, and He is over all things Capable. Whoever says it in the morning ten times is given ten qualities: the first: he is guarded from Iblis and his soldiers; the second: he is given a qintar of reward; the third: a degree is raised for him in Paradise; the fourth: he is married of the wide-eyed maidens; the fifth: twelve thousand angels attend it; the sixth: he has of reward as one who recited the Torah, the Gospel, the Psalms, and the Furqan; and with this, O Uthman, he has of reward as one who performed Hajj and Umrah, and his Hajj and Umrah are accepted; if he dies that day, he is sealed with the seal of the martyrs. And Ibn Abi Asim narrated it."
"4421","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Waqid narrated to us, Hafs ibn Abdillah al-Ifriqi narrated, Hakim ibn Nafi' narrated, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about the keys of the heavens and the earth, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Glory be to Allah, praise be to Allah, there is no god but Allah, Allah is Greatest, the keys of the heavens and the earth; there is no power nor strength except with Allah, of the treasures of the Throne; the hadith."
"4422","Abu Yala said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Umar ibn Muhammad, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he asked Jibril, peace and blessings be upon him, about this verse: {And the Trumpet is blown, and whoever is in the heavens and whoever on earth falls swooning, except whom Allah wills}, who are those He did not will to swoon? He said: They are the martyrs carrying their swords around the Throne of the Merciful; the angels meet them on the Day of Resurrection to the gathering on noble mounts of ruby whose reins are softer than silk, the span of their stride the span of men's sight; they travel in Paradise saying at the length of the excursion: Take us to our Lord, mighty and majestic, that we may see how He judges among His creation; my God laughs to them, and when He laughs to a servant in a place, there is no reckoning upon him. And there will come, Allah willing, in the description of Paradise, a hadith on: {Those who feared their Lord, to Paradise in groups}, the verse."
"4423","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Amir al-Bajali, from Sa'id ibn Nimran, from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said regarding: {Indeed, those who said: Our Lord is Allah, then stood firm}, he said: They are those who did not associate anything with Allah, mighty and majestic."
"4424","Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Isma'il ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Sufayr al-Makki, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite a verse, then interpret it; I would not love to have the world and what is in it for it. He said: {And whatever affliction befalls you is for what your hands earned, and He pardons much}. Then he said: Whom Allah, mighty and majestic, takes for his sin in this world, Allah, majestic and exalted, is more generous than to repeat it upon him in the Hereafter; and what Allah the Exalted pardoned in this world, Allah is more generous than to pardon it in this world and take from him in the Hereafter."
"4425","Yazid ibn Abi Hakim al-Adani informed us, al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated, he said: I heard Dhiyab ibn Murrah saying: While Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was with his companions narrating to them, he said to them: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he stood and did not explain. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, turned back and said: Shall I not show you? They said: We would not disperse until you explain to us what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: {And whatever affliction befalls you is for what your hands earned, and He pardons much}. What Allah the Exalted pardoned will never return, and it is in Ha Mim Ayn Sin Qaf."
"4426","Abu Yala said: I read to al-Husayn ibn Yazid al-Tahhan, he said: It is as I read to Sa'id ibn Khuthaym, from Fudayl ibn Marzuq al-Kufi, from Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When: {And give the near relative his right} was revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and gave her Fadak."
"4427","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Salamah ibn Sabrah who said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us and said: You are the believers, you are the people of Paradise, and I hope that those you capture of Persia and Rome will enter Paradise. When one of them does a deed, you say: You did well, may Allah have mercy on you; you did well, may Allah bless you. And Allah, mighty and majestic, says: {And those who believed and did righteous deeds respond, and He increases them of His bounty}."
"4428","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Dawud narrated, from al-Sha'bi who said: The people pressed us much about this verse: {Say: I ask you no reward for it except love of kinship}. So I wrote to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, wrote to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was of middle lineage in Quraysh; there was no belly of their bellies except they had borne him. So Allah the Exalted revealed: {Say: I ask you no reward for it except love}, for what I call you to, except that you love me for my kinship among you and guard me for it. Sahih. And in al-Bukhari through another way from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, is its meaning."
"4429","Abu Yala said: Abu Talib Abd al-Jabbar ibn Asim narrated to us, Abu Abd al-Malik al-Hasan ibn Yahya al-Khushani narrated, from Abu Mu'awiyah who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, ascended the minbar and said: O people, has any of you heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) interpreting: Ha Mim Ayn Sin Qaf? Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, leapt up and said to us: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ha: A name of the names of Allah, mighty and majestic. He said: And Ayn? He said: The polytheists witnessed the torment of the Day of Badr. He said: And Sin? He said: {And those who wronged will know what overturning they will overturn}. He said: And Qaf? Then he sat and fell silent. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure you by Allah the Exalted, has any of you heard? Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, leapt up and said as Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said. He said: And Qaf? He said: A calamity from heaven striking the people."
"4430","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydillah, from Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah who said: When Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, camped at al-Jabiyah, he said to Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him: O Mu'adh, what is the bond of this matter? He said: I said: Sincerity, O Commander of the Faithful, and obedience. Then he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Three, whoever does them has committed a crime: whoever holds a banner without right, whoever disobeys his mother, or whoever walks with an oppressor aiding him has committed a crime. Allah, blessed and exalted, says: {Indeed, from the criminals We take retribution}."
"4431","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Sufyan and Shu'bah narrated, from Asim, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding: {And indeed, it is knowledge of the Hour}, he said: The descent of Isa ibn Maryam, peace and blessings be upon him."
"4432","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yusuf ibn Atiyyah al-Saffar narrated to us, from Harun ibn Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Abu Umamah, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: Whoever recites al-Dukhan on Friday night is forgiven. And from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the like of it."
"4433","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Basri narrated to us, Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Yazid al-Raqashi informed me, Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no servant except he has two doors in heaven: a door from which his deed enters, and a door from which his deed and speech exit. When he dies, they miss him and weep over him. And he recited this verse: {Neither heaven nor earth wept over them}. He mentioned that they used not to do a righteous deed on earth that would weep over them, nor did there ascend to heaven of their speech or deed any good speech or righteous deed, that it would miss them and weep over them. This chain is weak, and al-Bukhari and al-Tirmidhi brought part of it through another way."
"4434","Abu Yala said: Abu Hisham narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, from Muslim, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: On the day of Ad, the wind carried the desert people with their wealth and livestock; when it raised them from earth to sky, they said: This is a cloud bringing us rain. He said: So the desert fell upon the town."
"4435","Ahmad ibn Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated, the like of it."
"4436","Al-Harith said: Hawdhah narrated to us, Awf narrated, from al-Hasan who said: When Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, intended Islam, he entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I testify you are the Messenger of Allah, sent with guidance and the religion of truth, and the Jews find you with them in the Torah described. Then he said to him: Send for some men of the Jews, so-and-so and so-and-so, and he named them to him, and hide me in a house, and ask them about me and my father, for they will inform you. And I will come out to them and testify you are the Messenger of Allah, sent with guidance and the religion of truth, that perhaps they will accept Islam. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that: he hid him in his house and sent for the men he commanded him with, and called them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: What is Abdullah ibn Salam among you, and what was his father? They said: Our master and son of our master, our scholar and son of our scholar. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What if he accepts Islam, will you accept Islam? They said: He will not accept Islam. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What if he accepts Islam, will you accept Islam? They said: He will not accept Islam. He said: What if he accepts Islam? They said: He will never accept Islam. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him, and he came out to them, then said: I testify you are the Messenger of Allah, sent with guidance and the religion of truth, and they know of you as I know. The Jews said to Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him: We did not fear this of you, O Abdullah. He said: They went out from him, and Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed about that: {Say: I am not an innovation among the messengers, and I do not know what will be done with me nor with you; I follow only what is revealed to me, and I am only a clear warner. Say: Have you considered, if it is from Allah}, the verses."
"4437","Al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Talhah, he is Ibn Amr, narrated, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when he was expelled from Makkah: I depart from you, and I know you are the best of Allah's lands, mighty and majestic, and most honored to Him. Were it not that your people expelled me from you, I would not have left. O sons of Abd Manaf, if you are rulers of this matter after me, do not prevent one circumambulating the House of Allah the Exalted for an hour of night or day."
"4438","Abu Yala said: Mahmud ibn Khidash narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated, Talhah narrated with it."
"4439","Hasan ibn Umar narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated, he said: My father narrated, Hanash narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left Makkah toward the cave, he looked at Makkah and said: You are the most beloved of Allah's lands to me; were it not that your people expelled me from you, I would not have left you. The most hostile enemy is one who transgresses against Allah the Exalted in His sanctuary, or kills other than his killer, or kills for a pre-Islamic grudge. He said: So Allah revealed to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {And how many a town stronger than your town which expelled you, We destroyed them}, the verse."
"4440","Ishaq said: al-Mughirah ibn Salamah informed us, Wuhayb narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was teaching a youth to recite; he recited: {Do they not ponder the Qur'an, or are there locks upon hearts}. The youth said: Upon them are their locks until Allah, mighty and majestic, opens them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You spoke the truth. And some people of Yemen came to him asking him to write a letter for them, so he commanded Abdullah ibn al-Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, to write a letter for them, and he wrote for them, and brought it to him, and he said: You did right. And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, thought he would hold something of people's affairs; when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was made caliph, he asked about the youth, and they said: He was martyred. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said such-and-such, the youth said such-and-such, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You spoke the truth. So I knew Allah would guide him. And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, employed Abdullah ibn al-Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, over the treasury."
"4441","Musaddad said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadhdhan, from al-Hasan, regarding His saying: {You will be called to a people of great might, you will fight them or they will submit; if you obey, Allah will give you a good reward, but if you turn away as you turned away before, He will torment you with a painful torment}, he said: Persia and Rome."
"4442","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, Abu Sa'id, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated, from Abu Khalaf, from Abdillah ibn Awf who said: I heard Abu Jum'ah Junayd ibn Sabu' saying: I fought the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the start of the day as a disbeliever, and fought with him at the end of the day as a Muslim. We were three men and seven women, and about us was revealed: {And were it not for believing men and believing women}, the verse. Abu Khalaf is named Hujr."
"4443","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Mughirah who said: I came to Ibrahim al-Nakha'i and said: A man disputed with me, called Sa'd al-Anazi, and Ibrahim said: He is not al-Anazi, but al-Zubaydi, regarding His saying: {The bedouins said: We believed. Say: You have not believed, but say: We submitted}. He said: It is submission. Ibrahim said: No, rather it is Islam."
"4444","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Bakr narrated, from Ibn Jabir, Ata al-Khurasani narrated to me, he said: I came to Medina and met a man of the Ansar, and said: Narrate to me the hadith of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas. He said: Yes, come with me. I stood with him until we reached the door of a house; he seated me at its door, then entered, stayed a while, then called us and admitted us to a woman. He said: This is the daughter of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas, may Allah be pleased with her, so ask her what you wish. I said: Narrate to me of him, may Allah have mercy on you. She said: When Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {O you who believed, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet}, the verse, he entered his house, closed his door, and began weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed him and said: What is the matter with Thabit? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we do not know his matter, except he closed his door weeping in it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and asked: What is your matter? He said: O Messenger of Allah, this verse was revealed to you: {O you who believed, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet}, and I am loud of voice, and I fear my deed is void. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are not of them; rather, you will live well and die well. She said: Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed to His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Indeed, Allah does not love every arrogant boaster}. So he closed his door and began weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed him and said: Thabit, what is his matter? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we know only that he closed his door. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent for him and said: What is your matter? He said: I am arrogant and boastful, and I fear I am of the people of this verse. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are not of them; you will live praised and be killed as a martyr, and enter Paradise."
"4445","Musaddad and Ishaq both said: Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: I heard Dawud al-Tufawi saying: Abu Muslim al-Bajali narrated to me, he said: I heard Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Some people came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Take us to this man; if he is a prophet, we bear witness to him, and if he is a king, we live under his protection. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of that. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) calling him from his chamber: O Muhammad, O Muhammad. So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: {Indeed, those who call you from behind the chambers, most of them do not understand}. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my ear, stretched it, and kept saying: Allah has confirmed your word, O Zayd; Allah has confirmed your word, O Zayd."
"4446","Abu Yala narrated it: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Abi Saminah narrated to us, Mu'tamir narrated with it."
"4447","Ishaq said: Rawh, he is Ibn Ubadah, informed us, Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated, Thabit, freedman of Umm Salamah, informed me, from Umm Salamah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs before Asr. A delegation of Banu al-Mustaliq came to him, and he had sent to them al-Walid ibn Uqbah to take the charities of their wealth after the battle. When they heard of that, some men among them rode out, saying: We magnify the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), honor him in the lands, and speak to him. When he heard of them, he returned and said: O Messenger of Allah, Banu al-Mustaliq withheld their charities. When they heard he returned, they followed on his trail until they came to Medina, and prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the first row of the first prayer. When he gave salam, they said: We seek refuge in Allah and His Messenger from His anger and His Messenger's anger. They kept excusing themselves until the caller came with the Asr prayer. So Allah the Exalted revealed: {O you who believed, if a disobedient one comes to you with news, verify, lest you strike a people in ignorance}, the verse."
"4448","Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Abd al-Jalil, he is Ibn Atiyyah, narrated, Abu Mijlaz narrated, he said: Then Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, lay on his back in a garden of Medina's gardens, placing one leg over the other. The Jews used to fabricate against Allah, mighty and majestic, saying: Allah, blessed and exalted, finished creation on Saturday, then rested. So Allah, mighty and majestic, said: {And We certainly created the heavens and the earth and what is between them in six days, and no fatigue touched Us}. And some people disliked placing one leg over the other until Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did it."
"4449","Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, regarding the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: {And tall palm trees with arranged clusters}, he said: Tall means long, and arranged means piled."
"4450","Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: I asked him, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about: {And the retreat of the stars}? He said: After prostrations are the two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and the retreat of the stars are the two rak'ahs before dawn."
"4451","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Ayyub, he is al-Sakhtiyani, from Mujahid, regarding His saying: {So turn away from them, for you are not blamed}, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: No verse came down harder upon us nor greater upon us than it. We said: This is only of anger or displeasure, until was revealed: {And remind, for reminder benefits the believers}. He said: Remind with the Qur'an."
"4452","Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, Ayyub narrated, from Mujahid who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came out to us wrapped in a cloak, covered in a garment. He said: When: {So turn away from them, for you are not blamed} was revealed to us, it was hard upon the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); none of us remained except he feared destruction, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to turn away from them, until was revealed: {And remind, for reminder benefits the believers}; so our souls were eased."
"4453","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, Ayyub narrated, and he mentioned it with the wording: That grieved us, and we said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to turn away from us, until was revealed; and the rest is the like of it, and he did not say: So our souls were eased."
"4454","I said: Al-Haytham ibn Kulayb narrated it in his Musnad, from Isma'il ibn Ishaq the judge, from Sulayman ibn Harb, from Hammad ibn Zayd with it, and fuller than it. And the hadith of Mujahid from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, is with Ahmad in his Musnad for a hadith other than this."
"4455","Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Ar'arah who said: When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, that frightened me severely, and drawing the sword among us was grave. So I sat in my house, then I had a need, and went to the market, and there were a group in the shade of the palace sitting, about forty men, and a chain was laid across the door. I said: I shall surely enter. I went to enter, and the doorkeeper prevented me, and the people said to him: Let him, woe to you. I went, and there were the nobles of the people, and a cushion. Then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came, a handsome man in a suit of his, without shirt or turban. He gave salam, then sat. None of the people objected except me. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Ask me what you will, and do not ask me except what benefits and does not harm. A man said to him: What did you say until we wished to say to you, so I ask you? He said: Ask me what you will. He said: {By the scattering winds}? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Will you not ask of other than this? He said: I ask you of what I want. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The winds. He said: And the heavily laden? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The clouds. He said: And the smooth runners? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The ships. He said: And the distributors of command? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The angels; and he mentioned the hadith at length, and in it the one asked is Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4456","Al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Azraq al-Abdi narrated to us, dictating in Baghdad, he of Basrah's people, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, and he mentioned it at length."
"4457","Ibn Mani' said: al-Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated, from Abu Harb, from al-Aswad, and from a man, from Zadhdhan, they said: While the people were one day with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, they found him in a good mood, and said: Narrate to us of your companions, O Commander of the Faithful; and he mentioned the hadith. He said: Abdullah ibn al-Kawwa the one-eyed, a man of Banu Bakr ibn Wa'il, stood and said: O Commander of the Faithful, what are the scattering winds? And he mentioned the like of it, and added: He said regarding: {By the sky full of paths}? He said: Of goodly creation. And he added therein also: Do not return to the like of this; do not ask me of the like of this. This is part of a hadith he drove at length, and I separated it in its chapters."
"4458","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Mas'adah ibn al-Yasa' narrated to us, al-Jurayri narrated, from Abu al-Ala ibn al-Shikhkhir who said: When: {By the Lord of heaven and earth, indeed it is truth, just as you speak} was revealed, men went out with sticks in their hands, saying: Where are those who burdened our Lord until He swore?"
"4459","Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Ar'arah who said: Then another stood and said: Inform me of what I ask you. He said: Ask me of what benefits and does not harm. He said: What is the raised roof? He said: The sky. He said: And the frequented House? Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to his companions: What do you say? They said: This House: the Ka'bah. He said: No, but it is a house in heaven facing the Sacred House, called al-Durah; its sanctity in heaven is as the sanctity of this on earth; seventy thousand angels enter it each day, then never return to it."
"4460","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Abdillah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Nafi' who said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, while present, was asked about this verse: {Those who avoid major sins and shameful deeds, except small faults}. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It is the glance, the wink, the kiss, and the embrace; if the two circumcised parts meet, it is adultery, and washing becomes obligatory."
"4461","Yahya narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, recited al-Najm, prostrated, then stood and recited another surah."
"4462","Abu Yala said: Abdillah ibn Umar narrated to us, Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding: {When there covered the Lote Tree what covered}: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw it till I confirmed it, then golden moths intervened."
"4463","Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ubaydullah al-Ashja'i narrated, from Sufyan, from Hakim ibn al-Daylam, from al-Dahhak, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding: {While you are proudly strutting}: They used to pass by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrogantly; have you not seen the stallion how it strides arrogantly?"
"4464","Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated, from Qatadah who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: When: {The gathering will be defeated and they will turn their backs}, the verse, was revealed, I kept saying: Which gathering will be defeated? When the Day of Badr came and I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaping in his armor, saying: {The gathering will be defeated and they will turn their backs}, I knew it was it. Ma'mar said: Ayyub informed me, from Ikrimah, the like of it. This is disconnected."
"4465","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated, from Ali ibn Abi Talhah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His saying: {The Hour drew near and the moon split}, he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The splitting of the moon at Makkah is past."
"4466","With it, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding: {The gathering will be defeated and they will turn their backs}, he said: The Day of Badr."
"4467","Regarding His saying: {Then it will be an inevitable binding}, he said: The Day of Badr."
"4468","The hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, on its virtue has passed in manners, in the chapter: What one says when a dog barks at him. Abu Bakr said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us."
"4469","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Nasr narrated to us. They said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from al-Jurayri, from Muhammad ibn Sa'd ibn Malik who said: Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, when he recited this verse: {And for whoever feared the standing of his Lord are two gardens}, said: Even if he fornicated and stole. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught it to me. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: This is when he repented."
"4470","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Muhammad, Sadqah ibn Hurmuz narrated, from al-Jurayri, from Muhammad ibn Sa'd who said: I was with Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: He recited to us this verse: {And for whoever feared the standing of his Lord are two gardens}, even if he fornicated and stole. I said: The people do not recite it thus. He repeated it three times and said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited."
"4471","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said regarding: {Wide-eyed maidens restrained in pavilions}: The hollow pearl."
"4472","Al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated, Shuja' narrated, from Abu Zabyah, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites Surah al-Waqi'ah every night, poverty will never strike him. And Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to command his daughters to recite it every night."
"4473","Abu Yala said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Munib al-Adani narrated, al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to me with it."
"4474","Abdan narrated to us, Nuh narrated, from his brother Khalid ibn Qays, from Qatadah, regarding His saying: {And piled bananas}, he said: Bananas."
"4475","Al-Tayalisi said: Shayban narrated to us, from Jabir, from Yazid ibn Murrah, from Salamah ibn Yazid al-Ju'fi who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying regarding the saying of Allah, mighty and majestic: {Indeed, We produced them in a creation, then made them virgins, loving, of equal age}, he said: Among them are the previously married and other than the previously married."
"4476","Abu Dawud and Musaddad both said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Uqbah ibn Suhban, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying: {A multitude of the former and a few of the latter}, he said: Both are of this nation. Al-Hajjaj narrated it from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ali, raised."
"4477","Musaddad said: Khaqan ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ahtam narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Uqbah ibn Suhban, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with it."
"4478","Abu Yala said: Muhammad al-Muqaddami narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated, from Mutarrif, from Abu Hazim, from Awn ibn Abdillah, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When: {Has not the time come for those who believed that their hearts relent to the remembrance of Allah} was revealed, some of us turned to others: What have we innovated? What have we done?"
"4479","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In the Book of Allah is a verse none acted upon before me nor will act upon after me, the verse of private counsel: {O you who believed, when you confer privately with the Messenger, advance before your private counsel a charity}, to the end of the verse. He said: I had a dinar, I sold it for ten dirhams, and conferred privately with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); each time I conferred with him, I advanced a dirham before my private counsel. Then it was abrogated, and none acted upon it. So was revealed: {Do you fear to advance before your private counsels charities}, to the end of the verse."
"4480","Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated it, from Abdillah ibn Idris, from Layth with it. I said: Al-Tirmidhi narrated it through Ali ibn Alqamah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, with other than this wording."
"4481","Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, al-Thawri informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Humayd ibn Abdillah al-Saluli, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A monk used to worship in a cell, and a woman having brothers, something befell her, so they brought her to him. She adorned herself to him, and he fell upon her, and she conceived. Satan came to him and said: Kill her, for if they appear against you, you will be disgraced. So he killed her and buried her. They came to him, seized him, and took him away. While they walked with him, Satan came to him and said: I am the one who adorned it to you, so prostrate to me once and I will save you. So he prostrated to him. That is His saying: {Like Satan when he said to man: Disbelieve; and when he disbelieved, he said: I am quit of you}."
"4482","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated, Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji narrated, he said: A Muslim man passed fasting three days walking, finding nothing to break fast with, waking fasting, until a man of the Ansar called Thabit ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, noticed him. He said to his family: Tonight I come with a guest of mine; when you lay your food, let one of you rise to the lamp as if fixing it, and extinguish it, then strike with your hands to the food as if eating, and do not eat until our guest is full. In the evening he went with him; they laid their food, his wife rose to the lamp as if fixing it and extinguished it, then they struck with their hands in the food as if eating, and did not eat, until their guest was full; their food was but a loaf, their sustenance. When morning came, Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Thabit, Allah the Exalted wondered last night at you and your deed. He said: So this verse was revealed about him: {And they prefer others over themselves though poverty be upon them}."
"4483","Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Qays, he is Ibn al-Rabi', narrated, from Uthman ibn Abi Zur'ah, from a freedwoman of Abu Musa, from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying, blessed and exalted: {And the foremost, the first of the Emigrants}, he said: Whoever prayed the two qiblahs with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4484","Abu Yala said: Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated to us, Hafs narrated, Ibn Jurayj narrated, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He permitted them to cut the palms, then was strict with them. They came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there sin upon us for what we cut or what we left? So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: {Whatever you cut of the soft palms or left standing upon their roots, it is by Allah's permission}."
"4485","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abu al-Malih, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said regarding His saying: {And that they not disobey you in what is right}: It is wailing. This is mursal, good in chain."
"4486","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Mindal narrated, from al-Kalbi, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, accepted Islam, and his wife stayed among the polytheists. So, mighty and majestic, revealed: {And do not hold to the ties of disbelieving women}, saying: If a man accepts Islam and his wife refuses, let him marry, if he wills, four others."
"4487","Al-Harith said: Asim ibn Ali narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated, from al-Agharr ibn al-Sabbah, from Khalifah ibn Husayn, from Abu Nasr al-Asadi who said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was asked: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) test the women? He said: When a woman came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to accept Islam, he made her swear by Allah: You did not go out hating your husband; by Allah, you did not go out seeking worldly gain; by Allah, you did not go out desiring one land to another; by Allah, you went out only loving Allah the Exalted and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4488","Al-Tayalisi said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated. And Abu Yala said: Ibrahim al-Sami narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Mus'ab ibn Thabit ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amir ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, divorced his wife Qutaylah in the pre-Islamic time, she is the mother of Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both. She came to them in the truce between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the disbelievers of Quraysh, and gifted to Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, earrings and things, but she disliked accepting from her until she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him. So Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: {Allah does not forbid you from those who did not fight you in religion}. The wording of Abu Dawud. And in the other's narration: Qutaylah bint Abd al-Uzza ibn Asad of Banu Malik ibn Hisl came to her daughter Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, with gifts: lizards, clarified butter, and dried curd. She did not accept her gifts nor admit her to her house. A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that for her, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Allah does not forbid you from those who did not fight you in religion}, the verse. So she admitted her to her house and accepted her gifts."
"4489","Abu Khaythamah narrated to us. And al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to me, Abu Zumayl narrated, he said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah wrote a letter to the people of Makkah, and Allah the Exalted informed His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Ali and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, after the letter; they overtook the woman on a camel and extracted it from her braids, and brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He sent for Hatib and said: O Hatib, did you write this letter? He said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What carried you to that? He said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I am indeed sincere to Allah the Exalted and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but I was a stranger among Makkah's people, my family among them, and I feared. So I wrote a letter harming nothing of Allah and His Messenger, hoping it be a benefit for my family. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I drew my sword, then said: O Messenger of Allah, let me have Hatib, for he disbelieved, that I strike his neck. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O son of al-Khattab, what informs you? Perhaps Allah looked upon this band of Badr's people and said: Do what you will, for I have forgiven you. Its chain is sahih. Al-Humaydi mentioned from al-Barqani that Muslim brought it. Al-Humaydi said: Neither Khalaf nor Abu Mas'ud mentioned it. I said: Muslim brought with this chain several hadiths other than this."
"4490","Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Harun al-Madani narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Abdillah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul said to his father: By Allah, you shall never enter Medina until you say: The Messenger of Allah is the mightiest, and I am the lowest. He said: And he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, it reached me you intend to kill my father. By Him who sent you with truth, I never looked at his face out of awe of him; if you wish I bring you his head, I shall bring it to you, for I hate to see my father's killer."
"4491","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from Amr ibn Salim, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When this verse in Surah al-Baqarah on women's waiting periods was revealed, they said: Waiting periods of women remain unmentioned: the young, the old whose menstruation ceased, and the pregnant. So Allah revealed the verse in the lesser Surah of women: {And those who despair of menstruation among your women, if you doubt, their period is three months, and those who have not menstruated, and those with loads, their term is to lay down their load}."
"4492","Yahya ibn Adam informed us, from al-Mufaddal ibn Muhalhal, from Mutarrif ibn Tarif, from Amr ibn Salim who said: When the verse in Surah al-Baqarah on the divorced woman's waiting period and the widow's waiting period was revealed, my father said: O Messenger of Allah, and he mentioned the like of it."
"4493","Al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, Muhammad ibn Amr narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Ibn Abbas, regarding His saying: {And let them not go out except they bring a clear indecency}, he said: The clear indecency is to abuse her family; if she does that, expelling her becomes lawful for them."
"4494","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Ma'mar ibn Aban narrated, al-Zuhri narrated, from Urwah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: When Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, swore not to spend on Mistah, may Allah be pleased with him, Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: {Allah has ordained for you the dissolution of your oaths}; so he dissolved his oath and spent upon him."
"4495","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding His saying: {A sincere repentance}, he said: He repents of the sin, then does not return to it. This chain is sahih."
"4496","Abu Ya'la said: Hudbah and Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, both said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned a hadith, he said: and with its isnad to Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Pharaoh drove four pegs into his wife's hands and feet; when they dispersed from her the angels released her, and she said: (My Lord, build for me with You a house in Paradise). He said: So her house in Paradise was unveiled to her. Sahih, mawquf."
"4497","Chapter Surah Tabarak; in it a hadith on the tafsir of Alif Lam Mim, Tanzil, al-Sajdah. 'Abd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said to a man: Shall I present you with a hadith that will delight you? He said: Yes, O Abu 'Abbas, may Allah have mercy on you. He said: Recite (Blessed is He in whose Hand is the dominion), memorize it, teach it to your family, all your children, the children of your house and your neighbors; for it is the savior and the disputant: it will dispute and plead on the Day of Resurrection before its Lord for its reciter, and ask its Lord to save him from the Fire if it was in his belly, and Allah the Exalted saves its companion by it from the punishment of the grave. Ibn 'Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I wish it were in the heart of every human being of my ummah. Al-Tirmidhi narrated it abridged. Al-Bazzar transmitted it from Salamah ibn Shabib, from Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam, limiting it to the marfu' part, but he said: meaning Ya Sin, and said: We do not know it narrated from Ibn 'Abbas except with this isnad."
"4498","Chapter Surah Nun. Abu Ya'la said: al-Qasim ibn Yahya narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abu Sa'id Rawh ibn Janah narrated to us, from a freed slave of 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about (The Day the shin will be uncovered): He said: From a great light, they will fall down to it prostrate."
"4499","Chapter Surah al-Haqqah. Ishaq said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, 'Imran Abu al-Hudhayl informed us, 'Abdullah ibn Wahb ibn Munabbih informed us, from his father, about His Exalted saying (And your Lord's Throne will be carried above them): He said: They are four angels carrying it on their shoulders; each one of them has four faces: the face of a bull, the face of a lion, the face of an eagle, and the face of a human; each one has four wings: two wings over his face lest he look at the Throne and fall thunderstruck, and two wings with which they rise with it; they have no speech except: Glorify Allah the Strong, the Most High; His greatness has filled what is between the heavens and the earth. This is mawquf, with a weak isnad."
"4500","Chapter Surah Sa'ala. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Salih, about His Blessed and Exalted saying (Stripping away the limbs skin): He said: The flesh of the legs."
"4501","Chapter Surah al-Jinn. Ishaq said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn Ghaylan al-Thaqafi, that he said to Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): I was told you were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn? He said: Yes, and he mentioned the hadith similar to it, meaning similar to the hadith of 'Alqamah, and said: He drew a line for me and said: Do not move from it; when he came he said to me: Had you left the line I would not have been sure that some of them would snatch you away; and he said: The jinn disputed over a slain one among them, and it was judged between them with truth. And he said: I saw them wrapping themselves in one another's garments; and he said: They are the jinn of Nasibin; they asked him for provision."
"4502","Ishaq was informed by Jarir, from Qabus ibn Abi Zibyan, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out, and I set out with him, until he reached the open land, then he drew a line for me and said: Do not move until I return to you; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not come until dawn approached, then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was sent to the jinn. I said: What are these voices I hear? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are their voices when they bade me farewell and greeted me."
"4503","Chapter Surah al-Muzzammil. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), about His Mighty and Majestic saying (And recite the Qur'an with tartil): He said: Expound it distinctly."
"4504","Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) recited this verse: (Indeed the night prayer is severer in tread and more upright in speech), and a man said to him: We only recite it: more upright in speech. He said: Aq wam, aswab, ahya and the like of this are one."
"4505","Ja'far ibn Mihran narrated to us, 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: When (Leave Me with the deniers of ease and grant them a little respite) was revealed, only a short while passed until the battle of Badr occurred."
"4506","Chapter Surah al-Muddaththir. Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, Sayf narrated to us, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: While Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) was sitting in the Zamzam enclosure and people were asking him, a man came and asked him about (And the night when it retreats). He remained silent, and when the caller gave the second call, he said: Where is the one asking about: The night when it retreats? He said: The night has retreated."
"4507","Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Ibn Sama'ah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Habibah or Umm Habibah, who said: We were in 'A'ishah's house when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and said: There are no two Muslims whose three young children die before reaching puberty except they are brought until made to stand at the gate of Paradise, and it is said to them: Enter Paradise, you and your fathers; that is His Exalted saying (So the intercession of intercessors will not benefit them). He added: The intercession of the children benefited the fathers."
"4508","Chapter Surah al-Mursalat. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn 'Ar'arah, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) who said about (By those sent forth in succession): The winds; he mentioned it in a long hadith."
"4509","Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn 'Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us, Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, al-Kalbi narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), about His Mighty and Majestic saying (And We sent down from the rain-laden clouds abundant water): He said: al-Mu'sirat are the winds; (abundant water) he said: poured forth."
"4510","Chapter Surah al-Naba'. Ibn Abi 'Umar said: Marwan narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about Allah Mighty and Majestic's saying (Abiding therein for ages): A huqb is a thousand months, a month is thirty days, a year is three hundred and sixty days, a day is a thousand years of what you count, and the huqub are thirty thousand thousand years."
"4511","Chapter Surah al-Takwir. Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn 'Ar'arah, who said: What are al-Jawar al-Kunnas? He said: The stars; meaning 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him); he mentioned it in a long hadith."
"4512","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Sufyan informed us, from Simak, from al-Sha'bi, who said: I heard 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) while on the minbar saying about (And when the souls are paired): Their pairing is that each people be joined to their likes."
"4513","Chapter Surah (When the sky splits). Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Suwayd, Abu Rafi' narrated to me, he said: I prayed 'Isha' behind 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and he recited (When the sky splits) and prostrated in it."
"4514","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us, Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) about (You will surely ride stage after stage): He said, meaning your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): condition after condition."
"4515","Chapter Surah al-Balad. Abu Ya'la said: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, Nu'aym ibn Maysarah narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, a man of Banu 'Amir informed me, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I prayed behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him say: (Does he think no one will have power over him) (Does he think no one saw him), meaning with fathah on the sin of yahsab."
"4516","Abu Bakr said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Hafs ibn Sa'id al-Qurashi al-A'war narrated to us, he said: My mother narrated to me, from her mother, who was a servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: A puppy entered the Prophet's house and went under the bed and died; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained four days with no revelation descending, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Khawlah, what has happened in the house of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Jibril (peace be upon him) does not come to me; what has happened in the house of the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: O Prophet of Allah, no day better than today has passed us. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took his cloaks, wore them and went out; I said to myself: If only I tidy the house and sweep it; I swept with the broom under the bed and felt something heavy, and kept moving it until the dead puppy appeared to me; I took it in my hand and threw it behind the house. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came with his beard trembling, and when revelation descended upon him shivering seized him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Khawlah, cover me up. So Allah Mighty and Majestic sent down upon him: (By the morning brightness, and the night when it covers, your Lord has not forsaken you nor hates you) up to His saying (and you shall be pleased). He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose, I placed water for him, he purified himself and wore his cloaks."
"4517","Chapter (When the earth is shaken). Ishaq said: Yazid ibn Harun al-Wasiti informed us, Sufyan ibn Husayn informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma', who said: While Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was having lunch with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), this verse was sent down: (So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it). Abu Bakr stopped and said: O Messenger of Allah, will we see all the evil we did? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What you see of what you dislike is part of what you are recompensed with, and good is stored up for its people in the Hereafter. Ahmad transmitted it with its meaning in his question about His Exalted saying (It is not by your wishes nor the wishes of the People of the Book) by way of Abu Bakr ibn Abi Zuhayr al-Thaqafi, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him). Al-Tirmidhi transmitted part of it by way of Ibn 'Umar from Abu Bakr. Its isnad is weak, and the route we cited is sound if Abu Asma' heard from Abu Bakr."
"4518","Chapter Surah al-Ma'un. al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah narrated to us, from Abi 'A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us, then he mentioned a very long hadith, in it: Whoever withholds ma'un from his neighbor when he needs it, Allah will withhold His bounty from him on the Day of Resurrection, entrust him to himself, and whoever is entrusted to himself perishes in the end, and no excuse is accepted from him."
"4519","Chapter on the merit of: Say O disbelievers, and what comes after it to the end of the Qur'an. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hisham Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Hakam al-Qudaydi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid al-Rifa'i, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im, that he heard Jubayr ibn Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Would you like, O Jubayr, when you go on a journey, to be the best of your companions in appearance and the most abundant in provision? I said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransomed for you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite these five surahs: (Say O disbelievers) and (When Allah's help and victory come) and (Say He is Allah, One) and (Say I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak) and (Say I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind), begin every surah with In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, and end your recitation with In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Jubayr said: I was rich with much wealth, yet I would go on journeys as the shabbiest in appearance and least in provision; ever since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught them to me and I recited them, I am among the best in appearance and most abundant in provision until I return from my journey."
"4520","Musaddad said: al-Jurayri narrated to us, a man of Kufah among them narrated to us, from a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more than twenty times saying: What excellent two surahs you recited in the two rak'ahs: al-Ahad al-Samad, and (Say O disbelievers)."
"4521","Abu Ya'la said: Jabbarah narrated to us, al-Hajjaj ibn Tamim narrated to us, from Maymun, who said: Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I guide you to a word that saves you from associating partners with Allah Blessed and Exalted? Recite (Say O disbelievers) at your sleep."
"4522","Chapter Surah (When Allah's help and victory come). Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn 'Ubaydah narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: This surah was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the middle of the Tashriq days at Mina while he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the Farewell Pilgrimage: (When Allah's help and victory come) until he completed it; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew it was the farewell. The hadith; it was already cited in the chapter on the sanctity of Makkah."
"4523","'Abd said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with its full length."
"4524","Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr, that is Ibn Harb, narrated to us, Buhlul, that is Ibn Muwarraq, narrated to us, Musa ibn 'Ubaydah narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Yasar and 'Abdullah ibn Dinar narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) with its full length."
"4525","Chapter Surah Tabbat. al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, al-Walid that is Ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Ibn Tadrus, from Asma' bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When (Perish the hands of Abu Lahab, and perish he) was revealed, al-'Awra' Umm Jamil bint Harb came wailing with a stone in her hand, saying: We refuse the blameworthy, his religion we hate, his command we disobey; while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat in the mosque reciting Qur'an, and with him was Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: O Messenger of Allah, she has come and I fear she may see you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She will not see me; and he recited Qur'an by which he sought protection, as He Mighty and Majestic said: (When you recite the Qur'an We place between you and those who do not believe in the Hereafter a hidden veil). She came until she stood over Abu Bakr without seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and said: O Abu Bakr, I was told your companion lampooned me. He said: No, by the Lord of this House, he did not lampoon you. She turned away saying: Quraysh know I am the daughter of their chief. Al-Walid said in his hadith, or another said it: Umm Jamil stumbled while circumambulating the House in her cloak and said: Perish Muzammam; Umm Hakim bint 'Abd al-Muttalib said: I am chaste, not to be spoken to, wise, not to be taught, and both of us are of the cousins, then Quraysh know best after."
"4526","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Musa al-Harawi Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Ibn Tadrus, from Asma' (may Allah be pleased with her) with its full length."
"4527","Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: When (Perish the hands of Abu Lahab) was revealed, the wife of Abu Lahab came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was with him; when Abu Bakr saw her he said: O Messenger of Allah, she is a foul-tongued woman and I fear she may harm you; if only you stand? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She will not see me. She came and said: O Abu Bakr, your companion lampooned me. He said: He does not compose poetry. She said: You are believed with me; and she left. I said: O Messenger of Allah, she did not see you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An angel kept shielding me with his wing."
"4528","Al-Bazzar said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari and Ahmad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, both said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us with it."
"4529","Chapter Surah al-Ikhlas. al-Tayalisi said: Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd narrated to us, from 'Umayr the freed slave of Banu 'Adi, that he heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is any of you unable to recite a third of the Qur'an before sleeping? It was said: O Messenger of Allah, who can bear a third of the Qur'an before sleeping? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He recites (Say He is Allah, One) and (Say I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak) and (Say I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind), as if he recited a third of the Qur'an."
"4530","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn 'Amir al-Aslami narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Sahl ibn Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, or a man of the Ansar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites (Say He is Allah, One), it is as if he recited a third of the Qur'an."
"4531","Yusuf ibn 'Atiyyah al-Saffar narrated to us, from Harun ibn Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Abu Umamah, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with them both), raising it, who said: Whoever recites (Say He is Allah, One), it is as if he recited a third of the Qur'an, and good deeds are written for him as many as those who believed and those who associated partners."
"4532","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, al-'Ala' Abu Muhammad al-Thaqafi informed us, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned the hadith about the story of Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Laythi (may Allah be pleased with him) and the Prophet's prayer at Tabuk."
"4533","Chapter Surah al-Mu'awwidhatayn. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yusuf ibn 'Atiyyah narrated to us, Harun ibn Kathir narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites the Mu'awwidhat, it is as if he recited all that Allah Exalted sent down upon Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4534","Abu Ya'la said: Khalifah ibn Khayyat narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, Mughallis al-Khurasani narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Yazid, from Abi Razin, from 'Amr ibn 'Abasah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in Fajr: (Say I seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak) and (Say I seek refuge with the Lord of mankind); and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: al-Falaq is Jahannam."
"4535","al-Azraq ibn 'Ali narrated to us, Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, al-Salt ibn Bahram narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, who said: 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) used to scrape the Mu'awwidhatayn from the mushaf, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded seeking refuge with them; and 'Abdullah did not recite them."
"4536","Book of Merits; chapter on the signs of prophethood. Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and others (may Allah be pleased with them), joining the hadith from 'Urwah, who said: The first apostasy among the Arabs was the apostasy of Musaylamah ibn Habib the liar, chief of Yamamah, and al-Aswad ibn Ka'b al-'Ansi in Yemen, in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw on my arm two bracelets of gold, I blew on them and they flew away; I interpreted them as the liar of Yamamah and the liar of San'a'. In it is a break."
"4537","'Abd al-Muhaymin that is Ibn 'Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd informed us, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Before the mosque was built the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray toward a wooden post; when the mosque was built a mihrab was built for him, he advanced to it, and that post moaned like the moaning of a camel; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his hand on it and it calmed."
"4538","al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah narrated to us, from Abi 'A'ishah al-Sa'di, from Yazid ibn 'Amr, from Abi Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them) both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us with a sermon before his death, then he mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: O people, there will be thirty liars in this ummah, the first of them the chief of Yamamah and the chief of San'a'."
"4539","Abu Bakr said: Hushaym narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given the openings of speech, its comprehensives and its seals. He said: We said: Teach us of what Allah Exalted taught you; so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the tashahhud."
"4540","Ishaq said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us, Isma'il ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn al-Sufayra' informed us, from Abi al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey; he would not relieve himself until he was out of sight, so we stopped in a barren land with neither tree nor landmark; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Jabir, go, put water in the waterskin, then come with us until we are unseen. He said: There were two trees with some cubits between them; he said to me: O Jabir, go to these two trees and tell them: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you to join, so that I may sit behind you; they came, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat behind them, then they returned to their places. He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a desert as if birds shaded us over our heads; a woman with her boy met us and said: O Messenger of Allah, this boy is seized by the devil three times a day. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped, took the boy, carried him between himself and the front of the saddle, then said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Begone, enemy of Allah, I am the Messenger of Allah. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed the boy to her. When we finished our march we passed that place; the woman met us with her boy, with two rams, and said: O Messenger of Allah, accept these two from me; by Him who sent you with truth, it has not returned to him since. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take one of them and return the other. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled, we traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among us as if birds shaded us; a stray camel came until it fell prostrate between the two rows; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped and said to the people: Who owns this camel? Some Ansari youths said: It is ours, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is its matter? They said: We worked it for twenty years; it had some fat, so we wanted to slaughter it and divide it among our boys. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you sell it to me? They said: Rather it is yours, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nay; be kind to it until its term comes. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we are more entitled to prostrate to you than the beasts. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it proper for a human to prostrate to a human, I would have commanded the wife to prostrate to her husband for his great right over her. Al-Darimi narrated it in his Musnad from 'Ubaydullah with its full length. Isma'il is bad in memory; al-Daraqutni mentioned he alone narrated this hadith in its full length. Abu Dawud and Ibn Majah transmitted of it in Purification: When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to relieve himself he would go until no one saw him, only."
"4541","al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Nu'aym, from Hibban ibn Buhh al-Suda'i, the Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): My people disbelieved, and I was told he had dispatched an army to them; I came to him and said to him: My people are upon Islam. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is that so? I said: Yes. He said: I followed him my night until morning, I called the adhan for prayer when morning came, he gave me a vessel in which I performed ablution; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) put his fingers in the vessel and springs gushed forth, then he said: Whoever of you wants to perform ablution, let him do so. I performed ablution and prayed; he appointed me over them and gave me their charity. A man stood before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: So-and-so wronged me; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no good in leadership for a Muslim man. Then a man came asking for charity; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Charity is a headache in the head, a burning in the belly, and a disease. So I gave him my two documents: the document of my governorship and of my charity. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is your affair? I said: How can I accept them when I heard from you what I heard? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is as you heard."
"4542","Chapter on his generosity and liberality (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ya'la said: Zakariyya and Ishaq narrated to us, both said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Sayyar, from Abi Hubayrah al-Ansari, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned the hadith of the camel. Jabir said: I met a Jewish man and told him what happened; he kept marveling, saying: He gave you the price and returned the camel to you."
"4543","Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abbadani narrated to us, Suwayd ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, from Nuh ibn Dhakwan, from his brother Ayyub, from al-Hasan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Shall I tell you of the Most Generous? Allah is the Most Generous, and I am the most generous of Adam's children (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4544","Chapter on his giving justice from himself (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Abd said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from Abi Harun al-'Abdi, from Abi Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A weak man of the Muhajirin had a need of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he wanted to meet him in private to disclose his need. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was encamped at al-Batha'; he would come at night and circumambulate the House until dawn approached, then return and lead his Companions in the morning prayer. He said: The circumambulation detained him one night until morning; when he mounted his camel the man confronted him, seized the halter of his she-camel and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have a need of you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will attain your need; but he refused. When he feared he would detain him, he struck him lightly with the whip, then went and led them in the morning prayer. When he finished he turned his face to the people, and when he did that they knew something had happened; the people gathered around him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is the one I struck just now? He repeated it: If he is among the people, let him stand. The man kept saying: I seek refuge with Allah then with His Messenger; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept saying: Come near, come near, until he drew near to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat before him and handed him the whip, saying: Take your retaliation and exact it. He said: I seek refuge with Allah from flogging His Prophet. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take your retaliation, there is no harm upon you. He said: I seek refuge with Allah from flogging His Prophet. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Unless you pardon. He said: He threw the whip and said: I have pardoned, O Messenger of Allah. Then Abu Dharr stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, do you remember the night of al-'Aqabah? I drove for you while you slept; when she slowed or I took her halter she turned aside, so I struck you lightly with the whip and said: The people have come to you; you said: No harm upon you. Take, O Messenger of Allah, and exact it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have pardoned. He said: Exact it, for it is dearer to me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged him; he said: I saw him writhing from it. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, fear Allah; by Allah, no believer wrongs a believer except Allah Exalted avenges him on the Day of Resurrection."
"4545","Chapter on the blessing of his supplication (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); in it the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij about wiping his belly, and he never complained of it after; and the hadith of Anas in the chapter of proposal in the Book of Marriage. Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar narrated to us, a man of Banu Salaman ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from his mother, who said: Her maternal uncle Habib ibn Fuwayk told her that his father took him out to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his eyes white, seeing nothing with them; he asked him: What befell you? He said: I was training a camel of mine and placed my foot on a snake's egg, so I was afflicted. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat into his eyes and he saw. He said: I saw him threading the thread into the needle at eighty years old, though his eyes were white."
"4546","'Abd said: 'Umar ibn Sa'd al-Hafari narrated to us, from Badr ibn 'Uthman, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Marwan, Abu 'A'ishah narrated to us, from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one morning and said: Before Fajr prayer I saw as if I was given the keys and the scales; as for the keys, they are these keys, and as for the scales, they are those weighed with. I was placed in one pan and my ummah placed in the other; I outweighed them. Then Abu Bakr was brought and weighed and outweighed them, then 'Umar was brought and weighed and outweighed them, then 'Uthman was brought and weighed and outweighed them; then I woke and they were lifted."
"4547","Abu Ya'la said: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Adhrami narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Imran narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahim ibn al-Harith ibn 'Ubaydah, from his grandfather, who said: Abu Dharr's eye was struck on the day of Uhud; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat into it, and it became his soundest eye."
"4548","Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Rifa'ah Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya al-Sadafi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, Kharijah ibn Zayd informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his Hajj which he performed; when we descended the valley of al-Rawha', a woman with a boy met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she greeted him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he stopped for her, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, this is my son So-and-so; by Him who sent you with truth, he has remained in one fit ever since I bore him until now, or a word like it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bent toward her, spread his hand, placed him between himself and the saddle, then spat (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into his mouth, then said: Come out, enemy of Allah, for I am the Messenger of Allah. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed him to her and said: Take him; you will never see anything with him that alarms you after today, if Allah Exalted wills. Usamah said: We completed our Hajj, then departed; when we stopped at al-Rawha', that woman, the boy's mother, appeared with a roasted sheep, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I am the mother of the boy I brought to you; by Him who sent you with truth, I have seen nothing alarming from him until this hour. Usamah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Usaym, and al-Zuhri said: thus he used to call him endearingly, hand me its foreleg. He said: I tore off the foreleg and handed it to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he ate it (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said: O Usaym, hand me the foreleg; I tore off the foreleg and handed it to him, and he ate it. Then he said: O Usaym, hand me the foreleg. I said: O Messenger of Allah, you said hand me and I handed it and you ate it, then said hand me and I handed it and you ate it, then said hand me the foreleg, yet a sheep has only two forelegs. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you but reached for it you would have kept finding a foreleg in it as long as I asked. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Usaym, rise, go out and see whether you see a place that conceals the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I went out, walked until I tired, not passing the people, and saw nothing I thought would conceal anyone, while the people filled what was between the two dams. I told him; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did you see trees or heaps of stones? I said: Yes, I saw small palms beside heaps of stones. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Usaym, go to the palms and tell them: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands you that some of you join others until you are a screen for the Messenger of Allah's relief, and say likewise to the heaps. I came to the palms and told them what he commanded; by Him who sent him with truth, I watched them tear up by their roots and soil until they stuck together as one palm, and I said that to the stones; by Him who sent him with truth, I watched them tear up stone by stone until some rose upon others as a wall. I came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take the waterskin; I took it, then we walked; when we neared them I preceded him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), placed the waterskin, then withdrew to him; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withdrew until he relieved himself, then came back carrying the waterskin; I took it from him, then we returned. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the tent he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Usaym, go to the palms and tell them: The Messenger of Allah commands you that every palm return to its place, and say that to the stones. I came to the palms and told them; by Him who sent him with truth, I watched them tear apart with their soil until every palm returned to its place, and I said that to the stones; by Him who sent him with truth, I watched them tear apart stone by stone until every stone returned to its place; I came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and told him that. This is a fair isnad; Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya al-Sadafi is weak, but his hadith has a witness by way of Ya'la ibn Murrah, transmitted by Ahmad and others."
"4549","al-Harith said: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Nu'aym al-Hadrami, who said: I heard Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Suda'i, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrating, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pledged Islam; I was told he had sent an army to my people, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, turn back the army, and I guarantee you my people's Islam and obedience. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Go and turn them back. I said: O Messenger of Allah, my mount is exhausted. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O brother of Suda', you are obeyed among your people. I said: Rather Allah Exalted guided them through you to Islam. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Shall I not appoint you over them? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote a document for me and appointed me. I said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), order for me something of their charities; so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote another document for me. Al-Suda'i said: That was on one of his journeys; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped at a dwelling, and its people came complaining of their agent, saying: O Messenger of Allah, he seized us for something between him and his people in Jahiliyyah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did he do that? They said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to his Companions, I among them, and said: There is no good in leadership for a believing man. Al-Suda'i said: His saying entered my heart. Then another came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, give me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever asks people out of sufficiency, it is a headache in the head and a disease in the belly. The man said: Give me of the charities. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Great and Majestic was not pleased therein with the judgment of a prophet or other than him until He judged therein Himself and apportioned it; if you are of those portions I will give you by your right. Al-Suda'i said: That entered my heart, that I had asked him while rich. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled with us from the first of the night; I kept with him, and I was strong, while his Companions kept breaking off and lagging until none remained with him but I. When dawn adhan time came he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered me and I called the adhan; I kept saying: Shall I iqamah, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would look toward the east at the dawn and say: No, until dawn rose. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dismounted, relieved himself, then returned to me while his Companions caught up. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is there water, O brother of Suda'? I said: No, except a little that will not suffice you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put it in a vessel, then bring it to me. I did; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his palm in the vessel, and I saw between every two fingers of his fingers (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a spring gushing. He said: O brother of Suda', were I not shy before my Lord Mighty and Majestic, we would drink and draw water. Call among my Companions: whoever needs water. I called; whoever wanted took of it. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood for prayer; Bilal wanted to iqamah, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The brother of Suda' called the adhan, so he gives the iqamah. Al-Suda'i said: I gave the iqamah; when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished prayer I brought him the two documents and said: O Messenger of Allah, relieve me of these two documents. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What has occurred to you? I said: I heard you, O Prophet of Allah, saying: There is no good in leadership for a believing man, and I believe in Allah and His Messenger; and I heard you say to the asker: Whoever asks people out of sufficiency, it is a headache in the head and a disease in the belly, yet I asked you while rich. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is as you heard; if you wish accept, if you wish leave. I said: Rather I leave. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Guide me to a man I may appoint over you. I guided him to a man of the delegation who had come to him, and he appointed him over us. Then I said: O Prophet of Allah, we have a well which in winter suffices us with water and we gather at it, and in summer its water lessens and we disperse to surrounding waters; we have embraced Islam while all around us are enemies, so supplicate Allah Exalted for us in our well that its water suffice us so we gather at it and not disperse. He called for six pebbles, rubbed them in his hand and supplicated over them, then said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Take these pebbles; when you reach the well, throw them one by one, mentioning Allah Exalted's name. Al-Suda'i said: We did; after that we could not see its bottom. Ahmad and Abu Dawud transmitted part of it separately."
"4550","Abu Ya'la said: Yahya ibn Ayyub and Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, the wording is Yahya's, both said: Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Among what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for me: O Allah, grant him wealth and children. I know none who attained a more pleasant life than I attained; with these two hands of mine I buried more than a hundred of my children, not counting grandchildren nor miscarriages. This hadith is transmitted with them with other than this wording."
"4551","'Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, he said: My father mentioned, though I do not think I heard it from him, from Yusuf ibn Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from his father, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the market, a woman seized his mount's bridle while he was on a donkey, saying: O Messenger of Allah, my husband does not approach me; separate me from him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is her husband? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said: What is with you and her? She comes complaining of your coldness, complaining you do not approach her? He said: O Messenger of Allah, by Him who honored you, my last time with her was this very night. The woman wept and said: He lies; separate me from him, for he is of Allah's most hateful creation to me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled, took his head and her head, joined them, and said: O Allah, draw each of them close to his companion. Jabir said: We stayed as long as Allah willed, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed through the market and saw a woman carrying tanned hides; when she saw him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) she threw the hides and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, by Him who sent you with truth, Allah created nothing dearer to me than him except you."
"4552","Muhammad ibn 'Abbad narrated to us, Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah, meaning Ibn Abi Muzarrid, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from his father, from Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: I entered the mosque and recognized hunger in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I went out until I came to Umm Sulaym (may Allah be pleased with her), the mother of Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who was under Malik father of Anas, and said: O Umm Sulaym, I recognized hunger in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); do you have anything? She said: I have something, gesturing with her palm. He said to her: Prepare and do well. She sent Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Whisper in his ear and invite him. When Anas came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Did your father send you inviting us, my son? He said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions (may Allah be pleased with them): Go, in Allah's name. He said: Anas hurried back until he reached Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) coming to you with the people. He said: I went out with him until we met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the door at the stair landing; I said: O Messenger of Allah, what have you done to us? I only recognized hunger in your face, so we prepared something for you to eat. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enter, and rejoice. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it, gathered it in the dish with his hand, then set it right, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Is there any? as if meaning condiment. He said: They brought him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a small skin with something in it, or nothing in it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) moved his hand in it and ghee flowed from it. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Admit ten by ten to me. They all ate and were satisfied. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of the remainder: Eat, you and your household. They ate and were satisfied. It is in the Sahih from the hadith of Anas."
"4553","Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Burjumi narrated to us, from Abi Zilal, from Anas ibn Malik, from his mother (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I had a ewe; I collected its ghee in a skin and filled the skin, then sent it with Rabibah, saying: O Rabibah, deliver this skin to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to use as condiment. Rabibah went with it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is ghee Umm Sulaym sent you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Empty her skin for her. The skin was emptied and handed back to her; she left and came to Umm Sulaym (may Allah be pleased with her), who saw the skin full and dripping. Umm Sulaym said: O Rabibah, did I not order you to go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with it? She said: I did; if you do not believe me, go ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umm Sulaym went with Rabibah and said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I sent you with her a skin with ghee? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She did; she brought it. She said: By Him who sent you with truth and the religion of truth, it is full and dripping ghee? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you marvel if Allah fed you as you fed His Prophet? Eat and feed. She said: I came home, measured into a bowl of ours such-and-such, and left it from which we ate as condiment for a month or two."
"4554","Chapter on the tree's testimony to his prophethood (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and its obedience. Abu Ya'la said: Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, Abu Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I sat with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); a Bedouin came to him and he said: Will you have good? That you testify there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah? He said: Who testifies for you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This tree. He called it while it was on the valley bank; it came hastening until it stood before him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he called it to witness and it bore witness three times, then returned to its place. The Bedouin said: I shall go to my people; if they follow me I shall bring them to you, otherwise I shall return to you and be with you."
"4555","Al-Bazzar said: 'Ali ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us. Al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar al-Ju'fi narrated to us. Both said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us with it. Ibn Hibban authenticated it."
"4556","Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Abi Rafi', from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at al-Hajun, gloomy and grieved, and said: O Allah, show me today a sign after which I shall not care who of my people belies me. It was said to him: Call a tree. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called a tree from the side of the Madinah people's pass; it came splitting the earth until it reached him and greeted him; then he commanded it and it left. He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall not care who of my people belies me after it."
"4557","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Dawud ibn Shabib narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us with it."
"4558","He said: Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, 'Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, and he mentioned it with wording: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at al-Hajun, and the polytheists opposed him; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, show me a sign. He said: Call a tree. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called a tree, and said in it: Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded it and it returned to its planting place. Al-Bazzar said: We do not know it narrated from 'Umar except with this isnad. And the hadith of Usamah in the preceding chapter."
"4559","Chapter on Allah informing him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what his enemies and others say in his absence. al-Harith said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi al-Rijal, 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed us, he said: Abu Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) sat in a corner of the mosque; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out of one of his houses wrapped in a garment. Abu Sufyan, in his place, said: I wonder by what you overcame me? He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came until he struck his back with his hand, then said: I overcame you by Allah Mighty and Majestic. He said: I testify you are the Messenger of Allah."
"4560","Chapter on his informing (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the caliphs after him. al-Harith and Abu Ya'la together said: Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Hashraj ibn Nubatah narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Jumhan narrated to me, from Safinah, freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) built the mosque he placed a stone, then said: Let Abu Bakr place his stone, then let 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) place his stone beside Abu Bakr's stone. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let 'Uthman place his stone beside 'Umar's stone. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These are the caliphs after me."
"4561","Abu Ya'la said: 'Abdullah ibn Muti' narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from al-'Awwam, from his grandmother, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) founded the Madinah mosque, he brought a stone and placed it; Abu Bakr brought a stone and placed it; 'Umar brought a stone and placed it; 'Uthman brought a stone and placed it. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about that and said: This is the affair of the caliphate after me."
"4562","Abu Bahz al-Saqr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bint Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from al-Mukhtar ibn Fulful, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and entered a garden; someone came and knocked the door. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Anas, rise, open for him, give him glad tidings of Paradise and of the caliphate after me. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I inform him? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Inform him. It was Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). I said: Rejoice in Paradise and in the caliphate after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then someone came and knocked; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Anas, and he mentioned the like; it was 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him). I said to him: Rejoice in Paradise and in the caliphate after Abu Bakr. He said: Then someone came and knocked; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rise, O Anas, open for him, give him glad tidings of Paradise and of the caliphate after 'Umar, and that he will be killed. He said: I went out and it was 'Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him). I said to him: Rejoice in Paradise and in the caliphate after 'Umar, and that you will be killed. He entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to him: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah I never sang, nor wished, nor touched my private part since I pledged you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is as you say, O 'Uthman. This hadith is fabricated. Ibn Abi Khaythamah transmitted it in his Tarikh by way of 'Abd al-A'la ibn Abi al-Musawir. Al-Bazzar transmitted it by way of Bakr ibn al-Mukhtar. Bakr and 'Abd al-A'la are feeble, and al-Saqr is feebler than both; perhaps he took it from Bakr or 'Abd al-A'la and flipped it onto 'Abdullah ibn Idris to promote it. Had this occurred, Abu Bakr would not have said to the Ansar: I approve for you one of two men: 'Umar or Abu 'Ubaydah; nor would 'Umar have said: The affair is consultation among six."
"4563","Chapter on his knowledge (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the speech of beasts. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shimr ibn 'Atiyyah, from a man of Juhaynah or Muzaynah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Fajr and saw nearly a hundred wolves squatting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: These are the delegations of wolves; they ask you to spare them of your surplus food, and you are safe from what is besides that. They complained to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of need. He said: Then grant them. He said: They granted them, and they howled. A hadith about the wolf was already cited in Sacrifices."
"4564","Chapter on the purity of his blood and urine (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ya'la said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Hunayd ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Amir ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrating that his father (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was cupped; when he finished he said: O 'Abdullah, take this blood and bury it where no one sees you. When he went out of the Messenger of Allah's sight he drank the blood. When he returned, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O 'Abdullah, what did you do? He said: I placed it in the most hidden place I knew hidden from people. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perhaps you drank it? He said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Why did you drink the blood? Woe to the people from you, and woe to you from the people. Abu Salamah said: I narrated it to Abu 'Asim, who said: They used to see the strength in him (may Allah be pleased with him) was from that blood."
"4565","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us with it."
"4566","Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ar'arah narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us. Al-Bazzar said: Ishaq ibn Hatim narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Isma'il, that is Ibn Abi Fudayk, narrated to us, Burayh ibn 'Umar ibn Safinah narrated to me, my father narrated to me, from my grandfather Safinah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped, then said to me: Take this blood and bury it away from beasts and people. He said: I went and sought a place for it, then came. He said to me: What did you do? I said: I drank it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled."
"4567","Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Salm ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Harb, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from al-Walid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Ayman (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had an earthen pot in which he urinated; when morning came he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: O Umm Ayman, pour what is in the pot. I rose one night thirsty and drank what was in it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umm Ayman, pour what is in the pot. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I rose thirsty and drank what was in it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will never complain of your belly after this day ever."
"4568","Chapter on his blessing alive and dead. al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Habibah narrated to us, Jisr ibn Farqad narrated to us, from Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My life is good for you; you narrate and it is narrated for you; my death is good for you; your deeds are shown to me; whatever is good I praise Allah for, and whatever else I seek Allah's forgiveness for you. The hadith of Khalid ibn al-Walid about the hair of the Prophet's forelock, in the merits of Khalid ibn al-Walid."
"4569","Chapter on his humility (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and fairness. Musaddad said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Mujahid, who said: If a man of al-'Awali invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at midnight over barley bread, he would accept."
"4570","Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered 'A'ishah to prepare something for Usamah, either mucus or other, and it was as if she disliked it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it from her, undertaking it himself. It has a witness from the hadith of 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), transmitted by way of al-Bahiyy from her, authenticated by Ibn Hibban."
"4571","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Umm 'Abdullah bint Na'il, freed slave of 'A'ishah bint Sa'd, narrated to me, from 'A'ishah, from her father (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I walked with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he found a small cluster with dates in it, took a date and gave me a date."
"4572","Abu Bakr said: 'Abbad ibn al-'Awwam narrated to us, from al-Nu'man ibn Thabit, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never put his knees forward before a seated companion ever, nor ever gave his hand to anyone then withdraw it until he was the one to leave it, and none ever sat with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then stood until he stood."
"4573","al-Harith said: 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, 'Adi ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Yunus ibn 'Ubayd, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was of the gentlest of people with people; by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not refuse on a cold morning a slave, bondwoman or child bringing him water to wash his face and arms; no asker ever asked his ear but he listened to him, nor turned away from him until he was the one to turn away; no human ever took his hand (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but he gave it to him, not withdrawing it from him until he was the one to withdraw it from him."
"4574","Abu Ya'la said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Adhrami narrated to us, Abu Qatan narrated to us, Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us with it. Ibn Hibban authenticated it."
"4575","Abu Ya'la said: Jabbarah narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Suwayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, from Ibn 'Umar, from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: A man called the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) three times; each time he answered him: At your service, at your service."
"4576","Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Umarah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'mar, from a man of Banu Fahm who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a gift; he looked and found nothing to put it in, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Put it on the ground, for I am but a servant; I eat as the servant eats and drink as the servant drinks. The hadith."
"4577","al-Tayalisi said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was merciful to dependents."
"4578","Chapter on the fragrance of his sweat (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ya'la said: Bishr ibn Sayhan narrated to us, Halbas ibn Ghalib narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abi al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I married off my daughter and wish you to help me with something. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have nothing, but tomorrow morning come to me with a wide-mouthed bottle and a tree stick, and between me and you is that you knock the side of the door. He said: He brought him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wide-mouthed bottle and a tree stick; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped the sweat from his forearms until the bottle filled. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take it, and order your daughter to dip this stick in the bottle and perfume herself with it. He said: When she perfumed herself with it, the people of Madinah smelled that perfume, and they were called the houses of the perfumed ones."
"4579","Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, 'Umar ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed on a road of Madinah, the scent of musk was found from him, and it was said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed. Al-Bazzar transmitted it from this way."
"4580","Chapter on his forbearance (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Mawsili narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from al-Saq'ab ibn Zuhayr, from Zayd ibn Aslam, referring it to Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: A Bedouin man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing a cloak of sijan buttoned with gold; he stood over the Prophet's head and said: This companion of yours raises every shepherd son of shepherd and lowers every knight son of knight. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seized the folds of his cloak and said: Sit, for I see upon you the garments of one with no sense. Allah Exalted sent no prophet before me but he herded. He said: It was said: And you, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, for qirat and halves of qirat. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Prophet Nuh (peace be upon him) said when death came to him to his son: I bequeath you a bequest. The hadith. It was already cited in the chapter on condemning arrogance in the Book of Manners; al-Hakim transmitted it by way of Wahb ibn Jarir, from his father, from al-Saq'ab ibn Zuhayr, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Ibn 'Umar, adding 'Ata' in it."
"4581","Abu Ya'la said: Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never said to me for something he disliked: How ugly what you did, nor for something pleasing him: How fine what you did. Strange with this wording."
"4582","Chapter on his informing (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that Persia will vanish and Rome remain, and so it was. al-Harith said: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi 'Amr, from Ibn Muhayriz, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Persia is one or two butt assaults, then no Persia after this ever; Rome is horned, whenever a horn perishes another horn succeeds it, people of patience and people of sea; far be the end of time, they are your companions as long as there is good in life."
"4583","Chapter on the blessing of his hand (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wiping the faces of men and women, and his refraining from touching a strange woman. Abu Bakr said: 'Affan narrated to us, 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, Hanzalah narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, wipe my face and supplicate Allah Mighty and Majestic for me. He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped her face and supplicated Allah Exalted for her. She said: O Messenger of Allah, lower your hand. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lowered his hand onto her chest. She said: O Messenger of Allah, lower your hand. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused and kept her away."
"4584","Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hanzalah al-Sadusi, who said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for him to wipe her face; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped it and supplicated for her. She said: O Messenger of Allah, lower your hand after he had placed it on her chest. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Away from me. As in it; Anas is not in it."
"4585","Abu Ya'la narrated it: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, Hanzalah narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he wiped her face, and women would come to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he would wipe their faces and supplicate for them. She said: O Messenger of Allah, lower your hand. He said: He pushed her away and said: Away from me."
"4586","Chapter on his strength (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in intercourse. al-Harith said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given the strength of forty men in intercourse."
"4587","And from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Aban, from Isra'il, from Yunus, from Mujahid, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given the strength of some forty men, each man of the people of Paradise."
"4588","Chapter. Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Hafs ibn 'Umar ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: We brought dates to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he knelt on his knees, took a handful and said: Take this to So-and-so; and took a handful and said: Take this to So-and-so, until he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed handful by handful among his wives. Then he took a handful eating of it and throwing the pits with his left; a domestic sheep passed by him, so he offered it to it and it ate it."
"4589","Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ar'arah narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating from Abi 'Ubaydah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ammar, from al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: My paternal aunt sent me to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I came to him while he had jewelry gifted to him by the chief of Bahrayn; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took of that jewelry two handfuls, then said: O little daughter, this is yours."
"4590","Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq with this isnad: Cucumber pleased him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he loved cucumber. Ahmad transmitted it by way of 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil, from al-Rubayyi' (may Allah be pleased with her) abridged."
"4591","Chapter on his description (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). al-Tayalisi said: Shayban narrated to us, from Jabir, from Abi Salih, from Umm Hani' (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I never saw the belly of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but I remembered folded scrolls, some upon others."
"4592","Chapter on the vastness of the Prophet's knowledge (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us, Fitr, that is Ibn Khalifah, narrated to us, from Abi Ya'la that is Mundhir al-Thawri, from Abi al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left us, and no bird flapped its wings in the sky but he reminded us knowledge of it. Its narrators are trustworthy, except it is broken, and there is disagreement over Fitr."
"4593","Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Yahya narrated to us, from Fitr ibn Khalifah, from 'Ata' who said: Abu al-Darda' said with this."
"4594","Ishaq al-Harawi narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abi Burdah ibn Abi Musa al-Ash'ari, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given the keys of speech and its seals. We said: O Messenger of Allah, teach us of what Allah Mighty and Majestic taught you; so he taught us."
"4595","'Abd al-Ghaffar ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Khalifah ibn Qays, from Khalid ibn 'Urfutah, who said: I sat with 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), then he mentioned a long story in it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O people, I have been given the comprehensive words and their seals, and speech was abridged for me."
"4596","Chapter on what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was singled with over the prophets (peace be upon them). Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn 'Abbad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yahya narrated to me, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: Allah Exalted granted security to none of His creation except Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: (That Allah may forgive you your former and latter sin), and He said to the angels (peace be upon them): (Whoever of them says: I am a god besides Him, him We shall requite with Hell). In its isnad is criticism."
"4597","Musaddad said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, from Sharik ibn Tariq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is none of you but with him is a devil. They said: And with you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And with me, except Allah Exalted aided me against him and he submitted; and none of you will his deed admit to Paradise. They said: Not even you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Not even I, unless Allah Mighty and Majestic envelops me with His mercy."
"4598","Abu Ya'la said: Sufyan, that is Ibn Waki', narrated to us, my father narrated to us, my grandfather that is al-Jarrah ibn Malih narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn 'Ilaqah, and he mentioned the like up to his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: and he submitted. Thus al-Bazzar transmitted it from Bishr ibn Mu'adh, from Abi 'Awanah. He said: Sharik narrated only this hadith and another, meaning what Musaddad mentioned. This is a sahih hadith."
"4599","al-Harith said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Ya'qub narrated to us, from Bishr ibn Shighaf, from 'Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard him saying: The noblest of Allah Exalted's creation to Him is Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Paradise is in the heaven and the Fire in the earth. When the Day of Resurrection comes, Allah Mighty and Majestic will raise creatures nation by nation, prophet by prophet, until Ahmad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his ummah are the last of the nations in station; then the bridge of Jahannam is laid, then a caller calls: Where is Ahmad and his ummah? He rises, and his ummah follows him, righteous and wicked. This is mawquf."
"4600","'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, a shaykh of Banu Tamim narrated to us, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am the forerunner of the Arabs. Its isnad is weak."
"4601","'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, 'Amir ibn Yasaf narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn 'Uqbah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, are you the chief of the Arabs? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am the chief of Adam's children, without boast; Adam is under my banner, without boast. Its isnad is also weak."
"4602","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from his father, from Abi Ya'la that is Mundhir al-Thawri, from al-Rabi' ibn Khuthaym, who said: None excels Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor excels Ibrahim (peace be upon him), the intimate friend of my Lord Mighty and Majestic, anyone."
"4603","al-Harith said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, the like."
"4604","Chapter on the testimony of the People of the Book to his truthfulness (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr said: 'Affan narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, 'Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, from al-Falatan ibn 'Asim al-Jarmi, who said: We sat with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed his gaze at a man walking in the mosque, and he said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah, arguing with him only saying: O Messenger of Allah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you testify I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: No. He said: Do you read the Torah? He said: Yes. He said: And the Gospel? He said: Yes, and the Qur'an; by Him in whose Hand is my soul, had I wished I would recite it. He said: Then he adjured him: Do you find me a prophet in the Torah and Gospel? He said: I shall tell you; we find your like, your form and your emergence, and we hoped it would be among us. When you emerged we feared you might be he; we looked, and you were not he. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Him in whose Hand is Muhammad's soul, I am indeed he; they are my ummah, and they are more than seventy thousand and seventy thousand."
"4605","Ibn Abi 'Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Ya'qub, from Bishr ibn Shighaf, from 'Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When Nahawand was conquered, the Muslims took Jewish captives; the Exilarch came to receive the Jewish captives. A Muslim man took a bright beautiful slave girl and said to me: Will you walk with me to this man, perhaps he will price this girl for me? I went with him; we entered upon an arrogant shaykh with an interpreter; he said to a man with him: Ask this girl: Did this Arab fall upon her? He said: I saw he was jealous when he saw her beauty; he spoke to her in his tongue, and I understood what he said. I said to him: You sinned by what you find in your book, asking this girl about what is beneath her clothes. He said to me: You lie; what do you know of my book? He said: I said: I know your book better than you. He said: Are you more knowledgeable of my book than I? I said: Yes, I know your book better than you. He said: Who is this? They said: 'Abdullah ibn Salam. He said: I left him that day; he sent me a messenger that I must come, and sent me a mount. He said: I went to him in hope he would embrace Islam; he kept me three days, I reciting the Torah to him while he wept. I said to him: By Allah, he is indeed the Prophet you find in your book. He said to me: What shall I do with the Jews? He said: I said: The Jews will avail you nothing against Allah. He refused to embrace Islam, and wretchedness overcame him. Sahih, mawquf."
"4606","Chapter on the ancients' acknowledgment of the signs of his prophethood (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). al-Harith said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from 'Ikrimah ibn Khalid, who said: Some Quraysh sailed the sea at the Prophet's mission (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the wind cast them on an island; there was a man in it who said: Who are you? They said: We are people of Quraysh. He said: What is Quraysh? They said: The people of the sanctuary, and people of such. When he knew he said: We are its people, not you. He was a man of Jurhum. He said: Do you know why Ajyad was named? Our steeds were excellent and bent toward it. He said: They said: A man emerged among us claiming he is a prophet, and mentioned his affair to him. He said: Follow him; were it not for my condition, I would join you to him."
"4607","Chapter on the benefit of his intercession. Ishaq said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, who said: 'Umar saw a woman in her finery and said to her: Do you think your kinship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) avails you anything against Allah? She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: My intercession benefits. Ma'mar said: Khallad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me, from his father, that that woman was Umm Hani' bint Abi Talib, and that he said to her: My intercession benefits Wajj and Hakam. 'Abd al-Rahman said: They are two tribes; Wajj a tribe of Jawlan, and Hakam ibn Sa'd of Madhhij."
"4608","Chapter on the merit of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him). Abu Bakr said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Unays ibn Abi Yahya, from his father, from Abi Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were in the mosque, his head bound with a cloth in his illness in which he died; he inclined toward the minbar and we followed him. He said: By Him in whose Hand is my soul, I stand upon the Hawd this very hour. The hadith; in it the merit of Abu Bakr, and at its end: Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended and did not rise until this hour."
"4609","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Maymun informed us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to reconcile between them; prayer time came, and Bilal said to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both): Prayer time has come and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not present; will you have me call the adhan and iqamah and you lead the people? He said: If you wish. Bilal called the adhan and iqamah, Abu Bakr advanced and led the people. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came after they finished and said: Did you pray? They said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who led you? They said: Abu Bakr. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You did well; it is not proper for a people among whom is Abu Bakr that any other than him lead them."
"4610","al-Harith said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abu al-Harith al-Warraq narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Khunays, from Muhammad ibn Sa'id, from 'Ubadah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Exalted dislikes in heaven that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq be faulted on earth."
"4611","Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Husayn ibn 'Umar narrated to us, Mukhariq narrated to us, from Tariq, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: When (Indeed those who lower their voices before the Messenger of Allah) was sent down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Abu Bakr said: I vowed never to speak to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except as a confidant whispering."
"4612","Khalaf ibn Tamim narrated to us, Nafi' Abu Hurmuz narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: A man came from battle, related to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) through women, while he was in 'A'ishah's house (may Allah be pleased with her); he entered and greeted. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Welcome to a man safe and enriched; bring your need. He said: Which people are dearest to you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This one behind me, and she is 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her). He said: I did not mean women of you; I mean men of you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Her father. Nafi' is abandoned."
"4613","Abu Ya'la said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No one's wealth benefited us as Abu Bakr's wealth benefited us."
"4614","'Amr al-Naqid narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, the like. In the chapter from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4615","Abu Ya'la said: 'Amr ibn Muhammad Abu 'Uthman, that is al-Naqid, narrated to us, 'Amr ibn 'Uthman al-Kilabi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abi 'Abd al-Rahim, from Abi 'Abd al-Malik that is 'Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abi Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Between Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) was a reproach; Abu Bakr apologized to 'Umar and he did not accept from him. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and weighed heavily on him. Then 'Umar went to him in the evening and sat; he turned away from him. Then he moved and sat on the other side; he turned away from him. Then he stood and sat before him; he turned away from him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I see your turning from me, and I see it only for something that reached you of me; what is the news of my kneeling while you turn from me? By Allah, I care not to live in this world an hour while you turn from me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are the one to whom Abu Bakr apologized and you did not accept from him. I came to you all and you said: You lie; my companion said: You speak truth. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you leave me and my companion? three times. Its isnad is weak, but it has a witness in al-Bukhari from the hadith of Abi al-Darda' (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4616","al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah informed us, 'Abdullah ibn Ibrahim al-Ghifari narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was taken up to the lowest heaven; I passed no heaven but found therein: Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and Abu Bakr al-Siddiq behind me."
"4617","Musaddad said: Yusuf ibn al-Majishun narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman entered the Fire for a cat. The hadith; in it: Abu Bakr and 'Umar bear witness to that, though neither Abu Bakr nor 'Umar is there. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: While a man was among his sheep a wolf came and took a sheep. The hadith; in it: Abu Bakr and 'Umar bear witness to that, the like. And while a man rode a cow. The hadith; in it: Abu Bakr and 'Umar bear witness to that, though neither is there. He said: While a man walked in a suit admiring himself, Allah Exalted sank him. The hadith; in it: Abu Bakr and 'Umar bear witness, though neither is there. The origin of this hadith is in the Sahih and others separately; they mentioned the testimony only in the wolf story and the cow story."
"4618","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Qays, who said: I saw Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) a light-fleshed white man."
"4619","al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abi al-Nadr, who said: I heard Abu Salamah mentioning from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn 'Abd Yaghuth, who said: 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) mentioned that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) used to dye with henna."
"4620","Ishaq said: Shababab ibn Sawwar informed us, from Ishaq ibn Yahya, from Musa ibn Talhah, who said: While 'A'ishah bint Talhah (may Allah be pleased with her) said to her mother Umm Kulthum bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both): My father is better than your father, 'A'ishah Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her) said: Shall I judge between you? Abu Bakr entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: O Abu Bakr, you are Allah's freed one from the Fire. She said: From that day he was called 'Atiq. Talhah entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: You, O Talhah, are of those who fulfilled their vow. Ishaq is weak; if Musa heard it from 'A'ishah bint Talhah or from Umm Kulthum, otherwise it is also broken. The mention of Talhah in it they transmitted by other than this route; Mu'awiyah ibn Ishaq narrated it from 'A'ishah bint Talhah with other than this context."
"4621","Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Ishaq, from 'A'ishah bint Talhah (may Allah be pleased with them both), from 'A'ishah Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: By Allah, I was in my house one day, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the courtyard with his Companions, a curtain between me and them, when Abu Bakr came. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased to look at Allah's freed one from the Fire, let him look at Abu Bakr. His name which his family called him was 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman, and the name 'Atiq prevailed over him. Al-Tirmidhi narrated it from another way from 'A'ishah abridged with wording: Abu Bakr came, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are Allah's freed one from the Fire; from that day he was called 'Atiq."
"4622","Ishaq said: I heard Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid saying: If I do not prefer Abu Bakr and 'Umar over 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with them), I will have belied 'Ali; and I am in greater need to believe 'Ali than to belie him."
"4623","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from Abi 'Awn, from a man of Banu Asad who said: I saw Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) in the battle of Dhat al-Salasil, his beard like the flame of 'arfaj."
"4624","Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Nafi' ibn 'Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: I said to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): O caliph of Allah. He said: I am the caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I am content with that."
"4625","Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: I saw 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) with a palm frond in his hand, saying: Listen to the caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Sahih, mawquf."
"4626","Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna said in the additions of Musaddad: Abu Mukays al-Khadim narrated to us, Damrah ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Shawdhab, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Huzayl ibn Shurahbil, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Were Abu Bakr's faith weighed against the faith of earth's people, it would outweigh them."
"4627","Abu Mukays narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Malik ibn Mighwal al-Bajali who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I wish I were a hair on Abu Bakr's chest. I say: The first has a marfu' witness from the hadith of Ibn 'Umar with Ibn 'Adi, in the biography of 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad."
"4628","Musaddad said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from the family of Abi Hayyaj, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This minbar of mine is upon a rivulet of Paradise's rivulets; a man whose Lord gave him choice between living in this world as long as Allah wills and meeting his Lord; then Abu Bakr wept. The hadith, like the hadith of Abi Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him); in it: But love and the brotherhood of faith."
"4629","Umayyah ibn Khalid ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Sumayyah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) who said: Abu Bakr said to me: We have of wealth only a cup and a milch camel; when I die send them to 'Umar. When he died she sent them to 'Umar, who said: May Allah have mercy on Abu Bakr; he tired those after him. And the hadith of Asma' bint Abi Bakr about what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions met of the polytheists at Makkah, coming, Allah willing, in the Prophetic biography."
"4630","Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Abi 'Ubaydah ibn Ma'n narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abi Sufyan, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: They struck the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) once until he fainted. He said: Abu Bakr stood calling: Woe to you, would you kill a man for saying: My Lord is Allah? They said: Who is this? He said: Ibn Abi Quhafah."
"4631","Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Ibn Abi 'Ubaydah narrated to us with it, and at its end: the madman. Sahih; al-Hakim transmitted it by way of Ibn Numayr with it, al-Diya' selected it, and it has a witness from the hadith of 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As in al-Bukhari."
"4632","Ibn Abi 'Umar said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi 'Awn narrated to us, from Musa ibn Mannah, who said: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad was a truthful silent man; when 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz was made caliph (may Allah be pleased with him) he said: Today the virgin speaks from her chamber. I heard my paternal aunt 'A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, the Arabs apostatized altogether, the people craned, and the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became as goats scattered in a thicket. By Allah, they never differed over a word but my father flew with its decisive disposal. Then she mentioned 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: Whoever saw 'Umar knew he was created a toil for Islam. Then she said: By Allah, he was shrewd, unique, prepared for affairs their peers; I never saw the like of his character until she counted seven traits I do not remember."
"4633","al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi 'Awn, from al-Qasim, who said: 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died; by Allah, had what befell my father befallen the firm mountains. Then he mentioned it. He also narrated it from Ahmad ibn Yunus and Ishaq ibn Bishr, both from 'Abd al-'Aziz. The narration of Ibn Abi 'Umar shows 'Abd al-'Aziz's shortcoming."
"4634","Chapter on the merits of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, that he mentioned Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and praised him, saying: He bore me twice."
"4635","Yahya narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: A camel of the public funds broke and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) slaughtered it and invited some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: If you did this every day, would you narrate to us at your place? He said: I shall not return to its like; two companions of mine passed following a path; if I act other than their deed, I shall be led other than their path."
"4636","Hammad narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, who said: 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We, the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), did not doubt that tranquility spoke upon 'Umar's tongue."
"4637","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: 'Abbad ibn 'Abbad narrated to us, from Mujalid with it similarly."
"4638","al-Harith said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, Ja'far that is Ibn Burqan narrated to us, from Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj, from a man, who said: Abu Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) came and sat with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Have you not seen your in-law? 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) proposed to her and she refused him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What kept her from 'Umar? There is no man in Madinah, unless he be a prophet, better than 'Umar. He said: I said to the one who narrated to me: Was Abu Bakr in Madinah that day? He said: I do not know."
"4639","Musaddad said: 'Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, who said: The people of Najran came to 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: We ask your handwriting and your intercession with your tongue: to turn us back. He said: 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was sound in command; had he been maligned one day, he would have been maligned that day."
"4640","Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah narrated to us, al-Walid ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Isma'il al-'Ijli, from Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i, from 'Alqamah, from 'Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O 'Ammar, Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me just now; I said: O Jibril, tell me of the merits of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: O Muhammad, had I told you of 'Umar's merits for as long as Nuh remained among his people, a thousand years less fifty, 'Umar's merits would not be exhausted; and 'Umar is a good deed of Abu Bakr's."
"4641","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: If there was anyone who did not know lying, it is Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4642","Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If a camel were to die from neglect in my administration, I would fear that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, would question me about it."
"4643","Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, from Asma bint Umays, who said: A man from the Emigrants entered upon Abu Bakr while he was ill in his sickness, and said to him: You have appointed Umar over us, and he was harsh with us even when he had no authority, so how will he be if he rules us, even harsher and harsher, so what will you say to Allah when you meet Him? Abu Bakr said: Seat me up, so they seated him up, and he said: Do you threaten me with Allah? He said: I will say when I meet Him: I appointed over them the best of Your people. Its men are trustworthy."
"4644","Ishaq said: Abd al-Ala Abu Hammam informed us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, who said: I do not know anyone among the people who was more knowledgeable after the Messenger of Allah than Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4645","Ibn Abi Umar and al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, my father informed me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I wish I could come out of it - meaning the leadership - even, with nothing for me and nothing against me. The hadith, and it will come, Allah willing, in full in the Book of the Caliphate."
"4646","Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, from al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I climbed up to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Come down from my father's pulpit and go to your father's pulpit. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: My father never had a pulpit. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Then he took me before him, and I kept turning pebbles in my hand, and when he came down he took me to his house and said: Who ordered you to do this? I said: No one ordered me to do this. He said: You keep coming to us, you keep visiting us. He said: I came to him one day when he was alone with Muawiya, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, came and turned back, and when I saw him turn back I turned back, then he met me afterwards and said: I did not see you visiting us? I said: I did come, and you were alone with Muawiya, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, came and turned back, and when I saw him turn back I turned back. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You are more entitled to permission than Abdullah ibn Umar; you are held over our heads; we see none but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and you. He said: And he, may Allah be pleased with him, placed his hand upon his head."
"4647","Isa ibn Yunus informed us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to say: O Allah, do not make my killing at the hand of a man who has prayed a single prostration to You. This is a sahih isnad."
"4648","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Abu al-Ashhab, from a man from Muzayna who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw a washed garment on Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Is this garment of yours new or washed? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Washed, O Messenger of Allah. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Wear new, live praiseworthy, die a martyr, and may Allah give you the delight of the eye in this world and the Hereafter. This is mursal, or interrupted, and it has been narrated connected from the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Ahmad and others brought it without its ending."
"4649","Ibn Abi Umar said: Ayyub ibn Wasil narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Umayr ibn Ishaq, from a man, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sent for me, so I came to him, and when I reached the door I heard his weeping. I said: The Commander of the Believers has been afflicted. I entered and took hold of his shoulder and said: No harm, no harm, O Commander of the Believers. He said: Rather the severest harm. He took my hand and led me inside the door, and there were bags, some upon others. He said: Now the family of al-Khattab has become insignificant before Allah, Exalted. If Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had willed, He would have granted this to my two companions - meaning the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both - and they would have laid down a practice for me in it to follow. I said: Sit with us and let us think, sit with us and let us think. So we assigned for the Mothers of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with them, four thousand each, and we assigned for the Emigrants four thousand each, and for the rest of the people two thousand each."
"4650","Ahmad ibn Mani and al-Harith both said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Habib ibn Ubayd al-Rahabi, from al-Miqdam ibn Madi Karib, who said: When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was struck, Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, entered upon him and said: O companion of the Messenger of Allah, and O in-law of the Messenger of Allah, and O Commander of the Believers. Umar said to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Seat me up, O Abdullah, seat me up, for I have no patience for what I hear. So he leaned him against his chest, may Allah be pleased with him, and said to her: I adjure you by what right I have over you not to eulogize me after this sitting of yours; as for your eyes I cannot control them. No dead person is eulogized with what is not in him except that the angels curse him."
"4651","Musaddad said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, who said: When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, departed from Mina he made his camel kneel at al-Abtah, then heaped up a heap of pebbles, then threw himself upon it, so it stuck to his garment, and he lay on his back and stretched his hand to the sky and said: O Allah, my strength has weakened, my age has grown old, and my subjects have spread, so take me to You without neglecting or falling short. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, came to Madina and addressed the people and said: O people, I have laid down practices for you, and prescribed obligations for you, and left you upon clarity - and Yahya clapped his hands - unless you go astray with people right and left. And he mentioned the hadith. Said said: Dhul-Hijja had not ended before Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed."
"4652","Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, who said: I witnessed Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, on the morning he was stabbed. I was in the second row, and nothing kept me from being in the first row except his awe. He, may Allah be pleased with him, used to face the row when prayer was established, and if he saw a man forward or backward he would strike him with the stick. That is what kept me from being in the first row, so I was in the second row. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came intending prayer, and Abu Lulua, the slave of al-Mughira ibn Shuba, intercepted him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, spoke privately with him not far off, then left him, then spoke privately with him, then left him, then spoke privately with him, then left him, then he stabbed him. I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, gesturing with his hand like this, saying: Seize the dog, for he has killed me. The people surged, and someone said: Prayer, O servants of Allah, the sun has risen. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, led them in prayer with the two shortest chapters of the Quran: When the help of Allah comes, and Indeed We have given you al-Kawthar. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was carried, and said: O Abdullah, hand me the shoulder blade. If Allah, Exalted, had willed to carry out what is in it, He would have carried it out. Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I will suffice you, I will erase it. He said: No, by Allah, none shall erase it but me. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, erased it with his hand, and in it was the ruling on the grandfather. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Call Ali and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Talha, al-Zubayr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Sad, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: They were called, and he, may Allah be pleased with him, spoke to none of the group except Ali and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both. He said: O Ali, these people may recognize your kinship to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and what Allah, Exalted, has given you of understanding and knowledge, so if they entrust you with this affair, fear Allah therein. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Uthman, these people may recognize your marriage tie to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and your nobility, so if they entrust you with this affair, fear Allah, and do not place the Banu Abi Muayt upon the necks of the people. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Suhayb, lead the people in prayer for three days, and bring these into a house; when they agree upon a man, whoever opposes them, strike his head. When they went out he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If they appoint al-Ajlah he will lead them upon the path. Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to him: What prevents you? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I dislike bearing it alive and dead. This is a sahih hadith; al-Bukhari brought it more completely than this wording, and I have aimed at what is additional to it."
"4653","Chapter on the account of the killing of Umar. Ishaq said: Qays ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Tariq ibn Shihab: When Umar was killed, Umm Ayman said: Today Islam has been weakened. He mentioned it from Qabisa ibn Uqba, from Sufyan, from Qays."
"4654","Ibn Abi Umar said: Thumama ibn Ubayda al-Abdi narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was stabbed, we entered upon him while he was saying: Do not hasten against this man. If I live I will form my own judgment concerning him, and if I die then he is up to you. They said: O Commander of the Believers, by Allah he has been killed and cut off. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Woe to you, who is he? They said: Abu Lulua. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah is Greatest. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, looked at his son Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: My son, what kind of father was I to you? He said: The best of fathers. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure you to carry me until you press my cheek to the ground, so that I die as the slave dies. Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, that is hard upon me, O father. He said: Then he said: Stand, and do not argue with me. He said: He stood and carried him until he pressed his cheek to the ground. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Abdullah, I adjure you by the right of Allah, Exalted, and the right of Umar, when I die and you bury me, not to wash your head until you sell some of the houses of the family of Umar for eighty thousand and place it in the treasury of the Muslims. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, who was at his head, said: O Commander of the Believers, what is the worth of these eighty thousand; you have harmed your dependents, or the family of Umar? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Away from me, O Ibn Awf. He looked at Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: My son, and thirty-two thousand I spent on twelve pilgrimages I performed during my rule, and on deputations that used to come to me from the regions with messengers. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: O Commander of the Believers, rejoice and think well of Allah, Exalted, for there is none of us among the Emigrants except that he took like what you took from the spoils which Allah, Exalted, assigned to us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, passed away while pleased with you, and you had with him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, precedents. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Ibn Awf, Umar wishes he could come out of it as he entered it. I wish to meet Allah, Exalted, with neither little nor much sought from me. Thumama was criticized by Ali ibn al-Madini and others, and the wording of the story of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in the two Sahihs does not contain most of what is mentioned here."
"4655","Abu Yala said: Qatan ibn Nusayr al-Ghubari narrated to us, Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abu Rafi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Lulua was a slave of al-Mughira ibn Shuba, and he used to make millstones, and al-Mughira ibn Shuba used to take from him four dirhams every day. Abu Lulua met Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: O Commander of the Believers, al-Mughira has burdened me heavily with my tax, so speak to him to lighten it from me. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Fear Allah, Exalted, and be good to your master. And it was Umars intention, may Allah be pleased with him, to meet al-Mughira and speak to him to lighten it from him. The slave became angry and said: His justice has encompassed all people except me. So he resolved to kill him. He fashioned a dagger with two heads, sharpened it and poisoned it, then brought it to al-Hurmuzan and said: What do you think of this? He said: I think you will strike none with it but you will kill him. Abu Lulua watched for his chance, and came at the dawn prayer until he stood behind Umar, may Allah be pleased with him - and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when prayer was established, would speak, saying: Straighten your rows - so he went to say as he used to say, and when he said takbir, Abu Lulua struck him in his shoulder and struck him in his side. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, fell, and he stabbed thirteen men with his dagger; seven of them died and six were wounded. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was carried and taken to his house. The people surged until the sun was about to rise. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, called: O people, prayer, prayer. They rushed to prayer, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, stepped forward and led them with the two shortest chapters of the Quran. When he finished his prayer, they turned to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He called for a drink to see the extent of his wound. Nabidh was brought and he drank it, and it came out of his wound, and it was not known whether it was nabidh or blood. He, may Allah be pleased with him, called for milk and drank it, and it came out of his wound. They said: No harm upon you, O Commander of the Believers. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If killing is harm, then I have been killed. The people began praising him. He said: What are you saying; I wish I could come out of it even, and that my companionship of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, remained safe for me. Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, spoke and said: No, by Allah, you shall not come out of it even. And he mentioned the hadith. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to find comfort in the speech of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, so he said: Repeat. He repeated it to him. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What are you saying; if I had the earth full I would ransom myself with it from the terror of the ascent. Ibn Hibban brought it from Abu Yala in full, and its basis is in the Sahih with little of this wording, and most of it is not in it."
"4656","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabia, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who took a straw and said: I wish I were this, I wish my mother had never borne me, I wish I were utterly forgotten."
"4657","With the same chain to Asim, from Aban ibn Uthman, from his father, who said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Woe to me, and woe to my mother, if Allah does not forgive me - three times - and between them some speech passed. And the reports of the Islam of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, will come, Allah willing, in the chapter of the biography."
"4658","From the Book of Virtues, Chapter on the virtues of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Two men mentioned Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. One of them said: He was killed a martyr. The other seized him. He was brought to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: This one claims that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed a martyr?! Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Did you say that? He said: Yes. Do you not remember the day I came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and you were with him. I asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he gave me; I asked Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he gave me; I asked Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he gave me; I asked Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and he gave me; and I asked you and you refused me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, pray to Allah to bless for me. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Why should you not be blessed when a Prophet, a truthful one, and two martyrs have given you? Three times. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Leave him."
"4659","Abu Yala said: Hudba narrated to us, Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: A man in Kufa testified that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed a martyr. The guards seized him and raised him to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Were it not that you forbid us, or you forbade us to kill anyone, we would have killed him. He claims he testifies that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, is a martyr. The man said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: And do you testify, or do you remember, that I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he gave me, and I came to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him and he gave me, and the rest of the hadith he mentioned."
"4660","Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Ibn Lahia narrated to us, Yazid ibn Amr al-Maafiri narrated to me, who said: I heard Abu Thawr al-Fihri narrating from Abd al-Rahman ibn Udays al-Balawi, who was among those who pledged under the tree. He ascended the pulpit, praised Allah, Exalted, and extolled Him, then mentioned Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and spoke ill of him. Abu Thawr said: I entered upon Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was besieged, and said: Ibn Udays said such and such. Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And whence, when I have stored with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, ten things: I am the fourth of four in Islam; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, married me his daughter, then his daughter; I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with my hand and never covered my private part with it, nor sang, nor wished, nor drank wine in ignorance nor in Islam, and the rest of the hadith. I said: Some have part of it."
"4661","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us, al-Zubayr narrated to us, from one who narrated to him, who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, used to fast the day and stand the night, except for a short sleep at its beginning."
"4662","Al-Nadr ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn Ukaym, who said: When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the call to prayer, he said."
"4663","Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman, who said: I saw Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, one night at the Maqam, having stepped forward; he recited the Quran in one rakah, then departed."
"4664","Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu Layla, who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, looked out upon us on the day of the house and said: O people, do not kill me, for if you kill me you will be like this. And he interlaced his fingers, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4665","Abu Yala said: Shayban, that is Ibn Farrukh, narrated to us, Talha ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ubayda ibn Hassan, from Ata al-KaykharanI, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in a house among a group of Emigrants including Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, Ali, Talha, al-Zubayr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, and Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with them, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Let each man rise to his equal. And the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, rose to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and embraced him, and said: You are my ally in this world and the Hereafter. Al-Hakim narrated it in al-Mustadrak and declared it sahih, while overlooking the weakness of Talha ibn Zayd, for he is abandoned."
"4666","Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Abu Mashar narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: While the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was sitting with Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, behind him, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission and entered, then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission and entered, then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission and entered, then Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission and entered, then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission and entered, while the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was conversing with his knee uncovered. He extended his knee and said to his wife: Move back from me. They conversed for a while, then left. Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, your companions entered and you did not arrange your garment over your knees, nor move me away from you, until Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, entered?! He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Shall I not be shy of a man whom Allah and His Messenger are shy of? Had he entered while you were near me, he would not have raised his head nor spoken until he left."
"4667","Zuhayr narrated to us, Muawiya ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaida narrated to us, from Asim, from Shaqiq, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, met al-Walid ibn Uqba, may Allah be pleased with him. Al-Walid said to him: Why do I see you have shunned the Commander of the Believers, Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: Convey to him from me that I did not flee on the day of Aynayn - Asim said: that is the day of Uhud - nor did I stay back from Badr, nor did I abandon the practice of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He went to inform Uthman of that. Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for his saying about the day of Aynayn, how does he reproach me for a sin which Allah, Exalted, has already pardoned, for He, Mighty and Majestic, said: Those of you who turned back on the day the two hosts met, to the end of the verse. As for his saying that I stayed back on the day of Badr, I was nursing Ruqayya, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until she died, and he assigned me a share; and whoever the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, assigns a share has indeed witnessed. As for his saying that I abandon the practice of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, neither I nor he can bear it. So I came to him and told him that."
"4668","Al-Bazzar said: Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, Uthman ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Sallam Abu al-Mundhir narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, raised his voice against Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf said to him: Why did you raise your voice when I witnessed Badr and you did not witness it, and I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and you did not pledge, and you fled on the day of Uhud and I did not flee?! Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for your saying that you witnessed Badr and I did not, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, left me behind over his daughter, and assigned me a share and gave me my reward. As for your saying that you pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and I did not pledge, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent me to some of the polytheists, and you know that; when I was detained, he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, struck his right upon his left and said: This is for Uthman. And the left of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is better than my right. As for your saying that I fled on the day of Uhud and you did not flee, Allah, Exalted, said: Those of you who turned back on the day the two hosts met, to the end of the verse. Why do you reproach me for a sin which Allah, Exalted, has pardoned?! Al-Bazzar said: We know none narrated it from Ali ibn Zayd except Sallam."
"4669","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from his father, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, that he witnessed that when Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, gave the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what he equipped the army of hardship with; he, may Allah be pleased with him, brought seven hundred ounces of gold."
"4670","Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Bashshar ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ziyad narrated to us, who said: I heard Qatada saying: The first to emigrate among the Muslims with his family to Abyssinia was Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. News of him was delayed to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kept going out expecting news. A woman from Quraysh came and said to him: O Abu al-Qasim, I saw your son-in-law setting out on his journey, with his wife upon a donkey of these mounts, he driving her, walking behind her. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: May Allah accompany them. Uthman is the first to emigrate to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, with his family after Lut, peace be upon him."
"4671","Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who said: While I was sitting, Jibril, peace be upon him, came to me and carried me upon his right wing and admitted me into the Garden of Eden. While I was in it, I caught sight of an apple with my eyes, and the apple split into two halves and a maiden came out of it. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I never saw anyone more beautiful or more perfect in loveliness than her, glorifying Allah, Exalted, with a glorification the like of which the first and the last never heard. I said: Who are you? She said: I am the houri; my Lord, Majestic is His majesty, created me from the light of His Throne. I said: For whom are you? She said: I am for the trustworthy, the fair-skinned caliph, the wronged, Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4672","Chapter on the virtues of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ammar, who said: An old man from the people of Madina stayed with Masruq, and narrated from Safiyya, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: I stood before the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said to him: There is none of your wives but has kin and clan, so to whom do you entrust me? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I entrust you to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4673","Al-Fadl, that is Abu Nuaym, narrated to us, Fitr ibn Khalifa narrated to us, Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me, who said: I heard Thalaba ibn Yazid saying: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: By Allah, it is indeed the covenant of the unlettered Prophet: They will betray you after me."
"4674","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, the freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Salim, from Abu Idris al-Awdi, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This nation will betray you after me. Al-Bazzar narrated it, and Ibn Majah."
"4675","Habib narrated to us, from Thalaba ibn Yazid al-Himmani, from his father, who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying upon the pulpit: By Allah, it is indeed the covenant of the unlettered Prophet: This nation will betray me."
"4676","Abu Bakr said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Talha ibn Jabr, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah, from Musab ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, conquered Makka he turned to Taif and besieged it nineteen or eighteen nights without conquering it, then he went on at midday or morning, alighted, then set out at midday heat, and said: O people, I am your forerunner, and I enjoin upon you my kin with good, and your appointment is the Pool. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall establish prayer and give zakat, or I shall surely send to you a man from me, or as myself, who shall surely strike the necks of their fighters and take their offspring captive. He said: The people thought he was Abu Bakr or Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Then he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took the hand of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: This."
"4677","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us with this."
"4678","Abu Yala also said: Dawud ibn Amr narrated to us, Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Salama ibn Kuhayl, from his father, from al-Minhal, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, and from Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Are you not pleased to be to me as Harun was to Musa, except that there is no prophet after me. Ibn Hibban declared it sahih."
"4679","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu Qatan narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Alqama, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We used to say that among the most judicious of the people of Madina was Ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4680","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Junayd narrated to us, Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us with it. And al-Hakim declared it sahih."
"4681","Al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Hashim narrated to us, al-Thawri narrated to us, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Sadiq, from Hanash ibn al-Mutamir, from Ulaym al-Kindi, from Salman al-Farisi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The first of you to come to me at the Pool is the first of you in Islam: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4682","Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythama narrated to us, Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, Fudayl narrated to us, from Abu Hariz, from al-Shabi, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me when I returned from a funeral something I would not love to have the whole world for."
"4683","Sahl ibn Zanjala al-Razi narrated to us, al-Sabbah ibn Muharib narrated to us, from Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Yala ibn Murra al-Thaqafi, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made brotherhood among the people and left me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, you made brotherhood among your companions and left me?! He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do you see that I left you? I only left you for myself. You are my brother and I am your brother. He said: If anyone disputes with you, say: I am the slave of Allah and the brother of His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; none shall claim it after you except a liar."
"4684","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid al-Khurasani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Amr narrated to us, Ismail ibn Rafi narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, or Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir - the doubt is from Hammad - who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: O Ali, hold the door and let none enter upon me, for with me is a company of angels who sought their Lords permission to visit me. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, held the door, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came and sought permission. He said: O Ali, seek permission for me from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: There is no seeking permission upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, went back, thinking that was from displeasure of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, could not refrain but returned and said: Seek permission for me from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: There is no seeking permission upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Why? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Because a company of angels is with him, who sought their Lords permission to visit him. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: How many are they, O Ali? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Three hundred and sixty angels. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered the door opened, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, he told me that a company of angels sought their Lords permission to visit you, and he told me, O Messenger of Allah, that their number was three hundred and sixty angels. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Did you tell of the company? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: And did you tell him their number? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Yes. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: How many are they, O Ali? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Three hundred and sixty angels. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: And how did you know that? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard three hundred and sixty voices, so I knew they were three hundred and sixty angels. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, struck his chest and said: O Ali, may Allah increase you in faith and knowledge."
"4685","Abu Bakr said: Abu Muawiya narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Sad ibn Ubayda, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent us in an expedition and put Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in command over us. When we came to him he said: How did you find your companion? He said: Either I complained of him or another besides me complained of him. I raised my head, and I was a man who kept his head bowed, and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had reddened in face, saying: Whomsoever I am his master, Ali is his master."
"4686","Muttalib ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were at al-Juhfa at Ghadir Khumm when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to us and took the hand of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Whomsoever I am his master, Ali is his master."
"4687","Sharik narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi, from his father, who said: Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, entered the mosque and the people gathered to him. A youth stood up to him and said: I adjure you by Allah, did you hear the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Whomsoever I am his master, Ali is his master; O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him and oppose whoever opposes him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Allah, yes."
"4688","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this. Al-Bazzar said: Ali ibn Shubruma al-Bahili narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Sufi narrated to us, a man narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Dawud and Idris, from their father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, with it."
"4689","He said: And I found in my book from Muhammad ibn Miskin, from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, from Ikrima ibn Ibrahim, from Idris al-Awdi with it."
"4690","Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, and from Abu Yazid al-Madani, who both said: When Fatima was given to Ali, and the hadith, and it has already come in the Book of Marriage. In it: He said: O Fatima, I did not spare to marry you to the most beloved of my family to me."
"4691","Abu Yala said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Harami ibn Umara narrated to us, al-Fadl ibn Umayra Abu Qutayba al-Qaysi narrated to us, Maymun al-Kurdi Abu Basir narrated to me, from Abu Uthman, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was holding my hand and we were walking in one of the lanes of Madina, we came upon a garden and I said: O Messenger of Allah, how beautiful a garden! He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For you in Paradise is better than it, until we passed seven gardens, each time I said: How beautiful! and he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For you in Paradise is better than it. When the road was clear for me he embraced me, then wept loudly. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what makes you weep? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Grudges in the chests of people which they will not reveal except after me. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, with my religion safe? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: With your religion safe."
"4692","Al-Bazzar said: Amr ibn Ali and Muhammad ibn Mamar narrated to us; both said: Harami ibn Umara narrated to us with it. It is narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, only with this isnad, and nothing came from Abu Uthman, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, except this."
"4693","Al-Harith said: Hawdha narrated to us, Awf narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Hind al-Jamali, who said: On the night Fatima was given to Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Do nothing until I come to you. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not delay in following them. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stood at the door and sought permission, then entered, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was apart from her. He said: I knew that you are the flame of Allah and His Messenger. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, called for water, rinsed his mouth, then returned it into the vessel, then he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sprinkled her chest and his chest, may Allah be pleased with them both, and named Allah upon them, then he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out from them."
"4694","Abu Yala said: Qatan ibn Nusayr narrated to us, Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Muthanna, from Ubaydullah ibn Anas, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A roasted partridge with a loaf and a side dish was given to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Allah, bring me the most beloved of Your creation to You to eat with me from this food. Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: O Allah, make him my father. Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, said: O Allah, make him my father. Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I said: O Allah, make him Sad ibn Ubada. He said: I heard movement at the door, so I went out and there was Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. I said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is occupied. He turned away. Then I heard movement at the door, so I went out and there was Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, likewise. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard his voice and said: See who this is? I went out and there was Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and told him. He said: O Allah, my ally, O Allah, my ally."
"4695","Al-Bazzar said: Ahmad ibn Uthman narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Ismail ibn Salman al-Kahhal al-Azraq narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Birds were given to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided them among his wives, may Allah be pleased with them, and each wife received, and the hadith."
"4696","Abu Yala said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, Yunus ibn Arqam narrated to us, from Mutayr ibn Abi Khalid, from Thabit al-Bajali, from Safina, may Allah be pleased with him, the keeper of the Prophets provision, who said: A woman from the Ansar gave the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, two birds between two loaves, while he was in the mosque, and there was none in the house but me and Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came and called for lunch. I said: O Messenger of Allah, a woman has given you a gift. I brought the two birds to him, and he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Allah, bring me the most beloved of Your creation - I think he said - to You and to Your Messenger. He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came and knocked the door lightly. I said: Who is this? He said: Abu al-Hasan. Then he knocked and raised his voice. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Who is this? I said: Ali. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Open for him. I opened, and he ate with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, from the two birds until they were finished."
"4697","Suwayd ibn Said narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim ibn Sharus al-Sanani narrated to us, from Ibn Mina, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, embracing Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and kissing him, saying: With my father, the unique martyr, with my father, the unique martyr."
"4698","Ibn Abi Umar said: Marwan al-Fazari narrated to us, from Qanan ibn Abdullah, that he heard Musab ibn Sad narrating from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was sitting in the mosque with two men, and we discussed Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and spoke ill of him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came angry, with anger known in his face. I said: I seek refuge in Allah from the anger of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What is it with you and me; whoever harms Ali has harmed me, saying it three times. He said: I was afterwards approached, and it was said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, alludes to you, saying: Beware the trial of al-Ukhaynis. I would say: Did he name me? They would say: No. I would say: The hidden ones among people are many. Refuge in Allah from harming the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, after I heard from him what I heard."
"4699","Al-Bazzar said: Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, Marwan narrated to us with it, and said: We know it narrated from Sad only with this isnad."
"4700","Abu Yala said: Mahmud ibn Khidash narrated to us, Marwan narrated to us with it."
"4701","Abu Bakr said: Jafar ibn Awn narrated to us, from Shaqiq ibn Abi Abdullah, Abu Bakr ibn Khalid ibn Urfuta narrated to us, who said: I came to Sad ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, in Madina. He said: I was told that you revile Ali, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: We have done so. He said: Perhaps you reviled him? He said: I said: Refuge in Allah. He said: Do not revile him, for were the saw placed upon my crown to revile Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, I would never revile him after I heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, what I heard."
"4702","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from his brother Isa, from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Two men are ruined concerning me: an extravagant lover, and a slandering hater."
"4703","Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, Hilal ibn Khabbab narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfa, from his father, who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying upon the pulpit, pointing with his forefinger and middle finger: Two men are ruined concerning me: an excessive lover, and a spiteful hater."
"4704","From Abbad, from Hilal, from Zadhan, who said: I heard him narrating from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the like, except that he did not mention the two fingers."
"4705","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Marwan ibn Muawiya al-Fazari narrated to us, from Qanan ibn Abdullah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What is it with me and you; whoever harms Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, has harmed me."
"4706","Abu Yala said: Suwayd ibn Said narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abdullah al-Suhbani narrated to us, from Abd al-Mumin, from Abu al-Mughira, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sought me and found me sleeping in a stream bed. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Rise, how mean are people; they call you Abu Turab. He said: He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw me as though I found it in myself, so he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Rise, by Allah I shall surely please you: You are my brother, and the father of my two sons; you fight for my practice, and clear my liability; whoever dies in my time is Allahs captive, Exalted, and whoever dies in your time has fulfilled his vow; whoever dies loving you after your death, Allah, Exalted, shall seal for him with safety and faith as long as the sun rises or sets; and whoever dies hating you dies a death of ignorance, and shall be reckoned for what he did in Islam."
"4707","Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali, from his father, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, attended the tree at Khumm, then came out holding the hand of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Do you not testify that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, is your Lord? They said: Yes. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do you not testify that Allah and His Messenger are more entitled to you than yourselves, and that Allah and His Messenger are your guardians? They said: Yes. He said: Whomsoever Allah and His Messenger are his master, then this is his master. I have left among you what if you hold to it you shall never go astray: the Book of Allah, Exalted, its rope in My hand and its rope in your hands, and my household. This is a sahih isnad, and the hadith of Ghadir Khumm was brought by al-Nasai from the narration of Abu al-Tufayl, from Zayd ibn Arqam, Ali, and a group of Companions, may Allah be pleased with them. In this is an addition not there, and the basis of the hadith was also brought by al-Tirmidhi."
"4708","Ishaq said: Shababa ibn Sawwar al-Madaini informed us, Nuaym ibn Hakim narrated to us, Abu Maryam and some of his companions narrated to us, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took his hand on the day of Ghadir Khumm and said: O Allah, whomsoever I am his master, Ali is his master. He said: The people added afterwards: O Allah, befriend whoever befriends him, and oppose whoever opposes him. And the hadith of Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, has already come in Purification."
"4709","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to us, Abu Said narrated to us, from Sadaqa ibn al-Rabi, from Umara ibn Ghaziyya, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Said, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was at the house of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, among a group of Emigrants and Helpers, may Allah be pleased with them. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came out to us and said: Shall I not tell you of your best? They said: Yes. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Your best are those who fulfill and perfume; Allah, Exalted, loves the unobtrusive, God-fearing. He said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, passed by, and he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Truth is with him, truth is with him. And his hadith, may Allah be pleased with him, in the story of the daughter of Hamza, may Allah be pleased with them both, has already come in Custody."
"4710","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shuba, from Qatada, from Jurayy ibn Kulayb, who said: I saw Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, commanding something, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, forbidding it. I said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Between you two there is indeed evil. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Between us is only good, but the best of us is the most adherent of us to this religion."
"4711","Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abu Amr ibn al-Ala, from his father, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed and said: O people, by Allah besides Whom there is no god, I have taken nothing from your wealth, little or much, except this. And he, may Allah be pleased with him, brought out a bottle from the sleeve of his shirt containing perfume, and said: A dihqan gave it to me."
"4712","Ahmad ibn Mani said: al-Haytham narrated to us, Hafs narrated to us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from the two sons of Jabir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came while we were lying in the mosque, and struck us with a fresh palm stalk in his hand, saying: Do you sleep in the mosque?! None shall sleep in the mosque. He said: We jumped up, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, jumped up with us. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Come, O Ali, what is lawful for me in the mosque is lawful for you. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you shall surely drive from my Pool on the Day of Resurrection, as the stray camel is driven from water with a stick of awsaj you have, and it is as though I look at your station at my Pool."
"4713","Chapter on the virtues of Fatima, blessings of Allah upon her father and upon her, may Allah be pleased with her, and the merit of her two sons, may Allah be pleased with them both. Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Israil, from Maysara ibn Habib al-Nahdi, from al-Minhal Amr, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Hudhayfa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and prayed Maghrib with him, then he stood and prayed until he prayed Isha, then he went out and I followed him. He said: An angel appeared to me and sought his Lords permission to greet me and give me glad tidings that Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, is the mistress of the women of Paradise, and that al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, are the youths of Paradise."
"4714","Abu Bakr said: al-Ahwas al-Absi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Qarm narrated to us, from Harun ibn Sad, from Abu al-Safar, from Khayra, from Asma bint Umays, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, proposed to me, and that reached Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her. She came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Asma, may Allah be pleased with her, is marrying Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: She should not harm Allah and His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4715","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Ibn Abi Zunaym narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Jafar al-Mukharrami narrated to us, from Umm Bakr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhrama, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Ties and lineages and marriages are cut off except my marriage tie. Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, is a branch of me; what constricts her constricts me, and what comforts her comforts me."
"4716","Al-Harith said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, intended to propose to the daughter of Abu Jahl. People said: Do you think the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, will be affected by that? Some said: What is that? She is but a woman among women. Some said: He shall surely be affected by this: marrying the daughter of Allahs enemy over the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. That reached the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He praised Allah, Exalted, and extolled Him, then said: To proceed, what is with people who claim that I am not affected for Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her - Fatima is but a part of me? It is not for anyone to marry the daughter of Allahs enemy over the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. This is mursal, and the basis of the hadith is in the two Sahihs from the hadith of al-Miswar, may Allah be pleased with him, that Ali ibn al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated it; it turned upon Ali ibn Zayd, who is weak in memory."
"4717","Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad, that is Ibn Aisha, narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Khadija, may Allah be pleased with her, is the best of the women of her world, and Maryam, peace be upon her, is the best of the women of her world, and Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, is the best of the women of her world. This is mursal with sahih isnad, and al-Tirmidhi brought it by way of Urwa, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, with the wording: The best of her women is Maryam, the best of her women is Fatima. And this mursal explains that connected one. And there will come, Allah willing, in the Tribulations concerning al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, many things. And the hadith of Abu al-Hamra, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning the household and Fatima, may Allah be pleased with them, has already come in the commentary of al-Ahzab."
"4718","Al-Tayalisi said: Amr ibn Thabit narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Fakhita, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, visited us and stayed the night with us, while al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, were sleeping. Al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, asked for water, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, rose to a waterskin and squeezed it into the cup, then came to give him drink. Al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reached out to drink, but he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prevented him and began with al-Hasan. Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, said: As though he is more beloved to you? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No, but he asked for water first. Then he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I, and you, and these two - and I think he said - and this sleeper - meaning Ali, may Allah be pleased with him - shall be on the Day of Resurrection in one place."
"4719","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn Said narrated to us, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Amr ibn Thabit, from his father, from Abu Fakhita, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it."
"4720","Ishaq said: al-Nadr, that is Ibn Shumayl, informed us, Hammad, that is Ibn Salama, narrated to us, from Amr, that is Ibn Dinar, I heard Yahya ibn Jada saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Fatima: The Quran was presented to me every year, and this year it was presented to me twice, and I shall die. She wept. He said: You are the first of my family to join me. This is mursal, and it has been connected by another way."
"4721","Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from al-Shabi, from one of the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent me to Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her. She came walking with her fathers gait. He told her something and she wept. She, may Allah be pleased with her, was asked and said: I shall not disclose the secret of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, to anyone. This is a sahih isnad, and they brought from the way of al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, the like lengthened, except there is no sending in it; it may be another woman."
"4722","Abu Yala said: Umayya ibn Bistam narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I never saw anyone more truthful than Fatima except her father, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. And there was something between them. She said: O Messenger of Allah, ask her, for she does not lie."
"4723","Abu Yala said: Sahl ibn Zanjala narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Lahia narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, stayed days without tasting food until that distressed him. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went round the houses of his wives and found nothing with any of them. He came to Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: O my daughter, have you anything I may eat, for I am hungry? She said: No, by Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you. When he left her, a neighbor sent her two loaves and a piece of meat. She took it from her and placed it in a bowl of hers and covered it, and said: By Allah, I shall surely prefer the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, with this over myself and those with me, though all were in need of a meal. She sent al-Hasan or al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he returned to her. She said to him: May my father and mother be ransom for you, Allah, Exalted, has brought something which I have kept for you. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Bring it. She brought it to him, and he uncovered the bowl, and it was full of bread and meat. When she looked at it she was amazed, and knew it was a blessing from Allah, Mighty and Majestic. She praised Allah, Exalted, and invoked blessings upon His Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and presented it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. When he saw it, he praised Allah, Exalted, and said: Whence is this for you, O my daughter? I said: O father, it is from Allah; indeed Allah provides whom He wills without account. He praised Allah, Exalted, and said: Praise be to Allah Who made you, O my daughter, like the mistress of the women of the Children of Israil, for when Allah provided her something and she was asked about it, she said: It is from Allah; indeed Allah provides whom He wills without account. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent for Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Ali, Fatima, Hasan, Husayn, and all the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his household, may Allah be pleased with them all, ate until they were full, and the bowl remained as it was. She said: I shared its remainder with all my neighbors, and Allah, Blessed and Exalted, placed in it abundant blessing and good."
"4724","Abu Yala and al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Uqba al-Sadusi narrated to us, Muawiya ibn Hisham narrated to us, Amr ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, guarded her chastity, so Allah, Exalted, forbade her offspring to the Fire. Al-Bazzar said: We know none narrated it thus except Amr, who is a weak Kufan, and it has been narrated from Asim, from Zirr, mursal."
"4725","Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, from Abd al-Salam ibn Harb, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ibn Yuhannas, from Said ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, took al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: O Allah, I love him, so love him."
"4726","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"4727","Ubaydullah, that is al-Qawariri, narrated to us, Hammad ibn Masada narrated to us, from al-Mundhir ibn Thalaba, from Ilba ibn Ahmar, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I proposed to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for his daughter Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, sold a coat of mail of his, and some of his goods he sold, reaching four hundred and eighty dirhams. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered two-thirds to be placed in perfume, and a third in clothes. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, spat into a jar of water and ordered them to wash with it. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered her not to precede him in nursing her child. He said: She preceded him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in nursing al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: As for al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, placed in his mouth something I do not know what it was; he was the more learned of the two men."
"4728","Ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-Rabi ibn Sad al-Jufi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Whoever is pleased to look at a man of Paradise, let him look at al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying it."
"4729","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu Nasr narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ammar, who said: Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I heard the jinn wailing over al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4730","Abd ibn Humayd said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us with it."
"4731","Al-Tayalisi said: Musa ibn Mutayr narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said concerning al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both: Whoever loves me, let him love these two. Ibn Majah brought it with its meaning by way of Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4732","Abu Bakr said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, are the masters of the youths of Paradise. Its narrators are trustworthy."
"4733","Al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutayba narrated to us, Husayn al-Muallim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: Al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, wrestled before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, kept saying: Come on, Hasan. Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, said to him: O Messenger of Allah, as though - meaning al-Hasan - is more beloved to you than al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Jibril, peace be upon him, aids al-Husayn, and I love to aid al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with them both. This is mursal."
"4734","Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Muawiya ibn Abi Muzarrid, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: My eye saw and my ear heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he took the hand of Hasan or Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both - and most of my thought is that it was Husayn - and placed his feet upon his feet, and the hadith."
"4735","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Husayn - meaning al-Ashqar - narrated to us, from Ali ibn Hashim, from Ibn Abi Rafi, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: I saw al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, upon the shoulders of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I said: What an excellent mount beneath you. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: And what excellent riders they are."
"4736","Uthman narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Shayba ibn Naama, from Fatima bint al-Husayn, from Fatima the elder, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For every mothers sons there is a clan they ascribe to except the children of Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her; I am their guardian and I am their clan."
"4737","Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Nuh ibn Qays narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Dhakwan narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would prostrate, and al-Hasan or al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, would come and mount upon his back, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prolonging the prostration. It would be said: O Prophet of Allah, you prolonged the prostration?! He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would say: My son mounted me, and I disliked to hasten him."
"4738","Ishaq said: Yala ibn Ubayd informed us, Musa al-Juhani narrated to us, from Salih ibn Arbad al-Nakhai, from Umm Salama, who said: Al-Husayn ibn Ali entered upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, in the house while I was sitting at the door. I looked and saw the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, turning something in his palm, with the boy sleeping upon his stomach. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw you turning something in your palm with the boy sleeping upon your stomach and your tears flowing? He said: Jibril came to me with the soil in which he shall be killed, and told me that your nation shall kill him."
"4739","He said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi informed us, Ismail ibn Abi al-Salt narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Hasan saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This son of mine is a master; Allah shall reconcile through him two parties of Muslims. I said: It is in Sahih al-Bukhari by another way from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakr."
"4740","Chapter on the merit of the household, blessings of Allah upon them. Musaddad said: Abdullah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubayda, from Iyas ibn Salama, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The stars are a safety for the people of heaven, and my household, may Allah be pleased with them, are a safety for my nation."
"4741","Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubayda narrated to us with it."
"4742","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with it."
"4743","Suwayd ibn Said narrated to us, Mufaddal narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hanash, who said: I heard Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, holding the ring of the door, saying: O people, whoever knows me has known me, and whoever denies, denies; I am Abu Dharr. I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The likeness of my household among you is but the likeness of the ark of Nuh; whoever enters it is saved, and whoever stays back from it perishes."
"4744","Abdullah narrated to us, Abd al-Karim ibn Hilal narrated to us, Aslam al-Makki informed me, Abu al-Tufayl informed me, that he saw Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, standing at the door, calling: O people, do you know me? Whoever knows me has known me, and whoever does not know me, I am Jundub, the companion of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and I am Abu Dharr al-Ghifari. I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: The likeness of my household among you is the likeness of the ark of Nuh; whoever boards it is saved, and whoever stays back from it drowns. And the likeness of my household among you is the likeness of the gate of forgiveness."
"4745","Al-Bazzar brought it by way of al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Hanash."
"4746","Abu Yala said: Sahl ibn Zanjala narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Ikrima ibn Ammar, from Uthal ibn Qurra, from Ibn Hawshab al-Hanafi, who said: Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, saying: Fatima, the daughter of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, may Allah be pleased with her, came with dignity, leading al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, with a pot in her hand for al-Hasan containing porridge, until she brought it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. When she placed it before him, he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Where is Abu Hasan? She said: In the house. He called him, and they all sat eating. Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, never shared with me, nor ate food at all while I was with him except that he shared it with me before that day - meaning: he invited me to it. When he finished, he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wrapped them in his garment. He brought it abridged."
"4747","Al-Harith said: Khalaf ibn al-Walid narrated to us, al-Ashjai narrated to us, from Sufyan, who said: It reached me that Ali ibn al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, was visited by people who praised him. He said: Woe to you, how you lie and how bold you are against Allah, Exalted. We are people among the righteous of our people, and it suffices us to be among the righteous of our people."
"4748","Chapter on the merit of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him. Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother Umm Kulthum bint Uqba, may Allah be pleased with her - and she was among the first emigrants - who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, was overcome by fainting until they thought his soul had departed. Umm Kulthum, may Allah be pleased with her, went out to the mosque seeking help with what she was commanded of patience and prayer. When he recovered he said: Was I overcome? They said: Yes. He said: You spoke true. Two angels came to me and said: Come, we take you to dispute before the Mighty, the Trustworthy. Another angel said: Return him, for he is among those for whom happiness was written while in their mothers bellies, and his sons shall enjoy him as long as Allah, Exalted, wills. He lived a month after that, then died, may Allah be pleased with him. And Abu Usama said: Two men said two angels who used to come in the form of men. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: Had We made him an angel, We would have made him a man, that is, in the form of a man."
"4749","Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu al-Mualla al-Jazari informed us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the people of the council: Will you accept that I choose for you and withdraw from it? Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Yes, I am the first to agree, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: You are trustworthy among the people of heaven, trustworthy among the people of earth. And the hadith: I hope to wear it in this world and the Hereafter, has already come in Clothing."
"4750","Musaddad said: al-Mutamir, that is Ibn Sulayman, narrated to us, from his father, from al-Hadrami, who said: A man with soft voice and soft recitation recited before the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. None of the people remained but his eye overflowed except Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: If Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf did not overflow in eye, his heart overflowed."
"4751","Al-Harith said: Ibn Abi Umayya narrated to us, Abu Awana narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, some men of Banu Tamim narrated to us that they were with Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No prophet ever died until a man of his nation led him in prayer. Meaning in the story of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4752","Merit of al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Ibn Abi Aruba informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he heard a man saying: O son of the disciple of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to him: If you are of the family of al-Zubayr, otherwise not."
"4753","Al-Bazzar narrated it: Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yazid narrated to us with this. And he said: None narrated it from Ayyub except Said, nor from him except Yazid."
"4754","Abu Yala said: Zuhayr, that is Abu Khaythama, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Madani narrated to us, Umm Urwa - as I think - the daughter of Jafar ibn al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, narrated to me, from her father, from her grandfather al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with them, that he heard him saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed for me, for my children, and for my childrens children. I heard my father saying to a sister of mine older than me: You are among those touched by the supplication of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4755","Merit of Talha, may Allah be pleased with him. Ibn Abi Umar said: Said ibn Salim al-Qaddah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Saj, from a man - he named him, his name escaped me - that he entered with Musa ibn Talha to Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him. He drew him near until he seated him with him upon the bed, then took Musas arm and pressed it, then said: Take it easy, O son of my brother, for by Allah I hope that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, shall make me and your father - meaning Talha, may Allah be pleased with him - among those from whose chests Allah removed rancor, brothers, upon couches facing one another."
"4756","Abu Yala said: Suwayd ibn Said narrated to us, Salih ibn Musa narrated to us, from Muawiya ibn Ishaq, from Aisha bint Talha, from Aisha, the Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: By Allah, I was in my house one day, with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, in the courtyard, with a curtain between me and them, when Talha ibn Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him, approached. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever is pleased to look at a man walking upon the earth who has fulfilled his vow, let him look at Talha, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4757","Al-Tayalisi said: Abu Bakr al-Hudhali narrated to us, Abu al-Malih narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Talha, may Allah be pleased with him, was mentioned before Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: That is a man in whom there is pride since his hand was struck with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"4758","Merit of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him. Al-Tayalisi said: Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Sad informed us, from his father, from Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There is no death I would die more beloved to me than to be killed wronged."
"4759","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, a shaykh of ours narrated to us, from Aisha bint Talha, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Beware the supplication of Sad."
"4760","Chapter on the merit of in-laws and brothers-in-law, may Allah be pleased with them. Al-Harith said: Ishaq ibn Bishr narrated to us, Ammar ibn Sayf al-Dabbi, the executor of Sufyan al-Thawri, narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Umar, or Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I asked my Lord, Blessed and Exalted, that I marry none of my nation nor give any in marriage to my nation except he be with me in Paradise, and He granted me that."
"4761","Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Jarwal ibn Jandal narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Abu Abdullah ibn Marzuq or Ibn Rizq, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A resolve from my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, and a covenant He covenanted to me - that I marry into no household nor give any of my daughters in marriage except they be my companions in Paradise."
"4762","Ishaq said: Jafar ibn Muhammad informed us, from his father, who said: When Umar married Umm Kulthum bint Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: Will you not congratulate me, for I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: Every tie and lineage is cut off on the Day of Resurrection except my tie and my lineage."
"4763","He said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Sharik narrated to us, from Urwa al-Jufi, from Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, went out to the people of the bench and said: Will you not congratulate me? They said: What is that, O Commander of the Believers? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I married Umm Kulthum, may Allah be pleased with her, for the sake of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Fatima, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both. I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying, and he mentioned it. He said: So I loved to be. This is interrupted."
"4764","Chapter on what a group of Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, shared. Ahmad ibn Mani said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, and from Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Zadhan, both said: While the people were one day with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, they found him in good spirits, and said: Narrate to us of your companions, O Commander of the Believers. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Which of my companions? They said: The companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: All the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, are my companions, so which do you want? They said: The group we saw you favoring with your mention and prayer over others. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Which of them? They said: Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: He knew the practice and recited the Quran, and knowledge suffices him. Then he closed with him, and they did not know what he meant by his saying knowledge suffices him: whether Abdullah suffices, or the Quran suffices. They said: Hudhayfa, may Allah be pleased with him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: He knew, or was taught, the names of the hypocrites, and asked about difficult matters until he understood them; if you ask him about them you shall find him knowledgeable therein. They said: Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A vessel filled with knowledge; and he, may Allah be pleased with him, was sparing, eager - sparing of his religion, eager for knowledge - he would ask much, and be given and denied; yet it was filled for him in his vessel until it was full. They said: Salman, may Allah be pleased with him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A man of us and to us, the household; who has for you the like of Luqman the Wise; he knew the first knowledge, attained the last knowledge, read the first Book and the last Book, and he, may Allah be pleased with him, was a sea that cannot be emptied. They said: Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: That is a man whose flesh, blood, bone, hair and skin Allah, Exalted, mixed with faith; he never parts from truth for an hour; wherever it turns, he turns with it; the Fire should not eat anything of him. They said: Narrate to us of yourself, O Commander of the Believers? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Gently, Allah forbade self-praise. He said: A speaker said: Allah, Exalted, says: As for the favor of your Lord, proclaim. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I proclaim to you the favor of my Lord, Blessed and Exalted: when I asked I was given, and when I was silent I was inspired, and between my sides was filled with abundant knowledge."
"4765","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ruzmi narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, Said informed us, from Qatada, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The two tribes of the Ansar boasted, Aws and Khazraj. Aws said: Among us is the one washed by angels, Hanzala the monk, may Allah be pleased with him; among us is he for whom the Throne of the Merciful shook, Sad ibn Muadh, may Allah be pleased with him; among us is he whom the wasps protected, Asim ibn Thabit ibn Abi al-Aqlah, may Allah be pleased with him; among us is he whose testimony was accepted as that of two men, Khuzayma ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him. And Khazraj, may Allah be pleased with them, said: Among us are four who collected the Quran in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, none but they collected it: Zayd ibn Thabit, Abu Zayd, Ubayy ibn Kab, and Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with them. Al-Bazzar narrated it by way of Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata, and its basis is in al-Bukhari."
"4766","Muadh ibn al-Muthanna said in the additions of the Musnad of Musaddad: Abdullah ibn Muslim al-Qurashi narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was stabbed and ordered the council, Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, entered upon him and said: O father, people claim that these six are not pleasing. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Prop me up. They propped him. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What could they say of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him?! I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: O Ali, your hand is in my hand; you shall enter with me on the Day of Resurrection wherever I enter. What could they say of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him?! I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying: On the day Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, dies, the angels of heaven shall pray over him. I said: O Messenger of Allah, for Uthman specially or for people generally? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: For Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, specially. What could they say of Talha ibn Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him?! I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying one night when his saddle had fallen: Who will set my saddle right for me and be in Paradise? Talha ibn Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him, hastened and set it right for him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, until he mounted. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: O Talha, this is Jibril, peace be upon him, conveying greetings to you and saying: I am with you in the terrors of the Day of Resurrection until I save you from them. What could they say of al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him?! I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asleep, and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, sat warding off from his face until he awoke. He said to him: O Abu Abdullah, have you not ceased? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I have not ceased, may my father and mother be ransom for you. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: This is Jibril, peace be upon him, conveying greetings to you and saying: I am with you on the Day of Resurrection until I ward off from your face the sparks of the Fire. What could they say of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him?! I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying on the day of Badr when he had strung his bow fourteen times, handing it to him and saying: Shoot, may my father and mother be ransom for you. And what could they say of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him?! I saw the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saying in the house of Fatima, may Allah be pleased with her, while al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, were weeping from hunger and writhing. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Who will join us with something? Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, appeared with a tray containing hays and two loaves with clarified butter between them. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: May Allah, Exalted, suffice you the affair of your world; as for the affair of your Hereafter, I am its guarantor."
"4767","Abu Yala said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Nadr ibn Humayd, from Sad al-Iskaf, from Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Muhammad, Allah, Exalted, loves three of your companions, so love them: Ali ibn Abi Talib, Abu Dharr, and al-Miqdad, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: And Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him and said: O Muhammad, Paradise longs for three of your companions. With him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who hoped it would be for some of the Ansar. He said: He wanted to ask the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about them but was awed. He went out and met Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: O Abu Bakr, I was with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, just now, and Jibril, peace be upon him, came to him and said: Paradise longs for three of your companions. I hoped it would be for some of the Ansar, but I was awed to ask him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Will you enter and ask him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I fear to ask him and not be among them, so my people would rejoice at my misfortune. Then he met Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and said to him like what he said to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said to him like what Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said. Then he met Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Yes, I shall ask him; if I am among them I praise Allah, Exalted, and if I am not among them, meaning it shall not harm. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, entered and said: Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, told me that he was with you just now, and that Jibril, peace be upon him, came to you and said: Paradise longs for three of your companions; who are they, O Prophet of Allah? He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: You are among them, O Ali, and Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, who shall witness with you scenes whose merit is clear, its good great; and Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, who is of us, the household, and he is sincere, so take him for yourself."
"4768","Al-Bazzar said: Ahmad ibn Malik al-Qushayri narrated to us, Jafar ibn Sulayman narrated to us, and he mentioned it. And he said: al-Nadr and Sad were not strong, and none narrated it except Jafar."
"4769","Abd said: Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Numan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Hayyan, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Love of these four gathers only in the heart of a believer: Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them. This is interrupted."
"4770","Al-Harith said: al-Muqri narrated to us, Umar ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We, the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while numerous, used to say: The best of this nation after its Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, is Abu Bakr, then Umar, then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, then we would fall silent."
"4771","Yazid narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Husayn narrated to us, from Yala ibn Muslim, from Mujahid, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, recited upon the pulpit: Gardens of Eden, and said: None shall enter it except a prophet - glad tidings to you, O dweller of the grave - pointing to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him - or a truthful one - glad tidings to you, O Abu Bakr - or a martyr. And how would Umar attain martyrdom! Yet He Who brought me out of my house in al-Hathma is able to drive it to me."
"4772","Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Kunasa narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Habib, from Abu al-Bakhtari, who said: We mentioned before him Abu Bakr, Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: An excellent view, and I find in my heart for Ali a closeness I do not find for them."
"4773","Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated to us, Bashir ibn Zadhan narrated to us, from Rukayn, from Makhul, from Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Abu Bakr is the most merciful of my nation and most compassionate; Umar is the boldest of my nation and most just; Uthman is the most modest of my nation and most generous; Ali is the most intelligent of my nation and bravest; Abdullah ibn Masud is the most dutiful of my nation and most trustworthy; Abu Dharr is the most ascetic of my nation and most truthful; Abu al-Darda is the most excusing of my nation and most God-fearing; and Muawiya is the most forbearing of my nation and most generous."
"4774","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Harith narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that is Ibn al-Baylamani, narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The most merciful of my nation to my nation is Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; the sternest of them in Islam is Umar, may Allah be pleased with him; the truest of them in modesty is Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him; the most excellent of them is Ali, may Allah be pleased with him; the most learned of them in division is Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him; the most learned of them in lawful and unlawful is Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him; the best reciter of them is Ubayy ibn Kab, may Allah be pleased with him; and for every nation there is a trustworthy one, and the trustworthy of this nation is Abu Ubayda, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4775","Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah and Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani narrated to us, and I wrote it from the hadith of Abu al-Rabi; both said: Ismail ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, al-Nadr Abu Umar al-Khazzaz narrated to us, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was upon Hira, and the mountain shook. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Be firm, Hira, for upon you is none but a prophet, or a truthful one, or a martyr. Upon it were the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, Ali, Talha, al-Zubayr, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, Sad, and Said, may Allah be pleased with them."
"4776","Chapter on the merit of Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him. Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Hasan saying: Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I fought with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, against jinn and men. It was said: How did you fight the jinn? He said: We alighted at a place, and I took my waterskin and bucket to draw water. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: A comer shall come to you at the water preventing you. When I was at the well, a black man like a pitch-covered camel came to me and said: You shall draw no bucket from it today. He seized me and I seized him and threw him down. Then I took a stone and broke his nose and face. Then I filled my waterskin and came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: Did anyone come to you at the water? I said: A black man. I told him what I did. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: That was Satan. This is an interrupted isnad, and its men are trustworthy. And the hadith of Ali in the chapter on what a group shared. And the hadith of Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, in his biography. And the reports concerning: The rebellious party shall kill Ammar, shall come, Allah willing, in the battle of Siffin, as shall the description of his killing, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4777","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I met the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, at al-Batha. He took my hand, and I went with him, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He passed by Ammar and the mother of Ammar, may Allah be pleased with them both, being tortured, and said: Patience, family of Yasir, for your destination is Paradise."
"4778","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was building the mosque; when the people carried a stone, Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, would carry two stones, and when they carried a brick, he would carry two bricks."
"4779","Chapter on the merit of Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Yala said: al-Fadl ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, Abu Ubayda narrated to us, Muhtasib narrated to us, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, sat in his house, and people gathered to him, and he began reciting the Quran to them. A man came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not amaze you with Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him? He sat in a house with people gathered to him, reciting the Quran to them! The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Can you seat me where none of them sees me? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, went out. He said: The man seated him where none of them saw him. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard the recitation of Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, and said, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: He recites upon a flute of the flutes of the family of Dawud."
"4780","Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih and Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, separating them; both said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Qanan ibn Abdullah al-Nahmi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Awsaja, from al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, heard Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reciting, and said, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: It is as though the voice of this is from the flutes of the family of Dawud."
"4781","Account of Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Bakr said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Abu Mashar, from Said ibn Abi Said, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came down with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, from the pass of Harsha. The strap of his sandal, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, broke, so I offered him my strap, but he refused to accept it. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sat in the shade of a tree to mend his sandal. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me: See whom you see? I said: Such-and-such. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Evil is the slave of Allah, such-and-such. Then he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me: See whom you see? I said: Such-and-such. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Evil is the slave of Allah, such-and-such. He said: Then he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to me: See whom you see? I said: Such-and-such. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Excellent is the slave of Allah, such-and-such. And he whom he, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said of: Excellent is the slave of Allah, such-and-such, was Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him; as for the other two, I shall inform none of them. Abu Mashar is weak."
"4782","Abu Yala said: Surayj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was told that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not revile Khalid, for he is one of the swords of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, which Allah, Exalted, unsheathed against the disbelievers. Sahih isnad."
"4783","But Abu Ismail al-Muaddib narrated it, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, saying: From al-Shabi, from Ibn Abi Awfa, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it in a hadith brought by Abu Yala from Abdullah ibn Awn al-Khazzaz, from him. Its beginning: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, complained of Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. He said: O Khalid, why do you harm a man of Badr; had you spent the like of Uhud in gold, you would not reach his deed. He said: O Messenger of Allah, they speak ill of me, so I answer them. He, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Do not harm Khalid, for he is a sword of the swords of Allah, Exalted, which Allah poured upon the disbelievers."
"4784","Abu Yala said: Surayj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Ismail narrated to me, from Qays, who said: Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Striving in the path of Allah, Exalted, kept me much from recitation. Sahih."
"4785","With it Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, said: No night on which a bride is brought to my house whom I love, or in which I am given glad tidings of a boy, is more beloved to me than a night of severe frost in a company of strivers whom I lead to attack the enemy in the morning."
"4786","4011 - Surayj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abu al-Safar, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) stayed with the Marazibah (the Persian chiefs) and they said to him: Beware of poison; the non-Arabs may give you some to drink. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Bring it to me. So it was brought to him, and he took it in his hand, then swallowed it down, and said: In the name of Allah. And it did not harm him at all."
"4787","4012 - Surayj narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from his father, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We performed Umrah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in an Umrah he performed, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shaved his hair, so I raced to the forelock and took it, and I made a cap and placed it at the front of the cap, and I was never directed toward any front but that victory was granted to me."
"4788","Chapter 42: The merit of Salman (may Allah be pleased with him). 4013 - Abu Ya'la said: Qatan ibn Nusayr narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) established brotherhood between his Companions: between Abu al-Darda' and Salman (may Allah be pleased with them both), and between Awf ibn Malik and al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"4789","4014 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Abu Sufyan, from his elders, they said: Sa'd visited Salman (may Allah be pleased with them both) when he was ill, and Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) wept, so Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: What makes you weep, O Abdullah? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away while he was pleased with you, and you will come to him at the Basin (Hawd), and you will meet your companions. And the hadith of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) has preceded in the chapter of what a group shared in."
"4790","Chapter 43: The merit of Zayd ibn Suhan (may Allah be pleased with him). 4015 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Hudhayl ibn Bilal, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mas'ud al-Abdi, from Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is pleased to look at a man some of whose limbs will precede him to Paradise, let him look at Zayd ibn Suhan (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4791","Chapter 44: The merit of Hassan (may Allah be pleased with him). 4016 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: A man came to Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) and said: Hassan the accursed has come. So Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: He is not accursed; he strove alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his tongue and his self."
"4792","4017 - Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, Abdah narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: Hassan ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) recited verses to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I too."
"4793","Chapter 45: The merit of Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal (may Allah be pleased with him). 4018 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, al-Hasan informed me, from the keeper of the Prophet's provisions (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with him) - Ibn Awn said: he used to be called Safinah - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey, and his camel carried the provisions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal came and said: I am hungry. He said: I will not give you anything until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) orders me and the people stop so we may eat. He said: Then he did like this with the sword and cut open the hock of the camel. He said: And whenever a matter distressed them they would say: Hold back, the first one, hold back, the first one. So they heard and stopped, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and when he saw what Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal had done to the camel, he said to him: Go away, and he ordered the people to march on. So Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal kept following them until they stopped, and he kept coming to them in their dwellings saying: To where has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expelled me? Has he expelled me to the Fire? He said: So they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal has not ceased going around our dwellings since last night, saying: To where has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expelled me? Has he expelled me to the Fire? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal has a foul tongue but a good heart."
"4794","Chapter 46: The merit of Khuzaymah ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him). 4019/1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Husayn al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Za'idah, Abu Farwah al-Juhani narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: He passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he had bought a horse from a Bedouin, and the Bedouin denied him the sale and said: I did not sell to you. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have sold to me. Then Khuzaymah ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by them and heard what they said, and said: I testify that you sold. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: And how do you know that when you did not witness us? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We have testified to what is greater than that. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accepted his testimony as equal to the testimony of two men, until Khuzaymah (may Allah be pleased with him) died."
"4795","4019/2 - And Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Zurarah ibn Khuzaymah narrated to me, Umarah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit narrated to us, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a horse from Sawa' ibn Qays al-Muharibi, and he denied it, so Khuzaymah ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) testified for him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What carried you to testify when you were not present with us? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I believed you in what you brought, and I knew that you speak nothing but the truth. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever Khuzaymah testifies for or against, he suffices him (i.e. his testimony alone suffices)."
"4796","4019/3 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with this."
"4797","4020 - And al-Harith said: al-Khalil ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a horse from a Bedouin, and the Bedouin denied it. Then Khuzaymah ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) came and said: O Bedouin, do you deny? I testify against you that you sold it. So the Bedouin said: If Khuzaymah ibn Thabit testifies against me, then give me the price. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Khuzaymah, we did not have you as a witness, so how do you testify? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I believe you concerning the news of heaven; shall I not believe you concerning this Bedouin? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his testimony equal to the testimony of two men, and there was no man in Islam whose testimony was accepted as equal to two men other than Khuzaymah ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4798","Chapter 47: The merit of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4021 - Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Ikrimah, he said: Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I seek Allah's forgiveness - the Almighty - and repent to Him every day twelve thousand times, and that is according to his blood-money. Or he said: my blood-money."
"4799","Chapter 48: The merit of Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, and Waraqah, may Allah have mercy on them. 4022 - al-Tayalisi said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Nufayl ibn Hisham ibn Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl al-Adawi, of Adi of Quraysh, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: Zayd ibn Amr and Waraqah ibn Nawfal went out seeking the religion until they reached a monk in Mosul, and he said to Zayd ibn Amr: From where have you come, O owner of the camel? He said: From the edifice of Ibrahim. He said: What do you seek? He said: I seek the religion. He said: Return, for the one you seek is about to appear in your land. As for Waraqah, he became Christian, and as for me, Christianity was presented to me but it did not suit me, so he returned saying: At Your service, truly, truly, in worship and servitude; goodness I seek, not paternal uncles. And he says: I believe in what Ibrahim believed in, then he says, whatever You burden me with, then he falls down prostrating. He said: And his son came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my father was as you saw and as reached you; shall I seek forgiveness for him? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, for he will be raised on the Day of Resurrection alone as a nation. And Zayd ibn Amr came upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he and Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him) were eating from a food mat of theirs, and they invited him to their food, but Zayd ibn Amr said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We do not eat what has been slaughtered for the sacrificial stones (nusub)."
"4800","4023 - And Abu Ya'la said: Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ismail narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He will be raised on the Day of Resurrection alone as a nation, between me and Isa (peace be upon him). And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Waraqah ibn Nawfal, and he said: I saw him in the midst of Paradise wearing brocade (sundus). Ismail is Ibn Mujalid, and Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi followed him. Al-Bazzar narrated it, and Mujalid is alone in it, and there is weakness in him."
